window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("enterprise-integration", {"topics":[{"key":"enterprise-integration/enterprise-connectors","sources":["Official docs: Microsoft Graph (delta query, SharePoint and Teams resources)","Official docs: Salesforce REST, Bulk and Change Data Capture APIs","Official docs: Atlassian Confluence and Jira Cloud REST APIs","Official docs: Slack Web API and Events API"],"why":"- **Knowledge is scattered.** Enterprise knowledge lives in a dozen SaaS and on-prem systems (Salesforce,\n  SAP, ServiceNow, SharePoint, Confluence, Jira, Slack, Teams), each with its own API, object model, query\n  language, auth scheme and permission semantics.\n- **Reach decides value.** A RAG assistant or agent is only as useful as the systems it can see, so \"can it\n  read our Confluence and Jira?\" is usually the first question in a deal.\n- **The naive script demos well and fails in production.** Authenticate with an admin token, page through\n  everything, dump text into a vector store. It fails for four reasons:\n  1. it reads with admin rights and discards source ACLs, so a junior employee gets a page from a\n     restricted HR space;\n  2. nightly full re-fetches burn API quotas the customer's other integrations also depend on, and get\n     throttled;\n  3. it never notices deletions, so documents removed for legal reasons keep surfacing;\n  4. it hard-codes one tenant's quirks, so the second customer breaks it.\n- **A connector is the disciplined version.** A repeatable contract for auth, enumeration, change\n  tracking, permissions and normalization that every source implements, so every agent tool on top of it\n  behaves the same way.","how":"### The connector contract\nEvery connector implements the same five responsibilities, *because* the downstream pipeline (chunking,\nembedding, ACL-aware retrieval, tool calling) should not care where a document came from:\n\n1. **Authenticate** as an app registered in the customer's tenant (OAuth client credentials, a service\n   account, or delegated user tokens), with the narrowest scopes that work.\n2. **Enumerate** the containers in scope (sites, spaces, projects, channels, objects) and page through\n   items, honouring pagination cursors and rate-limit responses (HTTP 429, `Retry-After`).\n3. **Track change**: store a cursor (delta token, change-event replay ID, or `updated` watermark) so the\n   next run fetches only what changed, including deletions.\n4. **Capture permissions** alongside content: who can see this item, expressed as source principals that\n   can later be resolved to users.\n5. **Normalize** into a canonical document (id, source URL, title, body, author, timestamps, ACL, source\n   metadata) so one indexing pipeline serves all sources.\n\n### Two shapes an agent consumes\n| Shape | What the agent calls | Freshness | Permission enforcement |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Synced corpus | A `search_docs` retrieval tool over your index | One sync cycle behind | ACL filter on stored principals |\n| Live tool | An action or lookup tool calling the source API | Current | The source's own checks, if called as the user |\n\n- **Prose goes in the corpus** (Confluence pages, SharePoint files, Slack threads), because agents need\n  ranked semantic search across them.\n- **Records and actions go behind tools** (SAP orders, ServiceNow incidents, creating Jira issues), because\n  values change constantly and writes must hit the source.\n\n### What differs per system\n| System | Access and query | Change tracking | Permission model |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Salesforce | SOQL over REST; Bulk API for large extracts, because per-record REST calls burn the org's API allocation | Change Data Capture events or `SystemModstamp` watermarks | Profiles, permission sets, role hierarchy, sharing rules |\n| SAP | OData services (S/4HANA, SAP Gateway), RFC/BAPI calls, IDoc messages for async document exchange | Usually live calls rather than a synced corpus | Fine-grained authorization objects, enforced by calling SAP as the user |\n| ServiceNow | Table API (incidents, knowledge articles, CMDB items) with encoded queries | Record update timestamps | ACLs scripted per table and field |\n| SharePoint, Teams | Microsoft Graph | Drive and list items support delta queries returning changes and deletions since a token | Per site, library, folder and item, inheritance breakable at any level |\n| Confluence, Jira | Atlassian REST with CQL and JQL | Query by `lastmodified` or `updated` | Space permissions plus page restrictions inheriting down the tree; Jira project permission schemes plus optional issue security levels |\n| Slack | Web API (`conversations.history`, `conversations.replies`) for backfill | Events API for live changes | Channel membership, which changes often |\n\nPer-system caveats that change the agent design:\n- **Salesforce visibility is too complex to replicate faithfully**, so many connectors restrict scope to\n  objects with simple sharing or check access at query time.\n- **SAP data is structured transactional records, not prose**, so the connector is more often a tool an\n  agent calls (look up an order, check stock) than a corpus to embed.\n- **A ServiceNow service account's view is not a user's view**, because ACLs are scripted per table and field.\n- **Teams messages may need approval.** Channel messages are in Graph, but some message-export APIs are\n  classed as protected and require an approval or licensing step, so check before promising them.\n- **Slack limits vary by app type.** It uses method-specific rate-limit tiers and has tightened\n  history-read limits for some classes of apps (the 2025 changes for apps not distributed through its\n  Marketplace). Check current limits before sizing a backfill. Private channels and DMs are visible only\n  to apps added or granted the right scopes.\n\n### From source principals to people\n- **Each ACL is in the source's own vocabulary.** SharePoint and Teams use Entra object IDs plus SharePoint\n  site groups; Confluence and Jira use Atlassian account IDs and groups; Slack uses its own user IDs and\n  channel membership; Salesforce uses user IDs, roles and public groups. None is the sign-in identity.\n- **Maintain a principal crosswalk.** Map source principal ID to the IdP's immutable user or group ID,\n  built from each source's user directory API. Join on the IdP object ID where the source records it\n  (Microsoft sources do), or on a verified email where it does not.\n- **Expand groups.** Source-local groups are expanded to members, nested groups recursively, *because* the\n  retrieval filter can only test identities it knows.\n- **Model the special cases explicitly.** Sharing links (\"anyone in the organization with the link\",\n  anonymous links) grant access to a scope, not a listed principal. Guest or external users have no IdP\n  account. Neither may be dropped.\n- **Fail closed.** A principal that resolves to nobody grants nothing; it must never be ignored in a way\n  that widens access, because the agent will happily quote whatever retrieval returns.\n\n### Sizing the first crawl\nThe initial backfill, not the steady state, decides when the agent pilot can start.\n\n| Input (illustrative SharePoint tenant) | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Files | 2 million |\n| Calls per file | 1 content download + 1 permissions call |\n| Total requests | about 4 million |\n| Sustained rate (illustrative; real one depends on throttling for your app and tenant) | 10 requests per second |\n| Duration | 400,000 seconds, roughly four and a half days, plus every retry after a 429 |\n\nLevers that follow from the arithmetic:\n- **Batch where the API allows it.** Graph JSON batching groups up to 20 requests per call, though\n  throttling still counts the individual requests.\n- **Read permissions once per folder** whose inheritance is intact, instead of once per file.\n- **Crawl the highest-value containers first**, so the pilot agent is useful before the crawl finishes.\n- **Make the crawl resumable** by checkpointing per container.\n- **Expect steady state to be cheap.** After the backfill, cost scales with the change rate, typically a\n  small fraction of the corpus per day.\n\n### Build, buy or use MCP\n- **Buy** when unified-API vendors or the AI platforms' own connector catalogues cover the source.\n- **Build** when you need permissions, fields or freshness they do not provide.\n- **MCP for agent actions.** Wrapping each system as an MCP server gives a standard tool interface, but it\n  does not remove any of the auth, quota or permission work above.","example":"### SharePoint library sync with Graph delta queries\nThe first call returns every item; later calls with the saved `deltaLink` return only changes, and\ndeleted items carry a `deleted` facet, which is how the connector removes them from the agent's index.\n\n```python\nimport time, requests\n\nGRAPH = \"https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0\"\n\ndef sync_drive(token: str, drive_id: str, saved_delta: str | None, index) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Pull changes since saved_delta into `index`; return the new delta link to persist.\"\"\"\n    url = saved_delta or f\"{GRAPH}/drives/{drive_id}/root/delta\"\n    headers = {\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token}\"}\n    while True:\n        r = requests.get(url, headers=headers, timeout=30)\n        if r.status_code == 429:                       # throttled: honour the server's hint\n            time.sleep(int(r.headers.get(\"Retry-After\", \"10\")))\n            continue\n        r.raise_for_status()\n        page = r.json()\n        for item in page[\"value\"]:\n            key = f\"sharepoint:{drive_id}:{item['id']}\"\n            if \"deleted\" in item:\n                index.delete(key)                      # deletions arrive as tombstones\n            elif \"file\" in item:\n                perms = requests.get(f\"{GRAPH}/drives/{drive_id}/items/{item['id']}/permissions\",\n                                     headers=headers, timeout=30).json()[\"value\"]\n                index.upsert(key, {\n                    \"title\": item[\"name\"],\n                    \"url\": item[\"webUrl\"],\n                    \"modified\": item[\"lastModifiedDateTime\"],\n                    # sharing links carry a \"link\" facet with a scope instead of a named principal\n                    \"acl\": [p.get(\"grantedToV2\") or p.get(\"grantedToIdentitiesV2\")\n                            or {\"link\": p.get(\"link\", {}).get(\"scope\")} for p in perms],\n                })                                     # content download + parsing happens downstream\n        if \"@odata.nextLink\" in page:\n            url = page[\"@odata.nextLink\"]              # more pages in this round\n        else:\n            return page[\"@odata.deltaLink\"]            # persist; next run starts here\n```\n\nWhat the code commits to:\n- **One permissions call per changed file.** Fine for incremental runs, expensive for a 2-million-file\n  initial crawl, which is where batching and a separate permission-sync job come in. The permissions call\n  also needs the same 429 handling as the delta call, omitted here for length.\n- **Ordering makes it crash-safe.** The `deltaLink` is persisted only after the last page is processed, so\n  a crash mid-round replays the round from the previous token. Upserts and deletes are idempotent by key,\n  so replaying them is harmless.\n- **Sharing changes are not fully reported.** By default a delta round surfaces content and metadata\n  changes; a sharing change on a folder does not list every descendant whose effective access changed.\n  Graph has request options for reporting sharing changes on business drives; without them, or in\n  addition, run a periodic permission resync.\n\n### The agent's retrieval tool over that index\nThe tool the model calls never takes a user ID as an argument; the runtime injects the authenticated\ncaller's resolved principals, so a prompt injection cannot ask for someone else's documents.\n\n```python\nSEARCH_TOOL = {\n    \"name\": \"search_company_docs\",\n    \"description\": \"Search SharePoint, Confluence and Slack content the current user may see.\",\n    \"input_schema\": {\"type\": \"object\",\n                     \"properties\": {\"query\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"top_k\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}},\n                     \"required\": [\"query\"]},\n}\n\ndef run_search_tool(args: dict, session, index, crosswalk) -> list[dict]:\n    principals = crosswalk.principals_for(session.idp_user_id)   # user + expanded groups + link scopes\n    if not principals:\n        return []                                                # fail closed: unresolved user sees nothing\n    hits = index.search(args[\"query\"], top_k=min(args.get(\"top_k\", 8), 20),\n                        filter={\"acl_any\": principals})          # filter BEFORE ranking, not after\n    return [{\"title\": h[\"title\"], \"url\": h[\"url\"], \"modified\": h[\"modified\"],\n             \"snippet\": h[\"text\"][:500]} for h in hits]          # URLs let the agent cite sources\n```\n\n### Backfill planner: will the pilot be ready in time?\nPlug in the item count, calls per item and the rate the source really gives you.\n\n```python\ndef backfill_days(items, calls_per_item, rps, retry_overhead=0.0, perms_per_folder=None):\n    \"\"\"Days for an initial crawl. perms_per_folder: folder count if permissions are read per folder.\"\"\"\n    calls = items * 1                                   # content download per item\n    calls += perms_per_folder if perms_per_folder else items * (calls_per_item - 1)\n    seconds = calls * (1 + retry_overhead) / rps\n    return round(seconds / 86_400, 1)\n\nprint(backfill_days(2_000_000, 2, 10))                        # 4.6  per-file permissions\nprint(backfill_days(2_000_000, 2, 10, retry_overhead=0.2))    # 5.6  with 20% retried after 429\nprint(backfill_days(2_000_000, 2, 10, perms_per_folder=50_000))  # 2.4  inheritance intact per folder\n```\n\n| Plan | Requests | Days at 10 req/s |\n|---|---|---|\n| Content + permissions per file | 4,000,000 | 4.6 |\n| Same, 20% of calls retried after 429 | 4,800,000 | 5.6 |\n| Permissions once per folder (50,000 folders, illustrative) | 2,050,000 | 2.4 |\n\nThe per-folder plan only holds where inheritance is intact; folders with broken inheritance still need\nper-item reads.\n\n### Worked scenario: a sales agent eating the Salesforce quota\nAn account-research agent calls a live `get_account` tool and a `list_opportunities` tool. Numbers are\nillustrative; check the org's real daily allocation in its limits endpoint.\n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Agent sessions per day | 2,000 |\n| Steps per session (median) | 6 |\n| Salesforce calls per step (tool plus pagination) | 2 |\n| Calls per day | 24,000 |\n| Same agent with a retry-loop bug in the worst 1% of sessions (40 steps) | about 1,400 extra calls a day from just 20 sessions (20 × 34 extra steps × 2) |\n\nThe fix was a per-session tool budget (stop after N Salesforce calls and say so), caching account\nlookups for the session, and alerting when the agent's share of the org's API usage passed the agreed slice.","patterns":["**Persist the cursor after the batch commits.** For every container, write items first and the delta token or watermark last, in the same job step. Monitor sync lag (source change to searchable) and the age of each container's cursor.","**Run permission sync on its own, faster cadence.** A separate job re-reads ACLs and group membership (Slack channels, Entra groups) more often than content, because access changes matter more than edits. Monitor permission-sync lag and unresolved-principal count.","**Budget the customer's quota explicitly, crawler and agent together.** Agree a share of the API allocation with the customer's admins, throttle the crawler to it, cap live tool calls per agent session, and honour `Retry-After` on every call. Salesforce reports org API usage in a response header and a limits endpoint; Graph returns 429 with `Retry-After`. Monitor 429 rate and calls per sync and per agent session.","**Inject identity in the runtime, not the prompt.** Retrieval and action tools take the caller's identity from the authenticated session, never from model-supplied arguments, so injected text cannot widen access. Monitor tool calls rejected for missing identity.","**Canary as a restricted test user.** Keep test users with known narrow access per source and run scheduled agent queries as them; any hit on a document they cannot open in the source is a P1. Monitor the permission-mismatch rate.","**Reconcile deletions periodically.** Even with tombstones, list all live IDs per container on a slow schedule (weekly, say) and delete index entries the source no longer has. Monitor items deleted by reconciliation; a non-zero trend means the delta path is missing deletes."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent is only as useful as the systems it can reach. Retrieval tools\n  read the synced copy; action tools (create a Jira issue, look up an SAP order) call the source live. Both\n  inherit every auth, quota and permission decision the connector makes.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The ingestion pipeline behind your vector store, the MCP servers or\n  function tools your agent calls, the tool's OAuth app registration in the customer tenant, and the\n  retrieval filter that applies ACLs before the model sees a chunk.\n- **What you control.** Sync versus live-tool per source, which identity each tool call uses (service\n  account or the end user's delegated token), per-tool call budgets so a looping agent cannot exhaust the\n  customer's API quota, and the canonical document shape every source normalizes into.\n- **What you can skip.** Memorising each vendor's object model. Know the five-step contract, how each\n  source models change and permissions, and the backfill arithmetic; read the vendor docs per project.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Internal knowledge agents.** An employee assistant answers from Confluence, SharePoint and Slack with\n  per-user ACL filtering and citations back to the source.\n- **Service desk agents.** A ServiceNow-connected agent reads knowledge articles from the index and opens\n  or updates incidents through a live tool.\n- **Sales and account research.** An agent combines synced call notes with live Salesforce lookups,\n  under a per-session API budget.\n- **Order and inventory agents.** SAP exposed as OData lookup tools, called as the user so SAP's\n  authorization objects apply.","mini_project":"**Permission-aware Confluence search tool for an agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** give an agent a `search_company_docs` tool that only returns pages the caller may read.\n- **Steps:** sync one Confluence space (or a fixture of 50 pages with fake restrictions) with an `updated`\n  watermark; store each page's allowed principals; build the tool with identity injected from the session;\n  run the same agent question as two test users with different access.\n- **Done when:** the restricted user's trace shows zero hits from the restricted pages, a deleted page\n  disappears after the next sync, and a re-run from the saved watermark fetches only changed pages."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Source system API\"] --> A[\"1. Authenticate as tenant app\"]\n    A --> E[\"2. Enumerate and page\"]\n    E -->|\"HTTP 429\"| B[\"Back off per Retry-After\"]\n    B --> E\n    E --> C[\"3. Changes since cursor\"]\n    C -->|\"tombstone\"| DEL[\"Delete from index\"]\n    C -->|\"new or changed\"| P[\"4. Capture source ACL\"]\n    P --> X[\"Crosswalk to IdP IDs\"]\n    X --> N[\"5. Normalize canonical doc\"]\n    N --> I[(\"ACL-aware index\")]\n    I --> T[\"Agent search tool, filtered per user\"]\n    C -.->|\"persist cursor last\"| K[(\"Cursor store\")]","caption":"Every source runs the same five-step contract; only the cursor and the ACL vocabulary differ. The agent's\nretrieval tool reads the ACL-aware index at the bottom."},"interview":[{"q":"What are the core responsibilities of an enterprise connector, and why standardise them?","a":"- **Five jobs:** authenticate as a tenant app, enumerate and page with backoff, track change with a cursor,\n  capture permissions, and normalize into a canonical document.\n- **Why standardise:** chunking, embedding, ACL-aware retrieval and tool calling then work identically for\n  every source, so adding the ninth source is a connector, not a pipeline rewrite."},{"q":"Your first SharePoint crawl for a 2-million-file tenant is projected at five days. How do you shorten it?","a":"- **Do the arithmetic first:** about 4 million calls at the sustained rate the tenant gives you, plus 429\n  retries.\n- **Cut calls:** read permissions per folder where inheritance is intact, and use Graph JSON batching where\n  it helps, remembering throttling still counts each inner request.\n- **Change the order, not just the speed:** crawl high-value sites first and checkpoint per container so a\n  crash resumes instead of restarting."},{"q":"A user says the agent quoted a document they cannot open. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Find the chunk and its stored ACL** from the agent trace's retrieval step, then compare with the item's\n  current permissions in the source.\n- **Common causes:** admin-token crawl with no ACL, broken inheritance indexed with the parent's audience, a\n  sharing link mapped too widely, a permission removal not yet synced, or a tool that trusted a\n  model-supplied user argument.\n- **Fix the class, not the item:** add a restricted-user canary and a faster permission-sync job."},{"q":"When would you check permissions live at query time instead of syncing them?","a":"- **When the model is too complex to replicate**, as with Salesforce sharing rules or ServiceNow scripted\n  ACLs, or SAP authorization objects.\n- **When membership churns fast**, as with Slack private channels.\n- **Cost:** a source API call per candidate document, so it suits small candidate sets and sources with\n  generous rate limits."},{"q":"Does wrapping a source as an MCP server solve integration for your agent?","a":"- **It standardises the tool interface** an agent sees, which helps reuse across agents and hosts.\n- **It does not remove** OAuth app registration, scopes, rate limits, change tracking or permission\n  enforcement; the MCP server still has to implement all of them."},{"q":"Which customer systems would you sync for the agent and which would you expose as live tools?","a":"- **Sync prose** that needs semantic search across many items: Confluence, SharePoint, Slack threads.\n- **Expose as tools** records that must be exact and current or that the agent writes: SAP orders,\n  ServiceNow incidents, Salesforce opportunities, Jira issue creation.\n- **Guard the tools** with per-session call budgets and the user's own identity, because a looping agent\n  can drain a shared API quota in minutes."}],"tradeoffs":["**Copy-and-index vs federated live search.** Your own index gives the agent fast, rankable retrieval but creates a second copy whose permissions and freshness you must keep in sync. Federated search through source APIs avoids the copy and flips the cost to latency per agent step and per-query rate limits.","**Service accounts vs delegated tokens.** Application-level service accounts make crawling simple but see more than any user, so you must replicate ACLs. Delegated user tokens enforce source permissions natively on agent actions but see only one user's slice and stop working when the refresh token or consent is revoked.","**Buy vs build.** A connector platform gets twenty sources quickly, but you inherit its permission fidelity and sync cadence. Building wins when one source is business-critical and needs field-level control.","**Index-time vs query-time permissions.** Syncing permissions at index time makes agent retrieval cheap, but changes lag by one sync cycle. Checking access live is exact but adds a source API call per candidate document, multiplied by every retrieval step the agent takes."],"pitfalls":["**Crawling with an admin token and dropping ACLs.** Symptom: a user reports the assistant quoted a document they cannot open in the source system. Fix: capture ACLs at sync and filter per user.","**Nightly full re-fetches instead of delta cursors.** Symptom: HTTP 429s, and the customer's other integrations fail during your crawl window. Fix: delta tokens or change events.","**Ignoring deletions and permission removals.** Symptom: retrieval returns links that 404 or show \"access denied\" in the source, and the agent cites them confidently.","**Treating broken inheritance as rare in SharePoint or Confluence.** Symptom: a few sensitive folders indexed with their parent site's wider audience.","**Joining users by display name or unverified email.** Symptom: ACL entries resolve to nobody (users see less than in the source) or to the wrong person after a name collision.","**Dropping sharing-link permissions because they name no principal.** Symptom: documents shared \"with the organization\" are invisible to everyone, or, if mapped carelessly, visible to guests.","**Letting the model pass the user or tenant as a tool argument.** Symptom: a red-team prompt retrieves another user's documents. Fix: bind identity in the tool runtime from the session.","**Promising a source before checking scope, approval or licence.** Teams messages, Slack DMs or a SAP module can need extra steps. Symptom: a blocked project in week three."],"when":"- **Use when** an AI feature or agent must read or act on a customer system more than once.\n- **Pilot shortcut:** for a one-off pilot, an export file may be enough.\n- **Production minimum:** delta sync, deletion handling and permission capture from day one, because\n  retrofitting ACLs onto a populated index means re-crawling it.\n- **Sync or tool:** sync prose the agent searches; call live for records it reads exactly or writes.\n- **Size before committing:** item count times calls per item divided by the sustained rate the source\n  will actually give you.\n- **Measure:** sync lag (source change to searchable), API calls per sync and per agent session, share of\n  the customer's quota, 429 rate, permission-mismatch rate from spot checks as restricted test users,\n  unresolved-principal count, and items in error.\n- **Interviews:** name the five responsibilities and explain how one specific source models permissions\n  and change.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the connector contract per source: OAuth app, paging with backoff, delta cursors persisted per container, tombstone handling and ACL extraction into the canonical document.","architect":"Decides copy-and-index versus federated query per source, build versus buy, and where permission enforcement lives so every connector feeds one ACL-aware retrieval path.","fde":"Gets the app registered and admin consent granted in the customer tenant, negotiates API quota with the customer's admins and verifies permissions on real restricted spaces before go-live."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants their assistant to answer from Confluence and Slack. Security asks how you prevent\na user from seeing content they cannot see in the source. What is your design?","a":"Capture permissions as source principals during sync: Confluence space permissions plus inherited page\nrestrictions, Slack channel membership. Resolve principals to users through the customer IdP (groups via\nSCIM or Graph) and store them on each chunk. At query time, filter retrieval by the requesting user's\nresolved principal set before ranking, never after generation. Because Slack membership changes often, sync\nmembership more frequently than content, or check membership live for private channels. Log every answer's\nsource documents so access can be audited, and spot-check by querying as a restricted test user."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do production connectors use delta tokens or change events rather than nightly full crawls?","options":["Full crawls cannot read file contents","They fetch only changes and deletions, staying within API quotas and noticing removals","Delta tokens make the content more accurate for embedding","Source systems forbid full crawls after the first one"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A full crawl repeats work, competes for the customer's shared API allocation and cannot easily see what disappeared. Delta mechanisms return just the changes, including deletion tombstones, so the index stays fresh and consistent cheaply."},{"q":"A connector indexes SharePoint using an app-only token with read access to all sites. What must it add to be safe?","options":["A higher rate limit so crawling finishes faster","Encryption of the vector store at rest","Per-item permissions captured at sync and enforced as a retrieval filter for the requesting user","A rule that only site owners may use the assistant"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An app-only token sees everything, so the index must carry each item's ACL, including broken inheritance, and filter by the user's principals. Encryption protects storage but does nothing about authorized users seeing too much."},{"q":"Why is SAP more often integrated as an agent tool than as a document corpus?","options":["SAP has no API of any kind","SAP data is always encrypted and cannot be read","Its data is structured transactional records with fine-grained authorization best enforced by calling SAP itself","Embedding models cannot process German text"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Orders, materials and invoices are rows, not prose, and change constantly; querying them live through OData or BAPI calls returns current values and lets SAP's own authorization objects apply, which embedding snapshots would bypass."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/legacy-system-integration","sources":["Hohpe & Woolf, 2003 — Enterprise Integration Patterns","Evans, 2003 — Domain-Driven Design","Fowler, 2004 — StranglerFigApplication (bliki)"],"why":"- **The system of record is old and staying.** In most large enterprises policies, accounts, claims or\n  inventory live in mainframes, AS/400s, old ERPs or in-house databases. They work, they are audited, and\n  nobody will rewrite them for your assistant.\n- **Only they can answer.** \"What is the status of claim 4471?\" or \"raise a return for this order\" can only\n  be answered by that system.\n- **Ad-hoc SQL on production competes for capacity.** It fights the batch jobs and online transactions the\n  system was sized for, and on a mainframe CPU consumption can translate directly into the customer's\n  software bill.\n- **Writing to its tables corrupts state.** It bypasses business rules that live in application code, in\n  ways nobody notices until month-end.\n- **Copying its model spreads legacy semantics.** Cryptic eight-character field names, status code `07` and\n  dates as `YYDDD` leak everywhere, so every later change touches every consumer. Treating it like a cloud\n  API (retry instantly, call per user request, expect sub-second latency) collides with nightly batch\n  windows, single-threaded transaction monitors and maintenance freezes.","how":"### 1. Choose the access path, least invasive first\nOrdered roughly from safest to riskiest, *because* each step down couples you (and every agent tool) more\ntightly to internals:\n\n| Path | What it is | Why choose or avoid |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Existing interface** | A SOAP service, an MQ queue (IBM MQ is common on mainframes), a published batch extract, or an ESB/iPaaS flow someone maintains | Reusing it inherits its rules and its support |\n| **Batch file exchange** | Fixed-width or delimited files over SFTP on a schedule | Crude but decoupled: the legacy side controls load, and files are replayable |\n| **Read replica or CDC** | Read a replica, or capture changes from the database log | Reads never touch the primary; writes still go through the application |\n| **Transaction wrappers** | Expose an existing transaction program (for example a CICS transaction) through a gateway | Business rules still run on writes |\n| **Screen automation (RPA)** | Drive the green-screen or desktop UI | Last resort *because* any UI change breaks it, but sometimes the only path that runs full validation |\n\n### 2. Build an anti-corruption layer\nA translation service owned by your side converts legacy records into your canonical model and back:\n- **Decodes encodings:** EBCDIC, COBOL `COMP-3` packed decimal, implied decimal points.\n- **Maps codes to named states**, and normalizes dates and time zones, so the model reads \"pending\n  assessor review\" instead of guessing what `07` means.\n- **Exposes domain operations** such as `get_claim(id)`, hiding access-path details.\n- **Is the only interface consumers see, including LLM tools**, *because* when the legacy system is\n  eventually replaced, only this layer changes and the agent's tool schemas stay put.\n\nTwo encoding traps cause most \"the numbers are wrong\" incidents:\n1. **Zoned signs.** Signed numeric fields stored as display digits (`PIC S9(5)V99` without `COMP-3`) carry\n   the sign in the zone half of the last byte. After conversion the final character looks like a letter or\n   brace rather than a digit.\n2. **Mixed records converted as text.** Text fields beside packed binary fields must be transferred as\n   binary and decoded field by field from the copybook, *because* a transfer step that converts the whole\n   file from EBCDIC to ASCII rewrites the packed bytes as characters and destroys them without any error.\n\n### 3. Protect the legacy system from the agent\n- **Cache or replica in front of reads, a queue in front of writes**, and explicit concurrency limits\n  everywhere.\n- **Size the limit with Little's law:** concurrent calls = arrival rate × call latency.\n- **Worked number:** an illustrative 20 lookups per second at 1.5 seconds per legacy transaction means\n  about 30 calls in flight. If operations allows 10 concurrent sessions, you must cache, batch, or shed load,\n  *because* the gateway will otherwise queue requests until user-facing timeouts fire and retries make it worse.\n- **Agents amplify load.** One user question can fan out into several tool calls, and a model that sees an\n  error often retries the same call, so budget legacy calls per agent session as well as per second.\n- **Respect batch windows.** Schedule bulk extracts when the system says, and make agent tools return\n  \"system unavailable, try after 06:00\" rather than hammering it.\n\n### 4. Make writes safe\nA timeout on a legacy transaction has three possible meanings, and legacy transactions are rarely idempotent:\n\n| Meaning | Is a blind retry safe? |\n|---|---|\n| The request never arrived | Yes |\n| It ran and the reply was lost | No: duplicate write |\n| It is still running | No: duplicate write |\n\nSo:\n1. **Attach a client reference** to every write and record it in your own store before sending.\n2. **Put it in a field the legacy system persists and can be queried by** (a customer reference or batch\n   reference field, agreed with the system's owners).\n3. **After a timeout, look the reference up before retrying**; if it exists, the write happened.\n4. **No queryable field?** Route writes through a single queue consumer and confirm them against the next\n   extract, *because* only reconciliation can then tell you which in-doubt writes landed.\n5. **Tell the model the truth.** The tool result says \"applied\", \"scheduled\" or \"in doubt, reference X\", so\n   the agent never tells a customer a change is done when it is only queued.\n\n### 5. Migrate incrementally if replacing\n- **Strangler fig:** route one capability at a time from the legacy system to the new one behind a facade,\n  *because* big-bang rewrites of systems with decades of undocumented rules routinely fail. The agent's\n  tools sit on the facade, so they survive the migration.","example":"### Decoding a fixed-width EBCDIC extract\nA nightly SFTP extract contains fixed-width 30-byte records in EBCDIC: customer id `PIC X(10)`, name\n`PIC X(16)`, balance `PIC S9(5)V99 COMP-3` (seven digits plus a sign nibble = 4 bytes, two implied\ndecimals). The anti-corruption layer decodes it into a typed record the agent's lookup tool can return.\n\n```python\nfrom decimal import Decimal\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Customer:\n    customer_id: str\n    name: str\n    balance: Decimal\n\ndef unpack_comp3(raw: bytes, scale: int) -> Decimal:\n    \"\"\"COBOL packed decimal: two digits per byte, last nibble is the sign (C/F positive, D negative).\"\"\"\n    nibbles = []\n    for b in raw:\n        nibbles += [b >> 4, b & 0x0F]\n    sign = nibbles.pop()                       # final nibble\n    if sign < 0x0A or any(n > 9 for n in nibbles):   # A-F are signs, 0-9 digits\n        raise ValueError(f\"corrupt packed field {raw.hex()}\")\n    value = int(\"\".join(map(str, nibbles)))\n    if sign in (0x0B, 0x0D):                   # D preferred negative, B alternate\n        value = -value\n    return Decimal(value).scaleb(-scale)\n\ndef parse_record(rec: bytes) -> Customer:\n    assert len(rec) == 30, len(rec)\n    return Customer(\n        customer_id=rec[0:10].decode(\"cp037\").strip(),   # cp037 = EBCDIC US/Canada\n        name=rec[10:26].decode(\"cp037\").strip(),\n        balance=unpack_comp3(rec[26:30], scale=2),\n    )\n\n# 12345.67 packs to 12 34 56 7C; -1.50 packs to 00 00 15 0D\nassert unpack_comp3(bytes.fromhex(\"1234567C\"), 2) == Decimal(\"12345.67\")\nassert unpack_comp3(bytes.fromhex(\"0000150D\"), 2) == Decimal(\"-1.50\")\n```\n\n- **Wrong decoding fails silently.** Reading the file as UTF-8 raises decode errors or produces garbage\n  names. Worse, treating the packed bytes as text (or decoding them with `cp037`, which maps every byte to\n  some character) silently produces plausible-looking wrong balances that an agent will quote.\n- **So the layout comes first.** The COBOL copybook is the first artifact to request from the customer.\n\n### Idempotent write tool for an in-doubt timeout\nThe reference is recorded before sending and looked up after a timeout, so a lost reply never becomes a\nsecond write even if the agent calls the tool again. The fake gateway applies the write and then loses the\nreply once.\n\n```python\nimport uuid\n\nclass LegacyTimeout(Exception):\n    pass\n\ndef update_contact(pending: dict, gateway, claim_id: str, address: str) -> str:\n    ref = uuid.uuid4().hex[:12].upper()            # client reference, agreed field width\n    pending[ref] = (claim_id, address)             # 1. record BEFORE sending\n    for _ in range(3):\n        try:\n            gateway.send(claim_id, address, ref)   # 2. ref goes in a field the system persists\n            break\n        except LegacyTimeout:\n            # 3. wait out the transaction's max run time (not shown), then look before retrying\n            if gateway.find_by_reference(ref):\n                break                              # it ran; only the reply was lost\n    else:\n        return f\"in-doubt {ref}: confirm against next extract\"\n    del pending[ref]\n    return f\"applied {ref}\"\n\nclass FakeGateway:                                 # applies the write, then loses the reply once\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.rows, self.lose_reply = {}, True\n    def send(self, claim_id, address, ref):\n        self.rows[ref] = (claim_id, address)\n        if self.lose_reply:\n            self.lose_reply = False\n            raise LegacyTimeout()\n    def find_by_reference(self, ref):\n        return ref in self.rows\n\ngw, pending = FakeGateway(), {}\nprint(update_contact(pending, gw, \"CLM4471\", \"1 New St\"))\nprint(len(gw.rows), pending)                       # 1 {}: applied once, nothing in doubt\n```\n\n- **The string goes back to the model.** \"in-doubt\" tells the agent to report a pending change and stop,\n  rather than calling the tool a fourth time.\n- **Key the agent's retries too.** If the agent framework retries a whole tool call, pass a stable\n  idempotency key from the agent step so the second call reuses the first reference.\n\n### Worked capacity check with Little's law\nAgent lookups against a claims mainframe that allows 10 concurrent sessions (illustrative figures).\n\n| Scenario | Arrival rate to legacy | Latency | In flight | Fits 10? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Every lookup hits the mainframe | 20/s | 1.5 s | 30 | No |\n| Read cache absorbs 70% of lookups | 6/s | 1.5 s | 9 | Yes, barely |\n| Cache plus batching 2 lookups per call | 3 calls/s | 1.5 s | 4.5 | Yes, with headroom |\n\nThe cache is the lever, because latency is fixed by the legacy system and arrival rate is the only thing\nyou control. An agent that averages 2.5 claim lookups per conversation turns 8 user questions per second\ninto those 20 lookups per second, so per-session caching of the same claim matters as much as the shared cache.","patterns":["**Bulkhead the legacy calls.** Wrap every call in a limiter sized to the session count operations agrees (for example `asyncio.Semaphore(10)` per gateway in Python), and fail fast with a clear message when it is full. Monitor in-flight calls, queue wait time and rejections.","**Maintenance-aware tools.** Keep the batch and maintenance calendar in config and check it before calling, so agent tools answer \"system unavailable, try after 06:00\" during the window. Monitor calls attempted inside windows (should be zero).","**Per-session tool budgets.** Cap legacy calls per agent conversation and return a \"limit reached\" result the model can explain, because a retry loop in one session can consume the shared session pool. Monitor calls per session at p50 and p99.","**Binary transfer plus copybook-driven parsing.** Move mixed files without code-page conversion (FTP in binary mode, or SFTP, which does not convert), then decode per field from the copybook. Quarantine records that fail validation and alert on parse rejects per extract.","**Reference-tagged writes with daily reconciliation.** Every write carries a client reference; a daily job matches pending references against the next extract and pages on any still in doubt. Monitor in-doubt writes and how they resolved.","**Status-code translation table, owned and versioned.** Keep the mapping from codes like `07` to named states in one reviewed table in the anti-corruption layer, and fail on unknown codes rather than passing them through to the model. Monitor unknown-code counts."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** \"What is the status of claim 4471?\" and \"raise a return for this order\"\n  are tool calls that end in a legacy system. Agents call faster, retry more eagerly and loop more than\n  humans at terminals, so an unprotected legacy tool turns one confused agent into an outage.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Behind your function tools or MCP server: a domain operation such as\n  `get_claim(id)`, a concurrency limiter, a read cache, a write queue with client references, and a batch\n  calendar the tool checks before calling.\n- **What you control.** Which access path each tool uses, what the tool returns to the model (named states,\n  never raw codes like `07`), how timeouts are reported (in-doubt, not failed), and the agent-visible\n  message during maintenance windows.\n- **What you can skip.** Writing COBOL or CICS programs. You need to read a copybook, decode `COMP-3`, apply\n  Little's law and make writes idempotent; the legacy owners run the rest.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Insurance claims agents.** Look up claim status and update contact details through a CICS gateway,\n  with client references on every write.\n- **Order and returns agents on old ERPs.** Read order state from a replica; raise returns through the\n  existing MQ interface so business rules run.\n- **Banking assistants over nightly extracts.** Answer balance and transaction questions from parsed\n  EBCDIC files, stating the as-of time.\n- **Strangler migrations.** Agent tools sit on a facade that routes each capability to the old or new\n  system as it migrates.","mini_project":"**Protected legacy lookup tool for an agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** give a tool-calling agent a `get_customer` tool backed by a fake slow legacy system that allows\n  only 3 concurrent calls.\n- **Steps:** generate a fixed-width EBCDIC fixture with `COMP-3` balances and parse it; wrap a fake gateway\n  with 1.5-second latency in a semaphore, a read cache and a maintenance calendar; map status codes to named\n  states; run 20 concurrent agent conversations against it.\n- **Done when:** in-flight calls never exceed 3, the agent reports maintenance windows instead of retrying,\n  balances match the fixture exactly, and a trace shows each customer fetched once per conversation."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Agent tool or service\"] --> ACL[\"Anti-corruption layer\"]\n    ACL -->|\"get_claim(id)\"| C[(\"Read cache or replica\")]\n    ACL -->|\"writes with client ref\"| Q[\"Write queue\"]\n    C -->|\"miss\"| L[\"Concurrency limiter\"]\n    Q --> L\n    L --> P{\"Access path\"}\n    P --> I[\"Existing MQ or SOAP\"]\n    P --> F[\"Batch file over SFTP\"]\n    P --> T[\"CICS transaction gateway\"]\n    I --> M[(\"Legacy system of record\")]\n    F --> M\n    T --> M","caption":"Agent tools only ever see domain operations; protection and translation sit between them and the legacy system."},"interview":[{"q":"What is an anti-corruption layer and why put one in front of a legacy system?","a":"- **Definition:** a translation service you own that decodes legacy formats, maps codes to named states and\n  exposes domain operations like `get_claim(id)`.\n- **Why:** legacy semantics stop at one boundary, so consumers (including LLM tools) never learn status\n  codes or copybook layouts, and replacing the legacy system later changes only this layer."},{"q":"Balances from a new mainframe extract are wildly wrong but names look fine. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Suspect the transfer first:** a text-mode transfer or gateway converting the whole record from EBCDIC\n  to ASCII rewrites packed `COMP-3` bytes without any error.\n- **Check against the copybook:** confirm field offsets, `COMP-3` versus zoned display digits, and implied\n  decimals.\n- **Fix:** transfer as binary, decode field by field, and validate nibbles so corruption raises instead of\n  producing plausible numbers."},{"q":"An agent tool needs 20 lookups per second but the mainframe allows 10 sessions at 1.5 s each. What do you do?","a":"- **Little's law:** 20 × 1.5 = 30 calls in flight, three times the limit.\n- **Levers:** a read cache or replica, batching lookups, nightly extracts for data that tolerates staleness,\n  and load shedding with a clear message.\n- **Never** just raise timeouts and retry, because that grows the queue and the retries make it worse."},{"q":"When would you accept screen automation, and when would you refuse it?","a":"- **Accept** when volumes are low, it is the only path that runs full validation, and a proper interface is\n  on the roadmap.\n- **Refuse** for high-volume or latency-sensitive paths, because any UI change breaks it and it runs at\n  human interaction speed."},{"q":"How do you make a write to a non-idempotent legacy transaction safe to retry?","a":"- **Client reference recorded before sending**, carried in a field the system persists and can be queried by.\n- **After a timeout, look it up before retrying**, because the request may have run with the reply lost, or\n  still be running.\n- **No queryable field:** single queue consumer plus reconciliation against the next extract."},{"q":"Your agent keeps calling a legacy lookup tool after it times out, and operations complain. How do you fix the agent side?","a":"- **Return a structured result, not an exception string.** \"System busy, retry not useful for 10 minutes\"\n  or \"in maintenance until 06:00\" gives the model a reason to stop.\n- **Enforce a per-session budget in the runtime**, so the model cannot exceed N legacy calls whatever it\n  decides.\n- **Cache per conversation** so the same claim is fetched once, and trace tool calls per session to find\n  prompts that trigger loops."}],"tradeoffs":["**Batch files: decoupled vs stale.** Files are decoupled and replayable, but data is as stale as the last extract. It flips to CDC or a queue when users need answers about changes made in the last hour, and the agent must say how old its data is.","**Replica: protects the primary vs lag.** Replication lag means a just-submitted transaction may be invisible; read-your-writes flows (the agent writes, then reads back to confirm) must query the primary or wait.","**Screen automation: reach vs fragility.** It reaches logic no API exposes, but breaks on any UI change and is slow. Acceptable only when volumes are low and a proper interface is on the roadmap; computer-use agents do not change that trade-off.","**Anti-corruption layer: a service to run vs leaked semantics.** Without it every consumer, including every prompt, learns legacy codes; it stops paying off only for a single throwaway consumer."],"pitfalls":["**Querying production during the batch window.** Symptom: the nightly job overruns and operations asks who is running unplanned queries. Fix: a maintenance-aware tool.","**Wrong code page or ignored packed decimals.** Symptom: mojibake in names or balances off by orders of magnitude.","**Retrying non-idempotent writes on timeout.** Symptom: duplicate orders or payments discovered at reconciliation.","**Raw legacy codes as tool output.** Symptom: the model guesses what status `07` means and confidently tells a customer the wrong thing. Fix: map to named states in the layer.","**Text-mode transfer of mixed text-and-packed files**, or a gateway converting code pages for the whole record. Symptom: names look fine while numeric fields are garbage or fail validation.","**Assuming 24/7 availability.** Symptom: agent tools fail every night during maintenance with unhelpful error messages, and the agent retries them."],"when":"- **Use when** an AI feature or agent tool must read from or write to a system you cannot change.\n- **Ask first, code second:** existing interfaces, the batch schedule, the record layouts and the capacity\n  constraints.\n- **Release order:** read-only tools first; add write tools only through a path that runs the system's own\n  business rules, with idempotency references and, for consequential writes, human approval.\n- **Measure:** data freshness against the batch schedule, load on the legacy system (calls, concurrency, CPU\n  where the owners can report it), in-doubt writes and how they resolved, parse rejects per extract, and\n  reconciliation mismatches.\n- **Interviews:** explain why you would not point an agent at the mainframe database directly, and what you\n  would do instead; that is the core signal.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the anti-corruption layer: copybook-driven parsers, code-to-state mappings, idempotent write wrappers and concurrency limits around legacy calls.","architect":"Chooses the access path per capability, sets the boundary where legacy semantics stop, and plans strangler-style replacement rather than coupling new AI features to internals.","fde":"Finds the person who knows the batch schedule and copybooks, gets SFTP or MQ access approved, and validates parsed records against what the legacy screens show."},"check":{"q":"An insurer wants an agent that can update a claim's contact address. The claims system is a mainframe\nwith no API, a nightly batch and a green-screen UI. How do you approach it?","a":"First look for an existing interface: an MQ-fed transaction, a CICS transaction exposed through a\ngateway, or a batch update file the system already ingests. Any of these runs the system's own validation.\nPrefer them over direct table writes, which bypass rules, and over screen automation, which is brittle.\nWrap the chosen path in an anti-corruption layer with a domain operation like `update_contact(claim_id,\naddress, ref)`, record the reference before sending and, after a timeout, look it up in the claims system before\nretrying, so an in-doubt write is never applied twice, and queue requests so the\nagent never overloads the system or calls during batch. If only a nightly file exists, make the agent say\nthe change is scheduled, not done."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is screen automation usually the last-resort integration path?","options":["It cannot read data, only write it","Any UI change breaks it and it is slow, even though it does run the application's business rules","Mainframes do not allow terminal sessions","It requires rewriting the legacy application"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Driving the UI does pass through full validation logic, which is its appeal, but it couples you to screen layouts that change without notice and runs at human interaction speed, so it suits low volumes only."},{"q":"What is the main purpose of an anti-corruption layer?","options":["To encrypt legacy data before it leaves the data centre","To replace the legacy system with a modern one","To translate the legacy model into your own so legacy semantics do not spread into every consumer","To filter malicious prompts before they reach the mainframe"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The layer decodes formats, maps codes to named states and exposes domain operations, so only it changes when the legacy system changes or is replaced; replacement itself is the strangler fig's job."},{"q":"A packed-decimal balance field is read as text. What is the most likely outcome?","options":["An exception is always raised immediately","The value is correct but loses its sign","Garbled or plausible-but-wrong values flow through unless the parser validates the field","The database rejects the record"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Packed decimal stores two digits per byte plus a sign nibble, so text decoding yields nonsense characters or numbers that look valid; explicit COMP-3 decoding with nibble validation is required."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/sso-iam-integration","sources":["Official docs: Microsoft identity platform (Entra ID)","Official docs: Okta Developer","OpenID Foundation, 2014 — OpenID Connect Core 1.0"],"why":"- **Enterprises run identity centrally.** Thousands of employees join, move and leave. Security enforces\n  MFA, device posture and conditional access in one place, the IdP, and audits who can reach which app.\n- **A vendor password store breaks every control.** Leavers keep access, MFA policy is bypassed, and\n  passwords get reused. So \"supports SSO with our Okta / Entra tenant\" is a procurement checkbox, often a\n  hard gate.\n- **\"Add a Sign in with Microsoft button\" works for one tenant only.** As a B2B product it fails:\n  - accepting any Entra token lets any organization's users into any customer's workspace;\n  - keying by email breaks when an address changes or a mailbox is reassigned to a new hire;\n  - reading group names fails when groups are renamed, or when a user has so many groups the token omits them.\n- **Sign-in says nothing about the day someone is fired.** Your session keeps working unless you wire\n  deprovisioning in, and so does any background agent task started under it. SSO integration is the set of\n  decisions that make federated identity safe across many customer tenants.","how":"### Roles and protocols\n| | OIDC | SAML |\n|---|---|---|\n| Your app's role | Relying party | Service provider |\n| What comes back | Signed JWT ID token | Signed XML assertion |\n| When | Default for new integrations | Still required by many enterprises (see the SAML lesson) |\n| Okta and Entra ID | Supported | Supported |\n\n### The login flow (OIDC authorization code with PKCE)\n1. **Redirect to the tenant's authorization endpoint** with `client_id`, `redirect_uri`,\n   `scope=openid profile email`, a random `state`, a random `nonce`, and a PKCE `code_challenge`. Store\n   `state`, `nonce` and the PKCE verifier in the pre-login session, *because* each must later match *this*\n   browser:\n   - `state` defeats login CSRF;\n   - `nonce` ties the ID token to this request;\n   - PKCE stops a stolen authorization code from being redeemed by someone else.\n2. **The IdP authenticates and applies its policies** (MFA, device compliance, conditional access), then\n   redirects back with a short-lived `code`.\n3. **Exchange the code at the token endpoint**, authenticating as the client (secret or, better, a\n   certificate or private-key JWT). You receive an ID token (and an access token if you asked for API scopes).\n4. **Validate the ID token, resolve the user, and issue your own session.** The IdP's tokens are not your\n   session, *because* their lifetime and revocation are controlled elsewhere and you still need your own\n   idle and absolute timeouts.\n\n- **Step-up:** the ID token's `acr`/`amr` claims say how the user authenticated. If a tenant requires\n  stronger authentication for some actions (an agent about to approve a payment, say), send them back to the\n  IdP rather than trusting a stale login.\n\n### Per-tenant configuration\n- **Store per tenant:** protocol, issuer (or SAML entity ID), client ID, discovery or metadata URL, allowed\n  email domains, and claim mappings.\n- **Home realm discovery first**, *because* each customer brings its own IdP: the user types an email or\n  picks a workspace, and you route them to that tenant's IdP.\n- **Entra ID:** a SaaS app is typically registered once as multi-tenant and each customer's admin grants consent.\n- **Okta:** each customer org adds your app (often from the Okta Integration Network) and you receive an\n  org-specific issuer.\n- **Okta has two issuer shapes.** The org authorization server (issuer `https://<org>.okta.com`) differs\n  from custom authorization servers (issuer `https://<org>.okta.com/oauth2/<id>`, `default` being the common\n  one). They have different issuers and keys, so record which one the customer configured.\n\n### Validating the token\nFor every ID token check:\n1. **Signature** against the issuer's JWKS.\n2. **`iss`** equals the configured issuer for *this* tenant.\n3. **`aud`** equals your client ID.\n4. **`exp`/`nbf`** with small clock skew.\n5. **`nonce`** equals the one you sent.\n\nThe Entra multi-tenant hole:\n- **The issuer contains the tenant ID (`tid`)**, so you must check that `tid` belongs to a customer you\n  onboarded. Signature validity alone proves only that *some* Entra tenant issued it.\n- **Why it is common:** the multi-tenant discovery document (`/organizations` or `/common`) advertises an\n  issuer containing a `{tenantid}` placeholder. Generic libraries cannot compare against it literally, and\n  the tempting fix is to switch issuer validation off.\n- **Correct fix:** substitute the onboarded tenant's ID and compare exactly.\n\n### Stable identity\n- **Key users by issuer plus `sub`, or in Entra by `tid` plus `oid`**, *because* those are immutable for\n  the account; email and UPN are display attributes that change. Use the same key on agent traces, memory\n  stores and audit logs, so a user's agent memory never follows a recycled mailbox to a new hire.\n\n### Authorization inputs\n- **Prefer admin-assigned app roles (Entra) or a filtered groups claim (Okta)** over raw group lists.\n- **Entra caps groups in a token:** its documentation gives 200 for JWTs and 150 for SAML assertions.\n  Above the cap it omits them and signals an overage (`_claim_names`/`_claim_sources` in a JWT), and you\n  must fetch membership from Microsoft Graph.\n- **Emit only groups assigned to the app** to avoid most overage, *because* users rarely hold more than a\n  few of those.\n- **Map group or role *IDs*, not display names.**\n\n### Lifecycle and delegation\n- **SSO controls sign-in, not existence.** Pair it with SCIM so deactivation reaches you, and keep session\n  lifetime short enough that a disabled user cannot ride an old cookie for days.\n- **Worst-case exposure after termination** ≈ time for SCIM deactivation to arrive + whatever your app does\n  not revoke on receipt. If you revoke sessions on SCIM events, session length matters only as a backstop.\n- **Back-channel logout:** where the IdP supports OIDC back-channel logout, it can tell you to end a\n  session directly.\n- **Agents act as the user.** When an agent calls downstream APIs as the user, use a delegation flow such\n  as Entra's on-behalf-of or OAuth token exchange, so the downstream system sees the user's identity and\n  scopes rather than a god-mode service account.\n- **Long-running agent tasks need a lifecycle too.** A background task holding a refresh token outlives the\n  browser session, so revoke the task's tokens on SCIM deactivation as well, and re-check the user is\n  active before each consequential step.","example":"### Validating ID tokens for many tenants with PyJWT\nThe tenant record comes from your onboarding table; the issuer and JWKS come from that tenant's discovery\ndocument.\n\n```python\nimport jwt, requests\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\nTENANTS = {   # from your DB, written during customer onboarding\n    \"acme\":   {\"issuer\": \"https://login.microsoftonline.com/<acme-tenant-id>/v2.0\",\n               \"client_id\": \"<our-app-client-id>\"},\n    \"globex\": {\"issuer\": \"https://globex.okta.com/oauth2/default\",\n               \"client_id\": \"<our-okta-client-id>\"},\n}\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=256)\ndef jwks_client(issuer: str) -> jwt.PyJWKClient:\n    conf = requests.get(f\"{issuer}/.well-known/openid-configuration\", timeout=10).json()\n    return jwt.PyJWKClient(conf[\"jwks_uri\"])\n\ndef validate_id_token(tenant_slug: str, token: str, expected_nonce: str) -> dict:\n    t = TENANTS[tenant_slug]                       # routed by home realm discovery, never by the token\n    key = jwks_client(t[\"issuer\"]).get_signing_key_from_jwt(token).key\n    claims = jwt.decode(\n        token, key, algorithms=[\"RS256\"],\n        audience=t[\"client_id\"], issuer=t[\"issuer\"],  # rejects tokens from any other tenant\n        leeway=60, options={\"require\": [\"exp\", \"iat\", \"iss\", \"aud\", \"sub\"]},\n    )\n    if claims.get(\"nonce\") != expected_nonce:\n        raise PermissionError(\"nonce mismatch (possible replay)\")\n    user_key = (claims[\"iss\"], claims.get(\"oid\") or claims[\"sub\"])  # stable, not email\n    return {\"user_key\": user_key, \"email\": claims.get(\"email\"),\n            \"roles\": claims.get(\"roles\", []), \"groups\": claims.get(\"groups\", [])}\n```\n\n- **The crucial line is `issuer=t[\"issuer\"]`**, chosen from the tenant the user was routed to. A valid\n  token from Globex's Okta presented to Acme's workspace fails validation instead of logging someone in.\n\n### Home realm discovery and the `{tenantid}` substitution\nPure Python, runnable: route by a verified-domain table, then build the exact Entra issuer to compare.\n\n```python\nTENANT_BY_DOMAIN = {\"acme.com\": \"acme\", \"acme.co.uk\": \"acme\", \"globex.com\": \"globex\"}\nONBOARDED_ENTRA = {\"acme\": \"11111111-2222-3333-4444-555555555555\"}   # tid stored at onboarding\nISSUER_TEMPLATE = \"https://login.microsoftonline.com/{tenantid}/v2.0\"  # as /organizations advertises\n\ndef home_realm(email: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Route by verified-domain table before login; the token never picks the tenant.\"\"\"\n    domain = email.rsplit(\"@\", 1)[-1].lower()\n    if domain not in TENANT_BY_DOMAIN:\n        raise LookupError(f\"no SSO configured for {domain}\")\n    return TENANT_BY_DOMAIN[domain]\n\ndef expected_entra_issuer(tenant: str) -> str:\n    return ISSUER_TEMPLATE.replace(\"{tenantid}\", ONBOARDED_ENTRA[tenant])  # exact compare, never skip\n\ndef check_entra_claims(tenant: str, claims: dict) -> tuple:\n    tid = ONBOARDED_ENTRA[tenant]\n    if claims.get(\"tid\") != tid or claims.get(\"iss\") != expected_entra_issuer(tenant):\n        raise PermissionError(\"token from a tenant this workspace did not onboard\")\n    return (tid, claims[\"oid\"])                                     # stable user key\n\nt = home_realm(\"Ana@ACME.com\")\nprint(expected_entra_issuer(t))   # .../11111111-2222-3333-4444-555555555555/v2.0\n```\n\n### An agent tool calling Microsoft Graph on behalf of the user\nThe agent backend holds the user's access token for itself and exchanges it for a Graph token with the\non-behalf-of grant, so Graph enforces the user's own permissions. Uses MSAL for Python.\n\n```python\nimport msal, requests\n\napp = msal.ConfidentialClientApplication(\n    client_id=\"<agent-backend-client-id>\",\n    authority=\"https://login.microsoftonline.com/<acme-tenant-id>\",   # the user's tenant, not /common\n    client_credential=\"<secret-or-certificate>\",\n)\n\ndef list_my_recent_files(user_access_token: str) -> list[str]:\n    \"\"\"Tool: runs as the signed-in user; the model never sees either token.\"\"\"\n    result = app.acquire_token_on_behalf_of(user_assertion=user_access_token,\n                                            scopes=[\"https://graph.microsoft.com/Files.Read\"])\n    if \"access_token\" not in result:                 # consent missing, user disabled, MFA needed\n        return [f\"auth error: {result.get('error')}\"]  # tell the agent, do not fall back to an app token\n    r = requests.get(\"https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/me/drive/recent\",\n                     headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {result['access_token']}\"}, timeout=15)\n    r.raise_for_status()\n    return [item[\"name\"] for item in r.json()[\"value\"]][:10]\n```\n\n- **No fallback to app-only credentials.** If the exchange fails, the tool reports it, because silently\n  switching to a service token would let the agent see more than the user can.\n\n### Worked scenario: how long does a leaver keep access?\nExposure ≈ deactivation delivery time + what the app does not revoke. Figures are illustrative.\n\n| Setup | SCIM delivery | App behaviour on deactivation | Worst-case exposure |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| JIT only, no SCIM, 7-day sessions | never | nothing | up to 7 days, longer if sessions slide |\n| SCIM, sessions not revoked, 8-hour absolute session | 40 min | marks user inactive only | about 8 h 40 min |\n| SCIM with session revocation | 40 min | kills sessions and tokens | about 40 min |\n| SCIM + revocation + back-channel logout | 40 min | IdP also ends session directly | minutes |\n\n- **Add agents to the table.** A scheduled agent task that stored a refresh token keeps running after the\n  browser session dies unless the deactivation handler also cancels tasks and revokes their tokens.","patterns":["**Tenant-bound validation with a negative test.** Resolve the tenant from the login route, compare `iss` and `tid` exactly, and keep a CI test that presents a token from an unregistered tenant and must fail. Monitor validation failures by reason (issuer, audience, expiry, nonce).","**JWKS caching that survives key rotation.** Cache each issuer's keys, and on an unknown `kid` refetch the JWKS once before rejecting, so IdP rotation does not cause a login outage. Monitor JWKS fetches and unknown-`kid` errors per tenant.","**Revoke on SCIM deactivation, including agent tasks.** Treat a SCIM `active: false` or delete as a revoke-all-sessions event that also cancels the user's scheduled agent runs and drops stored refresh tokens, with session length as a backstop only. Monitor time from IdP deactivation to loss of access, tested monthly with a canary account.","**Delegated tokens per tool call.** Exchange the user's token for a downstream token scoped to that tool (on-behalf-of or token exchange), keep tokens out of the model's context, and log the stable user key on every tool span. Monitor exchange failures by error code.","**App roles over groups.** Ask the customer admin to assign app roles (Entra) or filter the groups claim (Okta) to app-specific groups; keep a Graph fallback for overage. Monitor how often overage occurs.","**Per-tenant login dashboards.** Track login success rate per tenant so a customer's IdP change (new certificate, renamed claim, moved authorization server) shows up as one tenant's dip, not a support queue."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Every tool call an agent makes is an action by someone. SSO tells you\n  who the user is and which tenant they belong to; that identity decides which documents retrieval may\n  return and which APIs the agent may call on their behalf.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The login in front of your agent UI, the session your agent runtime\n  reads the user from, the on-behalf-of or token-exchange step before each downstream tool call, and the\n  OAuth flows of MCP servers that call enterprise APIs.\n- **What you control.** Per-tenant issuer validation, the stable user key you log on every agent trace,\n  how roles from the token map to agent capabilities, session lifetime for long-running agent tasks, and\n  never handing the agent a shared god-mode credential.\n- **What you can skip.** Implementing OIDC yourself or memorising every claim. Use a maintained library;\n  know the five validation checks, the Entra `tid` hole, groups overage and delegation flows.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Employee copilots behind corporate SSO.** The agent UI signs users in through the customer's Okta or\n  Entra tenant and logs the stable user key on every trace.\n- **Agents that act in Microsoft 365 or Jira.** Each tool call exchanges the user's token on-behalf-of, so\n  downstream permissions and audit logs stay per person.\n- **Multi-tenant agent platforms.** Home realm discovery and tenant-bound validation keep one customer's\n  users out of another's workspace and memory.\n- **Scheduled and background agents.** Deactivation handlers cancel runs and revoke stored tokens when an\n  employee leaves.","mini_project":"**Delegated identity for a two-tool agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** run a small agent whose tools act as the signed-in user, never as a shared account.\n- **Steps:** set up an IdP tenant you administer (an Okta developer org or an Entra test tenant) with two\n  test users; implement OIDC login with\n  per-tenant issuer checks; give the agent a \"list my files\" or \"my profile\" tool that performs an\n  on-behalf-of or token-exchange call; log the stable user key on each tool span; add a negative test with a\n  token from another tenant.\n- **Done when:** the two users get different tool results for the same prompt, the foreign-tenant token is\n  rejected in CI, and disabling a user in the IdP makes their next tool call fail with a clear auth error\n  (bypass or clear the token cache for this test, since a cached downstream token stays valid until it\n  expires)."},"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant U as Browser\n    participant A as Agent app\n    participant I as Tenant IdP\n    participant D as Downstream API\n    U->>A: Enter email or pick workspace\n    Note over A: Home realm discovery picks tenant config\n    A->>U: Redirect with state, nonce, PKCE challenge\n    U->>I: Authorize, MFA and conditional access\n    I->>U: Redirect back with short-lived code\n    U->>A: Callback with code and state\n    A->>I: Exchange code plus PKCE verifier\n    Note over A: Check sig, iss, tid, aud, exp, nonce\n    A->>I: On-behalf-of exchange for tool call\n    A->>D: Tool call with user-scoped token","caption":"The tenant is chosen before the IdP is contacted, the app issues its own session only after validation,\nand the agent's downstream calls carry a delegated token derived from that user."},"interview":[{"q":"What do `state`, `nonce` and PKCE each protect against in the OIDC code flow?","a":"- **`state`** is matched against the browser's pre-login session, which defeats login CSRF.\n- **`nonce`** is echoed inside the signed ID token, tying that token to this request and blocking replay.\n- **PKCE** binds the authorization code to a verifier only your client holds, so a stolen code cannot be\n  redeemed by someone else."},{"q":"A customer renamed their \"AI-Users\" group and everyone lost access. What happened and how do you prevent it?","a":"- **Cause:** permissions were mapped by group display name, which is mutable.\n- **Fix:** map group or app-role object IDs, ideally admin-assigned app roles so intent is explicit.\n- **Guard:** alert when a mapped ID stops appearing in any token for a tenant."},{"q":"When is just-in-time provisioning enough, and when do you insist on SCIM?","a":"- **JIT is enough** for a pilot or a small team where leavers are rare and sessions are short.\n- **Insist on SCIM** when the customer's security team audits leaver access, because JIT never learns that\n  someone left and their session lives on."},{"q":"How do you put a number on deprovisioning exposure for a security questionnaire?","a":"- **Formula:** SCIM delivery time plus whatever the app does not revoke on receipt.\n- **With revocation on SCIM events**, exposure is roughly the delivery time; session length is only a\n  backstop.\n- **Prove it** with a canary account deactivated on a schedule and a measured time to loss of access."},{"q":"An agent must create Jira issues as the signed-in user. How do you authenticate the downstream call?","a":"- **Use delegation:** Entra's on-behalf-of or OAuth token exchange, so the downstream token carries the\n  user's identity and scopes.\n- **Not a shared service account**, because every action would then belong to one bot in the audit log and\n  the agent could exceed the user's permissions."},{"q":"A nightly agent job keeps running for a user who left two weeks ago. Where is the gap?","a":"- **The job holds its own credential**, usually a stored refresh token, so ending the browser session did\n  nothing to it.\n- **Fix the deactivation handler:** on SCIM deactivation, cancel the user's scheduled agent runs and revoke\n  stored tokens, not just web sessions.\n- **Defence in depth:** before each run and each consequential step, check the user is still active and the\n  delegated token exchange still succeeds."}],"tradeoffs":["**OIDC vs SAML.** OIDC is simpler to implement and debug, but some IdP policies or legacy setups mandate SAML. Supporting both doubles the test matrix, so add SAML when the first deal requires it.","**JIT provisioning vs SCIM.** Just-in-time provisioning on first login needs no extra integration but never learns about leavers. SCIM adds that at the cost of another endpoint and per-IdP quirks.","**Groups in tokens vs app roles.** Groups avoid extra calls but large memberships bloat tokens or trigger overage. App roles assigned by the customer admin keep tokens small and intent explicit.","**Short vs long sessions.** Short sessions make deprovisioning effective quickly but force more IdP round-trips and interrupt long agent runs. Long sessions are smoother and acceptable only when SCIM revocation kills sessions immediately.","**Delegated vs service credentials for agent tools.** Delegation keeps actions inside the user's permissions and auditable per person, at the cost of consent setup and exchange failures to handle. A service account is simpler and suits only system-level jobs with no user behind them."],"pitfalls":["**Accepting any Entra tenant's token**, often by disabling issuer validation to get past the `{tenantid}` placeholder. Symptom: a user from an unrelated company reaches a customer workspace in a penetration test.","**Email as the primary key.** Symptom: a rehired or renamed employee gets a second account, or a new hire inherits a predecessor's history and agent memory.","**Permissions mapped by group display name.** Symptom: access silently disappears after the customer renames a group.","**Ignoring groups overage.** Symptom: power users with many memberships have no permissions while everyone else works.","**Agents calling downstream systems with a shared service credential.** Symptom: audit logs where every action belongs to one bot account. Fix: on-behalf-of or token exchange per tool call.","**Putting tokens in the prompt or tool arguments.** Symptom: tokens show up in traces and model logs. Fix: tools read credentials from the runtime session, never from the model."],"when":"- **Use when** selling a B2B product or agent to companies with more than a handful of users; plan it before\n  the first enterprise deal, not after.\n- **Start with** OIDC plus per-tenant issuer validation; add SAML and SCIM as customers require.\n- **Route authorization** through a mapping layer rather than raw claims.\n- **Delegate** whenever an agent tool touches a system where the user has their own permissions.\n- **Measure:** login success rate per tenant, time from IdP deactivation to loss of access, and\n  token-validation failures by reason.\n- **Interviews:** know that SSO is authentication only, and explain tenant-bound issuer checks, stable keys\n  and deprovisioning.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements discovery, JWKS caching, issuer and audience checks per tenant, stable user keys, and the groups-overage fallback to Microsoft Graph.","architect":"Owns the identity model: which protocols are supported, where tenant-to-IdP mapping lives, session lifetime policy and how agent delegation preserves the user's identity downstream.","fde":"Walks the customer's IAM admin through app registration, consent, claim configuration and test users, then verifies a deactivated test account loses access within the agreed time."},"check":{"q":"Your multi-tenant app accepts Entra ID tokens. A pen tester signs in to a customer's workspace using an\naccount from their own personal Entra tenant. What went wrong and how do you fix it?","a":"The app verified the signature against Microsoft's common keys and the audience, but did not bind the\ntoken to the customer's tenant. Every Entra tenant's tokens are signed by the same key set, so a valid\nsignature proves nothing about which organization issued it. The fix is to resolve the tenant from the login\nroute (workspace or email domain), then require `tid` and `iss` to match the tenant ID stored at onboarding,\nand to key users by `tid` plus `oid`. Add a regression test with a token from an unregistered tenant."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should users be keyed by issuer plus subject (or tid plus oid) rather than email?","options":["Email addresses are too long to index","Subject and object IDs are immutable for the account, while emails change and can be reassigned","OIDC tokens never contain an email claim","Email keys break the token signature"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Immutable identifiers keep one account per person across renames and prevent a recycled mailbox from inheriting someone else's data; email is a mutable display attribute, though usually present in tokens."},{"q":"What does SSO by itself fail to handle when an employee leaves the customer?","options":["MFA enforcement on their next login","Revoking their access at the IdP","Ending sessions and access already established in your application","Signing the ID tokens they were issued"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Disabling the user at the IdP blocks new sign-ins, but your existing session lives on until it expires; SCIM deactivation plus session revocation or short sessions closes that gap."},{"q":"An Entra user in many groups has an empty groups claim. What is the likely cause?","options":["The app registration is single-tenant","The token signature failed verification","Group overage: Entra omitted the groups and expects the app to query Microsoft Graph for membership","SCIM has not run yet"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When membership exceeds the token's limit, Entra sends an overage indicator instead of the list; apps must fall back to Graph, or better, use app roles assigned by the admin to keep tokens small."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/saml-sso","sources":["OASIS, 2005 — Security Assertion Markup Language (SAML) V2.0 Core and Bindings","Somorovsky et al., 2012 — On Breaking SAML: Be Whoever You Want to Be","Official docs: OWASP SAML Security Cheat Sheet"],"why":"- **Every app kept its own passwords.** Cross-domain single sign-on meant cookies that browsers would not\n  share between companies.\n- **Enterprises wanted their directory to vouch for users.** Active Directory, later Okta or Entra ID,\n  needed to assert identity to an outside app without that app ever seeing a password.\n- **SAML solved it through the browser.** Standardized in its 2.0 form in 2005, it passes signed XML\n  statements via the browser, so the IdP and the service provider (SP) never need a direct network\n  connection, which suited firewalled corporate networks.\n- **The naive alternatives fail.** Sharing the customer's LDAP exposes credentials and violates every\n  security policy. A username in a URL parameter is trivially forged.\n- **Even \"signed XML\" is not enough.** Early implementations verified that *a* signature was valid, then\n  read the user from a *different*, unsigned element, letting attackers impersonate anyone. SAML SSO is a\n  protocol plus a checklist of validations that must be exactly right.","how":"### Background\n- **SAML is interactive web login only.** It produces a browser session for your app. Your agent's calls to\n  downstream APIs still need OAuth access tokens, obtained by delegation from the user's session.\n- **What the agent builder keeps from it:** a stable user key (persistent NameID scoped to the tenant's\n  IdP), role or group attributes for the authorization layer, and the authentication time for step-up\n  decisions before risky agent actions.\n- **The rest below is the mechanism** your SAML library implements and you must be able to verify.\n\n### Setup: metadata exchange\nExchanged once per customer:\n\n| Party | Publishes |\n|---|---|\n| SP (you) | Entity ID (a unique URI) and Assertion Consumer Service (ACS) URL |\n| IdP | Entity ID, SSO URL and signing certificate |\n\n- **Store the IdP's certificate per tenant**, *because* it is the only thing that makes an assertion trustworthy.\n\n### SP-initiated flow\n1. **Discover the tenant.** The user opens your app; home realm discovery finds their tenant.\n2. **Send an `AuthnRequest`.** Redirect the browser to the IdP's SSO URL (HTTP-Redirect binding: deflated,\n   base64, in the query string) with a fresh request ID and your entity ID. Save the ID.\n3. **The IdP authenticates** the user (password, MFA, conditional access).\n4. **The IdP posts back.** It returns an HTML form that auto-POSTs a base64 `SAMLResponse` to your ACS URL\n   (HTTP-POST binding, *because* assertions are too large for a URL).\n5. **You validate and create a session**, which the agent runtime then reads the user from.\n\n### What the assertion contains\n| Element | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| `Issuer` | The IdP entity ID |\n| `Subject/NameID` | The user identifier, in a format such as persistent, email or unspecified |\n| `SubjectConfirmationData` | `Recipient` (your ACS URL), `NotOnOrAfter`, `InResponseTo` (your request ID) |\n| `Conditions` | `NotBefore`, `NotOnOrAfter`, `AudienceRestriction` (your entity ID) |\n| `AttributeStatement` | Email, name, groups, roles |\n| Signature | An XML digital signature over the assertion, the response, or both |\n\n### The validation checklist\nEach check exists *because* skipping it enables a known attack:\n\n| Check | Attack it stops |\n|---|---|\n| Verify the signature with the tenant's stored certificate, require signing, and read every value **from the element the signature covers** | Signature wrapping: a forged unsigned assertion inserted next to a genuine signed one |\n| `Issuer` matches the tenant's IdP | One customer's IdP asserting into another's workspace |\n| `Audience` equals your entity ID | An assertion minted for another SP replayed at you |\n| `Recipient` equals your ACS URL; time window holds with small clock skew | Misdirected or stale assertions |\n| `InResponseTo` matches a request ID that *this browser session* started; assertion IDs recorded until expiry | Replay, and login CSRF |\n\n- **Why bind to the session, not a global set of pending IDs:** otherwise an attacker can start a login,\n  feed their own valid response into a victim's browser, and sign the victim in as the attacker. For an\n  agent product that is worse than usual: the victim's agent would then write memories and files into the\n  attacker's account.\n- **IdP-initiated SSO** (no `AuthnRequest`, so no `InResponseTo`) loses request correlation; many teams\n  disable it or accept it only with strict replay caching.\n\n### Parsing, encryption, RelayState and logout\n- **Parse defensively.** The response is attacker-controlled bytes until the signature checks out, and it is\n  parsed *before* that check. Disable DTDs and external entity resolution, *because* otherwise an XXE payload\n  can make your server read local files or call internal URLs during parsing.\n- **Read text nodes carefully.** In 2018 several SAML libraries were found to read only the first text node\n  of an element:\n  - an attacker who legitimately held `ana@acme.com.evil.com` at the IdP inserted a comment into their own\n    signed assertion, `<NameID>ana@acme.com<!---->.evil.com</NameID>`;\n  - the signature still verified (canonicalization drops comments) but the SP read the user as `ana@acme.com`;\n  - use a library version that has fixed this, and read the full canonicalized text.\n- **Encrypted assertions.** The IdP can encrypt to the SP's public key so the browser cannot read the\n  attributes. Decrypt, then verify the signature on the decrypted assertion; encryption hides data but does\n  not authenticate it, *because* anyone with the SP's public key can encrypt, so it never replaces the\n  signature checks.\n- **RelayState.** The opaque value the SP sends with the request and gets back, usually the page to return\n  to. Treat it as untrusted and accept only relative paths or an allow-list, *because* redirecting to\n  whatever it contains turns your login into an open redirect.\n- **Logout.** SAML Single Logout depends on every party's front-channel redirects succeeding and is\n  unreliable in practice. Do not rely on it for revocation, least of all for background agent tasks; use\n  short sessions and SCIM deactivation.\n\n### Certificates\n- **Support two active certificates per tenant during rotation**, *because* IdP signing certificates expire\n  and rotate, and a single hard-coded certificate turns a routine rotation into an outage for that customer.\n\n### From assertion to agent principal\n1. **User key** = (tenant, IdP entity ID, persistent NameID), never the email attribute.\n2. **Roles** = attribute values mapped through the tenant's reviewed mapping table to agent capabilities.\n3. **Auth time** = the assertion's authentication instant, so the agent runtime can demand a fresh login\n   before a consequential action.\n4. **Downstream tokens** come from a separate OAuth delegation step, because the SAML assertion is not an\n   API credential.","example":"### Validating a decoded response with `signxml` and `lxml`\nThe key design point: all values are read from `signed`, the element the verifier returns, never from the\nraw document.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime, timedelta, timezone\nfrom lxml import etree\nfrom signxml import XMLVerifier\n\nNS = {\"saml\": \"urn:oasis:names:tc:SAML:2.0:assertion\"}\nSKEW = timedelta(minutes=2)\n\ndef ts(s: str) -> datetime:\n    return datetime.fromisoformat(s.replace(\"Z\", \"+00:00\"))\n\ndef consume(xml: bytes, tenant: dict, pending_ids: set, seen_ids: set) -> dict:\n    # pending_ids: request IDs started by THIS browser session; seen_ids: shared, kept until expiry\n    signed = XMLVerifier().verify(xml, x509_cert=tenant[\"idp_cert_pem\"]).signed_xml\n    a = signed if etree.QName(signed).localname == \"Assertion\" else signed.find(\"saml:Assertion\", NS)\n    if a is None:\n        raise PermissionError(\"no signed assertion\")\n    now = datetime.now(timezone.utc)\n    if a.findtext(\"saml:Issuer\", namespaces=NS) != tenant[\"idp_entity_id\"]:\n        raise PermissionError(\"wrong issuer\")\n    cond = a.find(\"saml:Conditions\", NS)\n    nb = cond.get(\"NotBefore\")                                # optional; NotOnOrAfter missing fails closed\n    if (nb and now < ts(nb) - SKEW) or now >= ts(cond.get(\"NotOnOrAfter\")) + SKEW:\n        raise PermissionError(\"outside validity window\")\n    if tenant[\"sp_entity_id\"] not in [x.text for x in cond.iterfind(\".//saml:Audience\", NS)]:\n        raise PermissionError(\"wrong audience\")\n    scd = a.find(\"saml:Subject/saml:SubjectConfirmation/saml:SubjectConfirmationData\", NS)\n    if scd.get(\"Recipient\") != tenant[\"acs_url\"] or scd.get(\"InResponseTo\") not in pending_ids:\n        raise PermissionError(\"wrong recipient or unsolicited response\")\n    if now >= ts(scd.get(\"NotOnOrAfter\")) + SKEW:             # bearer confirmation has its own expiry\n        raise PermissionError(\"subject confirmation expired\")\n    if a.get(\"ID\") in seen_ids:\n        raise PermissionError(\"replayed assertion\")\n    seen_ids.add(a.get(\"ID\")); pending_ids.discard(scd.get(\"InResponseTo\"))\n    attrs = {at.get(\"Name\"): [v.text for v in at.iterfind(\"saml:AttributeValue\", NS)]\n             for at in a.iterfind(\".//saml:Attribute\", NS)}\n    return {\"name_id\": a.findtext(\"saml:Subject/saml:NameID\", namespaces=NS), \"attrs\": attrs}\n```\n\n- **In production, prefer a full SAML library per framework.** It also handles encrypted assertions and\n  metadata, but this checklist is what you should be able to point to in its configuration.\n\n### Turning the validated assertion into an agent principal\nRunnable standard-library code: the agent runtime gets a principal with a stable key and an explicit tool\nallow-list; unknown role values grant nothing.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\nROLE_MAP = {  # per tenant, reviewed: IdP attribute value -> agent capabilities\n    \"acme\": {\"grp-support-agents\": {\"search_kb\", \"read_ticket\", \"draft_reply\"},\n             \"grp-support-leads\":  {\"search_kb\", \"read_ticket\", \"draft_reply\", \"refund_under_100\"}},\n}\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass AgentPrincipal:\n    user_key: tuple          # (tenant, idp_entity_id, persistent NameID)\n    tools: frozenset\n\ndef principal_from_assertion(tenant: str, idp_entity_id: str, validated: dict) -> AgentPrincipal:\n    groups = validated[\"attrs\"].get(\"groups\", [])\n    tools = set().union(*(ROLE_MAP[tenant].get(g, set()) for g in groups)) if groups else set()\n    return AgentPrincipal((tenant, idp_entity_id, validated[\"name_id\"]), frozenset(tools))\n\np = principal_from_assertion(\"acme\", \"http://www.okta.com/exk1\",\n                             {\"name_id\": \"00u8a1b2\", \"attrs\": {\"groups\": [\"grp-support-agents\", \"everyone\"]}})\nprint(p.tools)               # search_kb, read_ticket, draft_reply: no refund tool, 'everyone' grants nothing\n```\n\n### RelayState allow-list and an expiring replay cache\nPure standard library, runnable on Python 3.10+. Both are small, and both are commonly missing.\n\n```python\nfrom urllib.parse import urlsplit\n\ndef safe_relay_state(rs: str | None, default: str = \"/\") -> str:\n    \"\"\"Accept only same-site relative paths; anything else falls back to the home page.\"\"\"\n    if not rs or \"\\\\\" in rs or rs.startswith(\"//\") or not rs.startswith(\"/\"):\n        return default\n    parts = urlsplit(rs)\n    return default if parts.scheme or parts.netloc else rs\n\nclass ReplayCache:\n    \"\"\"Remember assertion IDs until their NotOnOrAfter; reject a second use.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.seen: dict[str, float] = {}\n    def first_use(self, assertion_id: str, not_on_or_after: float, now: float) -> bool:\n        self.seen = {k: exp for k, exp in self.seen.items() if exp > now}   # expire old IDs\n        if assertion_id in self.seen:\n            return False\n        self.seen[assertion_id] = not_on_or_after\n        return True\n\nfor rs in [\"/reports/42?tab=q3\", \"https://evil.example\", \"//evil.example\", \"/\\\\evil.example\", None]:\n    print(repr(rs), \"->\", safe_relay_state(rs))          # only the first survives\n\ncache = ReplayCache()\nprint(cache.first_use(\"_a1\", not_on_or_after=1300, now=1000))   # True: first use\nprint(cache.first_use(\"_a1\", not_on_or_after=1300, now=1200))   # False: replay inside window\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: the negative test matrix\nEvery row is a tampered response your ACS endpoint must reject; run them in CI per library upgrade. The\nlast column is what a miss would mean for the agent.\n\n| Tampered response | Expected rejection | Agent impact if accepted |\n|---|---|---|\n| Genuine signed assertion plus a forged unsigned one | Only the signed element is read; exactly one assertion allowed | Agent runs as any user |\n| Valid assertion for another SP | Wrong audience | Agent runs for a user of another app |\n| Valid assertion from another tenant's IdP | Wrong issuer or signature (different stored certificate) | Cross-tenant agent memory and tools |\n| `NotOnOrAfter` 10 minutes in the past, skew 2 minutes | Outside validity window | Stale session revived |\n| `InResponseTo` started in a different browser session | Unsolicited response | Victim's agent writes into attacker's account |\n| Same assertion posted twice | Replayed assertion | Second agent session from a captured response |\n| Comment injected into NameID | Library reads full canonicalized text, user unchanged | Agent bound to another user's key |\n| DOCTYPE with an external entity | Parser refuses DTDs | Server-side file read |","patterns":["**Two-certificate rotation with expiry alerts.** Store up to two IdP certificates per tenant, accept either, and alert well ahead (for example 30 days) of each expiry date captured from metadata. Monitor signature failures per tenant on rotation days.","**Shared replay cache with TTL.** Keep seen assertion IDs in a store every app node shares, for example Redis `SET saml:<ID> 1 NX EX <seconds until NotOnOrAfter>`; a failed `NX` means replay. Monitor replay rejections, which should be near zero.","**Clock discipline on every node.** Run NTP everywhere and keep skew allowance small (2 minutes in the example). Monitor \"not yet valid\" errors by node; one node's spike means its clock drifted.","**Hardened parser configuration.** Parse with DTDs and external entities disabled (for `lxml`, a parser created with entity resolution and network access turned off), before any signature work. Monitor parse failures as a security signal.","**One principal object for the agent runtime.** Convert the validated assertion once into a principal (stable key, tenant, mapped tools, auth time) and pass only that to the agent loop and tools. Monitor sessions with an empty tool set, which usually means a renamed attribute.","**Login failures by reason, per tenant.** Emit a reason code (audience, signature, expiry, recipient, unsolicited, replay) for each rejection so FDEs can tell a customer metadata mistake from an attack."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** The SAML login is where your agent learns who it is working for. The\n  NameID and attributes become the user key and roles that decide which tools the agent may call and which\n  documents retrieval may return, so a forged assertion is a forged agent principal.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The ACS endpoint of the web app that hosts your agent, the SAML\n  library's per-tenant configuration, and the session your agent runtime reads identity from.\n- **What you control.** Which NameID format you key users on, how attributes map to agent roles, whether\n  IdP-initiated login is allowed, and the rule that API and tool calls use OAuth tokens, not SAML.\n- **What you can skip.** XML canonicalization details and hand-rolled signature code. Know the flow, the\n  validation checklist and the famous attacks well enough to configure and test a library.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Enterprise deals that mandate SAML.** The customer's Okta or Entra app signs employees into your agent\n  workspace with their corporate MFA.\n- **Role-gated agent tools.** SAML group attributes map to which tools a support or finance agent may call.\n- **Security reviews of agent products.** The negative test matrix is the evidence a customer's security\n  team asks for before go-live.\n- **Multi-tenant agent hosting.** Per-tenant certificates, issuers and audiences keep one customer's\n  assertions out of another's workspace.","mini_project":"**SAML login that yields an agent principal (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** sign test users into a tiny agent app with SAML and give each a tool allow-list from their groups.\n- **Steps:** create a SAML app in a free developer IdP tenant (or use a local test IdP); configure a\n  maintained SAML library; convert the validated assertion into a principal (stable key, tools); run one\n  agent prompt as two users in different groups; add CI tests for wrong audience, replay and an expired\n  assertion.\n- **Done when:** the two users see different tools offered to the model, all three negative tests reject,\n  and logs show a reason code for each rejection."},"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant U as Browser\n    participant S as Agent app (SP)\n    participant I as Customer IdP\n    U->>S: Open app\n    Note over S: Home realm discovery, save request ID in session\n    S->>U: Redirect with AuthnRequest\n    U->>I: AuthnRequest via HTTP-Redirect\n    Note over I: Password, MFA, conditional access\n    I->>U: Auto-POST form with SAMLResponse\n    U->>S: POST SAMLResponse to ACS URL\n    Note over S: Verify signature, read only signed element\n    Note over S: Issuer, Audience, Recipient, time, InResponseTo, replay\n    S->>U: Session cookie, agent principal bound","caption":"SP-initiated login: the request ID you save in step 2 is what `InResponseTo` must match at the end, and\nonly the validated assertion becomes the agent session."},"interview":[{"q":"Walk me through an SP-initiated SAML login.","a":"- **Discovery and request:** home realm discovery picks the tenant, then the SP redirects with an\n  `AuthnRequest` carrying a fresh ID it saves in the browser session.\n- **IdP and response:** the IdP authenticates (MFA, conditional access) and auto-POSTs a signed\n  `SAMLResponse` to the ACS URL.\n- **Validation:** signature from the signed element only, then issuer, audience, recipient, time window,\n  `InResponseTo` and replay, then a local session."},{"q":"One customer's SAML logins all started failing this morning. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Read the rejection reason** for that tenant: a signature failure on one tenant usually means the IdP\n  rotated its signing certificate.\n- **Confirm with a SAML tracer** on a test account and compare the certificate in the response with the one\n  stored.\n- **Fix and prevent:** add the new certificate as a second active one, and alert on expiry dates from metadata."},{"q":"Would you enable IdP-initiated SSO? Why or why not?","a":"- **Benefit:** users get a tile in their IdP portal, which some customers insist on.\n- **Cost:** no `AuthnRequest` means no `InResponseTo`, so replay and login CSRF are easier.\n- **If enabled:** strict shared replay cache, short validity, and a fixed landing page instead of an\n  arbitrary RelayState."},{"q":"How do you make replay protection work behind a load balancer with many app nodes?","a":"- **Use a shared store**, because a per-node in-memory set lets a replay succeed on a different node.\n- **Key by assertion ID with a TTL** until its `NotOnOrAfter`, using an atomic set-if-absent so two nodes\n  cannot both accept it."},{"q":"Users sign in to your agent product with SAML. How does the agent get credentials to call Salesforce as them?","a":"- **Not from the assertion:** it is a one-time login statement for your SP, not a credential Salesforce accepts.\n- **Run an OAuth flow** for the downstream system (user consent or an IdP-supported delegation or token\n  exchange), store the resulting tokens server-side per user key, and inject them into tool calls.\n- **Keep identity consistent:** the stable key from the SAML NameID links the agent session, the stored\n  tokens and the audit log."},{"q":"After an IdP change, every user at one tenant can sign in but their agent has no tools. What happened?","a":"- **Likely a renamed or removed attribute:** the groups attribute name or values changed, so the role\n  mapping matched nothing and the principal's tool set is empty.\n- **Diagnose** with a SAML tracer and the per-tenant count of empty-tool sessions.\n- **Fix** by updating the reviewed mapping, and fail visibly (an admin alert) rather than letting agents run\n  with silently reduced capabilities."}],"tradeoffs":["**Ubiquity vs implementation risk.** SAML works with nearly every enterprise IdP and corporate policy, but XML signatures, canonicalization and certificate handling make it harder to implement safely than OIDC. Prefer OIDC when the customer allows it.","**Browser delivery vs scope.** It needs no network path from the IdP to you, which suits locked-down networks, but it is only for interactive web login; API and agent calls still need OAuth tokens.","**IdP-initiated convenience vs risk.** A tile in the user's portal is convenient, but it removes `InResponseTo` correlation and widens replay and login-CSRF risk. Enable it only with strict replay caching and short validity.","**Persistent vs email NameIDs.** Persistent opaque NameIDs survive email changes; email NameIDs are easy to map but change, and agent memory keyed on them follows the mailbox. The choice flips if the customer cannot emit a persistent identifier."],"pitfalls":["**Reading the user from the raw document** after checking that some signature verifies. Symptom: a pen tester logs in as an arbitrary user with a modified response.","**No per-tenant audience or issuer check.** Symptom: an assertion from one customer's IdP is accepted for another customer's workspace.","**One hard-coded IdP certificate.** Symptom: a single customer's logins fail on the day their IdP rotates its signing certificate.","**Skipping replay protection.** Symptom: a captured `SAMLResponse` from browser history grants a new session hours later within a lax validity window.","**DTDs and external entities enabled.** Symptom: a pen-test finding that the ACS endpoint fetched an attacker-supplied URL or echoed local file contents in an error.","**Following `RelayState` to any URL.** Symptom: phishing links pass through your genuine login page and land on an attacker's site.","**Server clock drift.** Symptom: intermittent \"assertion not yet valid\" errors on only some application nodes.","**Using the SAML session as the agent's API credential.** Symptom: a tool forwards the session cookie or assertion to a downstream API and fails, or a service token is quietly substituted. Fix: OAuth delegation."],"when":"- **Use when** customers require it, which in practice means most larger enterprise deals; offer OIDC\n  alongside for those who can use it.\n- **Never hand-roll** XML signature verification; use a maintained library and verify its configuration\n  against the checklist above.\n- **Agent scope:** SAML signs the user in; tools get OAuth tokens through delegation.\n- **Measure:** login failures by reason (audience, signature, expiry) per tenant, and alert ahead of IdP\n  certificate expiry dates captured from metadata.\n- **Interviews:** walk the SP-initiated flow and explain signature wrapping and audience checks; that is the\n  expected depth for anyone shipping B2B software.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures the SAML library per tenant, stores certificates and entity IDs, enforces replay caches and writes tests with tampered, expired and wrong-audience responses.","architect":"Decides whether IdP-initiated SSO is allowed, how SAML coexists with OIDC, and how certificate rotation is handled without downtime across all tenants.","fde":"Exchanges metadata with the customer's IAM team, maps their attribute names to required fields, and debugs failed logins using a SAML tracer on a test account."},"check":{"q":"Your app verifies the SAML response signature and then extracts NameID with an XPath query over the\nwhole document. Why is this vulnerable, and what is the fix?","a":"XML signatures cover specific elements referenced by ID, not the whole document. An attacker can take a\ngenuine signed response, insert a second forged assertion elsewhere in the tree, and leave the signed one\nintact so verification still passes. A document-wide XPath may then read the forged NameID. The fix is to\nread every value only from the element the verifier reports as signed, require that exactly one assertion\nexists and that it is the signed one, and use a maintained library with signature wrapping defenses.\nThen still check issuer, audience, recipient, time window and InResponseTo."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the SAML HTTP-POST binding deliver the response through the user's browser?","options":["Browsers can decrypt assertions that servers cannot","The IdP and SP need no direct network connection, and assertions are too large for a redirect URL","It avoids the need to sign the assertion","POST requests cannot be replayed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Browser-mediated delivery works across firewalls because both parties talk only to the browser, and POST carries a large signed XML payload; it is still replayable, which is why replay caches exist."},{"q":"What does the AudienceRestriction check protect against?","options":["An expired IdP signing certificate","A user without MFA","An assertion issued for a different service provider being replayed at yours","Clock skew between servers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The audience names the SP the assertion was minted for; without checking it, a valid signed assertion for another application trusted by the same IdP could be accepted by yours."},{"q":"What is the main security cost of allowing IdP-initiated SSO?","options":["Users must re-enter passwords","Assertions can no longer be signed","There is no AuthnRequest, so InResponseTo cannot tie the response to a request you made","It disables attribute statements"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Unsolicited responses lose request correlation, which helps replay and login-CSRF attacks; mitigate with strict replay caching and short validity windows, or disable the flow."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/scim-provisioning","sources":["Hunt et al., 2015 — RFC 7643: SCIM Core Schema","Hunt et al., 2015 — RFC 7644: SCIM Protocol","Official docs: Okta and Microsoft Entra SCIM provisioning guides"],"why":"- **JIT provisioning never learns who left.** Creating an account at first SSO sign-in looks sufficient,\n  but a leaver never signs in again. Their account, API tokens, scheduled agent jobs and shared documents\n  remain.\n- **Moves lag too.** A user who moves from Finance to Sales keeps Finance group membership in your app until\n  their next login, and if sessions are long-lived, longer.\n- **You cannot address people who have not logged in.** You cannot assign work or share a document with\n  them, because they do not exist in your system yet.\n- **AI products feel it sharper.** Retrieval filters, agent permissions and audit trails are keyed by users\n  and groups; if membership lags the directory, the assistant keeps answering from documents a person\n  should no longer see.\n- **Naive fixes are bespoke and still lag.** A nightly CSV export or polling the customer's directory API is\n  per customer and per IdP and needs credentials into their directory. SCIM inverts the direction: the IdP\n  pushes changes to a standard endpoint you build once.","how":"### Resources and endpoints\nRFC 7643 defines the schema, RFC 7644 the protocol. Under a per-tenant base URL you expose:\n- **`/Users` and `/Groups`:** the core resources.\n- **`/ServiceProviderConfig`, `/Schemas`, `/ResourceTypes`:** discovery, telling the IdP which features you\n  support (PATCH, filtering, bulk, ETags).\n\n| Resource | Key attributes |\n|---|---|\n| User | `id` (yours, server-assigned), `externalId` (the IdP's identifier), `userName`, `name`, `emails`, `active`, optional enterprise-extension attributes such as `department` and `manager` |\n| Group | `displayName` and `members` |\n\n- **Agent-relevant attributes.** `department` and `manager` are useful ABAC inputs for agent tool policies\n  (an approval agent routes to the manager), so map them when the IdP sends them.\n\n### The operations\n| Request | Behaviour and why |\n|---|---|\n| `POST /Users` | Return 201 with your `id`; return 409 (`scimType: uniqueness`) if `userName` exists, *because* a retried create must not produce a second account. IdPs normally query by filter first and link to a match |\n| `GET /Users?filter=userName eq \"ana@acme.com\"` | Lookup before create or match; support at least the `eq` filter on `userName` and `externalId` |\n| `PATCH /Users/{id}` | A `PatchOp` body of `add`/`replace`/`remove` operations. Deprovisioning commonly arrives as `replace active=false`, *because* customers want the account recoverable and its history retained |\n| `DELETE /Users/{id}` | Hard removal; some IdPs send it, often later than deactivation, if at all |\n| `PATCH /Groups/{id}` | Adds or removes `members`; this is how group-based permissions stay current |\n\nGroup membership shapes:\n- **Add:** `{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"members\",\"value\":[{\"value\":\"<user id>\"}]}`\n- **Remove:** `{\"op\":\"remove\",\"path\":\"members[value eq \\\"<user id>\\\"]\"}`\n- **Prefer these incremental operations to a `replace` of the whole list**, *because* a 5,000-member group\n  replaced on every change is a 5,000-element write each time, and a replace racing another change can drop\n  members.\n\nPaging:\n- **List and filter responses are a `ListResponse`** with `totalResults`, `startIndex` (1-based) and\n  `itemsPerPage`.\n- **Pagination must work before the first big customer turns provisioning on**, because IdPs page through\n  `/Users` and `/Groups` during an initial sync.\n\n### Authentication and tenancy\n- **Per-tenant credential.** The IdP authenticates with a bearer token (or OAuth client credentials) that\n  you issue per tenant.\n- **The token, not the body, determines the tenant**, *because* an endpoint that trusts a tenant field in\n  the payload lets one customer's IdP write into another's directory.\n\n### Joining SCIM identities to SSO identities\nSCIM and SSO are two channels describing the same person, and they must land on one account:\n1. **Agree the join key with the customer's admin** when configuring provisioning.\n2. **Ideally use the same immutable identifier the SSO token carries** (for example the Entra object ID or\n   the Okta user ID), sent as `externalId` or a mapped attribute, so a SCIM-created user and the first SSO\n   login resolve to the same row.\n3. **Avoid email or `userName` joins.** They work until an address changes; then SCIM updates one account\n   while login creates another, *because* the channels no longer agree on who the person is. Agent memory\n   and history then split across two rows.\n4. **If JIT stays enabled alongside SCIM**, it must look up by the same key before creating.\n\n### What deactivation must do\n`active=false` is a security event, not a profile edit. Immediately:\n- revoke sessions and API tokens;\n- pause the user's scheduled jobs and agents, and cancel in-flight agent runs at their next step;\n- revoke stored OAuth refresh tokens the agent uses for downstream tools;\n- invalidate any permission cache that includes them.\n\nDeleting their data (including agent memory) is a separate retention decision.\n\n### Robustness\n- **Idempotent operations**, because IdPs retry and may resend the same change.\n- **Lenient parsing**, because implementations differ: some send `op` values in different letter case,\n  booleans as strings, or a `replace` with no `path` and an object value.\n- **Log the raw request** and test against each IdP you support.","example":"### Deactivation-aware `PATCH /scim/v2/Users/{id}` in FastAPI\nHandles the common deactivation shapes and does the security-relevant work, not just a column update.\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, Header, HTTPException\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\ndef as_bool(v):  # some IdPs send \"False\" as a string\n    return v if isinstance(v, bool) else str(v).lower() == \"true\"\n\n@app.patch(\"/scim/v2/Users/{user_id}\")\nasync def patch_user(user_id: str, body: dict, authorization: str = Header(...)):\n    tenant = await tenant_for_scim_token(authorization.removeprefix(\"Bearer \"))  # tenant from token\n    user = await db.get_user(tenant.id, user_id)\n    if user is None:\n        raise HTTPException(404, detail=\"User not found\")\n    if \"urn:ietf:params:scim:api:messages:2.0:PatchOp\" not in body.get(\"schemas\", []):\n        raise HTTPException(400, detail=\"Expected PatchOp\")\n    changes = {}\n    for op in body.get(\"Operations\", []):\n        kind, path, value = op[\"op\"].lower(), op.get(\"path\"), op.get(\"value\")\n        if kind == \"replace\" and path is None and isinstance(value, dict):\n            changes.update(value)                      # {\"op\":\"replace\",\"value\":{\"active\":false}}\n        elif kind in (\"replace\", \"add\") and path:\n            changes[path] = value                      # {\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"active\",\"value\":false}\n    if \"active\" in changes:\n        changes[\"active\"] = as_bool(changes[\"active\"])\n        if user.active and not changes[\"active\"]:      # idempotent: only act on the transition\n            await sessions.revoke_all(tenant.id, user_id)\n            await api_tokens.revoke_all(tenant.id, user_id)\n            await agent_jobs.pause_for_user(tenant.id, user_id)\n            await acl_cache.invalidate_principal(tenant.id, user_id)\n    user = await db.update_user(tenant.id, user_id, changes)\n    await audit.log(tenant.id, \"scim.patch_user\", user_id, raw=body)\n    return to_scim_user(user)                          # full resource, 200\n```\n\n- **Still to handle in a real implementation:** filtered paths such as `emails[type eq \"work\"].value`,\n  `remove` operations (ignored above) and group membership patches. The point here is that deactivation is\n  a security event with side effects.\n- **Add the agent's stored tool tokens to the transition block.** The handler revokes sessions and API\n  tokens and pauses jobs; the deactivation list above also requires revoking the OAuth refresh tokens the\n  agent holds for downstream tools, or a paused job's credentials stay live.\n\n### The agent loop's side of deactivation\nRunnable standard library: the agent runtime re-checks the principal before every tool step, so a run that\nstarted before the SCIM event stops at its next step instead of finishing.\n\n```python\nACTIVE = {(\"acme\", \"u1\"): True}            # updated by the SCIM handler\n\nclass UserDeactivated(Exception):\n    pass\n\ndef run_agent(tenant: str, user_id: str, planned_steps: list[str]) -> list[str]:\n    done = []\n    for i, step in enumerate(planned_steps):\n        if not ACTIVE.get((tenant, user_id), False):         # checked before EVERY tool call\n            raise UserDeactivated(f\"stopped before step {i}: {step}\")\n        done.append(f\"ran {step}\")\n        if i == 1:\n            ACTIVE[(\"acme\", \"u1\")] = False                   # SCIM active=false arrives mid-run\n    return done\n\ntry:\n    run_agent(\"acme\", \"u1\", [\"search_kb\", \"read_ticket\", \"send_email\", \"close_ticket\"])\nexcept UserDeactivated as e:\n    print(e)                                                 # stopped before step 2: send_email\n```\n\n- **Why per step:** a long research or batch agent can run for minutes or hours; checking only at start\n  leaves the window open for the whole run.\n\n### Idempotent group-membership patches with an ACL delta\nPure Python, runnable. It returns who was added and removed, so only those principals' permission caches\nare invalidated, and a replayed patch changes nothing.\n\n```python\nimport re\n\nREMOVE_FILTER = re.compile(r'^members\\[value eq \"([^\"]+)\"\\]$')\n\ndef apply_group_patch(members: set, body: dict) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"Apply a SCIM Group PatchOp; return (new_members, added, removed) for ACL invalidation.\"\"\"\n    out = set(members)\n    for op in body.get(\"Operations\", []):\n        kind, path, value = op[\"op\"].lower(), op.get(\"path\"), op.get(\"value\") or []\n        if kind == \"add\" and path == \"members\":\n            out |= {m[\"value\"] for m in value}               # re-adding is a no-op: idempotent\n        elif kind == \"remove\" and path == \"members\":\n            out -= {m[\"value\"] for m in value}               # some IdPs list removals in value\n        elif kind == \"remove\" and path and (m := REMOVE_FILTER.match(path)):\n            out.discard(m.group(1))                          # removing a non-member is a no-op\n        elif kind == \"replace\" and path == \"members\":\n            out = {m[\"value\"] for m in value}                # legal, but a full rewrite each time\n        else:\n            raise ValueError(f\"unsupported group operation: {op}\")\n    return out, out - members, members - out\n\nbody = {\"schemas\": [\"urn:ietf:params:scim:api:messages:2.0:PatchOp\"],\n        \"Operations\": [{\"op\": \"Add\", \"path\": \"members\", \"value\": [{\"value\": \"u3\"}]},\n                       {\"op\": \"remove\", \"path\": 'members[value eq \"u1\"]'}]}\nnew, added, removed = apply_group_patch({\"u1\", \"u2\"}, body)\nprint(sorted(new), added, removed)       # ['u2', 'u3'] {'u3'} {'u1'}\nprint(apply_group_patch(new, body)[1:])  # (set(), set()): replay changes nothing\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: one change to a 5,000-member group\n| Approach | Elements written | ACL cache entries invalidated | Race risk |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Incremental `add` of 1 member | 1 | 1 user | None: concurrent adds commute |\n| `replace` of the full member list | 5,000 | all 5,000 users, unless you diff | A replace built from a stale list drops the other change's member |\n| Daily: 20 changes as replaces | 100,000 | up to 100,000 | Grows with change rate |\n\n- **Agent impact:** invalidating 5,000 users' retrieval caches at once makes every one of their next agent\n  queries a cold, slower retrieval, so diffing also protects agent latency.","patterns":["**Canary leaver test.** Keep a test user in each customer's IdP, deactivate it on a schedule, and measure time until its session, API token and a scheduled test agent stop working. Monitor that time against the bound you promised the security team.","**Per-step principal checks in the agent runtime.** Before each tool call, confirm the user is still active (a cheap cached lookup invalidated by SCIM), so in-flight runs stop promptly. Monitor agent runs aborted by deactivation and steps executed after deactivation (should be zero).","**Hashed, per-tenant SCIM tokens with rotation.** Store only a hash of each tenant's bearer token, scope it to the SCIM endpoints, and support two valid tokens during rotation. Monitor auth failures per tenant, which usually mean the customer rotated on their side.","**Raw-request logging plus per-IdP contract tests.** Log each SCIM body (with personal data handled per policy) and replay captured Okta and Entra payloads in CI, including string booleans and path-less replaces. Monitor 4xx rate per IdP from your logs and the IdP's provisioning log.","**Membership deltas drive ACL invalidation.** Compute added and removed members per patch and invalidate only those principals in retrieval caches. Monitor lag from a SCIM group change to the retrieval filter reflecting it.","**Orphan and drift report.** Weekly, list accounts with no SCIM link (created only by JIT) and active users not seen in the IdP feed. Monitor both counts per tenant; a rising number means the join key or the push scope is wrong."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agents keep working when people do not: scheduled runs, stored tokens,\n  long-lived memory. SCIM deactivation is the signal that stops them, and SCIM group changes are what keep\n  ACL-aware retrieval from answering out of a team's documents after someone has left that team.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** A `/scim/v2` endpoint on your platform, the user and group tables\n  your retrieval filter and tool permissions read, the job scheduler that runs background agents, and the\n  permission caches in front of your vector store.\n- **What you control.** What deactivation triggers (revoke sessions and tokens, pause agents, invalidate\n  ACL caches), the join key between SCIM and SSO identities, and how fast a group change reaches the\n  retrieval filter.\n- **What you can skip.** The full RFC schema catalogue and bulk operations. Know `/Users`, `/Groups`,\n  `PatchOp` shapes, 409 on duplicates, paging and the per-IdP quirks.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Leaver offboarding for agent platforms.** Deactivation stops scheduled agents, revokes stored tool\n  tokens and blocks new runs within the promised bound.\n- **Team moves and ACL-aware RAG.** Group patches update the principals the retrieval filter checks, so a\n  user who left Finance stops getting Finance answers.\n- **Directory-driven agent routing.** `department` and `manager` attributes let an approval agent route\n  requests to the right person.\n- **Security questionnaires.** A measured canary leaver test answers \"when do they lose access?\" with a number.","mini_project":"**SCIM endpoint that stops a running agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** prove that deactivating a user in the IdP stops their agent mid-run and updates retrieval access.\n- **Steps:** build `/scim/v2/Users` and `/Groups` with `PatchOp` handling and 409 on duplicate `userName`;\n  replay captured Okta or Entra payloads (or hand-written ones with string booleans and path-less replaces);\n  give a toy agent a per-step active check and a group-filtered document search; deactivate and move a user\n  while their agent runs.\n- **Done when:** the agent aborts at its next step after deactivation, the moved user's next search excludes\n  the old group's documents, and replaying every payload twice leaves state unchanged."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    I[\"Customer IdP\"] -->|\"SCIM request\"| E[\"SCIM endpoint\"]\n    E --> T[\"Tenant from bearer token\"]\n    T --> O{\"Operation\"}\n    O -->|\"POST Users\"| C[\"Create or 409 on duplicate\"]\n    O -->|\"PATCH Groups\"| G[\"Apply member add or remove\"]\n    O -->|\"PATCH active false\"| D[\"Deactivate\"]\n    G --> AC[\"Invalidate ACL cache for changed users\"]\n    D --> R[\"Revoke sessions and API tokens\"]\n    D --> J[\"Pause agents and jobs\"]\n    D --> AC\n    E -.->|\"raw request\"| L[(\"Audit log\")]","caption":"Every request is scoped by its credential first; deactivation fans out to every place access lives,\nincluding running agents."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is SSO with just-in-time provisioning not enough for enterprise customers?","a":"- **JIT only learns about users at login**, so a leaver who never signs in again keeps their account, API\n  tokens and scheduled agents.\n- **Moves lag too:** old group memberships persist until the next login, so retrieval filters stay wrong.\n- **SCIM fixes both** by having the IdP push lifecycle and membership changes as they happen."},{"q":"Provisioning works with Okta but Entra shows 400 errors on every update. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Pull the raw request from your log** and the matching entry in Entra's provisioning log.\n- **Usual causes:** `op` in different letter case, `\"False\"` as a string, or a `replace` with no `path` and\n  an object value.\n- **Fix:** parse leniently, add the captured payload to a per-IdP contract test, and re-run provisioning."},{"q":"Should a deprovisioned user be soft-deactivated or hard-deleted?","a":"- **Soft by default:** IdPs usually send `active=false`, customers want accounts recoverable, and audit\n  history must survive.\n- **Cost:** every query, filter and job must exclude inactive users.\n- **Hard delete** belongs to the retention policy, run as a separate, deliberate process."},{"q":"How do you design the join between SCIM-created users and SSO logins?","a":"- **Agree one immutable key** with the customer admin, such as the Entra object ID or Okta user ID, sent as\n  `externalId` or a mapped attribute.\n- **Make JIT look up by that key** before creating, so the two channels land on one row.\n- **Watch the orphan report:** accounts with no SCIM link reveal a broken join early."},{"q":"A 5,000-member group changes 20 times a day. What does that cost and how do you keep it cheap?","a":"- **With full replaces** it is 100,000 member writes a day and, without diffing, cache invalidation for\n  everyone each time.\n- **With incremental add and remove** it is about 20 writes, and only the changed principals are invalidated.\n- **Also** sync only groups assigned to your app, which cuts volume and blast radius."},{"q":"A user was deactivated at 10:00 but their research agent, started at 09:55, sent an email at 10:20. What do you change?","a":"- **Check the principal per step, not per run:** the runtime must confirm the user is active before every\n  tool call, using a cache the SCIM handler invalidates.\n- **Make deactivation cancel in-flight runs** and revoke the stored tokens the agent's email tool uses.\n- **Prove it** with a canary: start a long agent run, deactivate the test user, and assert no tool call\n  executes after the SCIM event is processed."}],"tradeoffs":["**Near-real-time sync vs per-IdP quirks.** SCIM gives lifecycle sync with a standard you build once, but every IdP has quirks, so \"SCIM compliant\" still means per-IdP testing. JIT-only is acceptable for small customers without compliance requirements and without background agents.","**Soft deactivation vs hard deletion.** Soft deactivation preserves history and allows reactivation, but inactive users must be excluded everywhere, including agent schedulers and memory retrieval. Hard deletion is simpler to reason about but conflicts with audit retention.","**Pushed groups vs volume.** Pushing memberships enables group-based permissions in retrieval, but large groups generate many PATCH operations. Syncing only groups assigned to your app keeps volume and blast radius small.","**Authoritative attributes vs self-service.** SCIM-managed attributes stay authoritative, but users can no longer edit them in your UI. The trade-off flips if customers expect self-service profile edits."],"pitfalls":["**Treating `active=false` as a profile field update.** Symptom: a deactivated user's API token is still calling your API the next day.","**Forgetting background agents.** Symptom: a leaver's nightly report agent keeps emailing customers. Fix: deactivation pauses schedules and revokes stored tool tokens.","**Tenant from the payload or URL alone.** Symptom: a misconfigured IdP creates users in the wrong customer's directory.","**Returning 201 for a duplicate `userName` instead of 409.** Symptom: duplicate accounts after the IdP retries a create.","**Strict PATCH parsing.** Symptom: provisioning works with one IdP and fails with another, with 400 errors in the IdP's provisioning log.","**Joining SCIM users to SSO logins by email.** Symptom: duplicate accounts for one person after an email change, with permissions on one and history on the other.","**Not propagating group removals to retrieval ACL caches.** Symptom: a user who changed teams still gets answers from the old team's documents."],"when":"- **Use when** you sell to organizations with an IAM team, compliance requirements, or more than a few dozen\n  users; many enterprise security reviews ask about it explicitly.\n- **Pair it with SSO**, and make deactivation revoke everything, agents included.\n- **Measure:** time from IdP change to effect in your app (test end to end with a canary user), provisioning\n  error rate per tenant and IdP from your logs and the IdP's provisioning log, accounts with no SCIM link\n  (created only by JIT), and active users not seen in the IdP feed.\n- **Interviews:** explain why SSO alone does not deprovision, and what deactivation must trigger; that is the\n  core signal.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the `/Users` and `/Groups` endpoints, lenient PATCH parsing, idempotent operations and the revocation side effects of deactivation.","architect":"Decides the user lifecycle model (soft versus hard delete, retention), how SCIM identities join SSO identities, and how membership changes propagate to permission caches.","fde":"Configures the customer's Okta or Entra provisioning app, scopes which groups are pushed, and proves with a test user that removal from the IdP revokes access within minutes."},"check":{"q":"A customer's security team asks: \"If we terminate someone at 9:00, when do they lose access to your AI\nassistant and the documents it indexed?\" How do you answer, given SSO and SCIM are both in place?","a":"At 9:00 the IdP blocks new sign-ins and sends a SCIM `active=false` on its next provisioning cycle, which\ncan be near-immediate or periodic depending on IdP and configuration (Okta pushes on the change event; Entra's\nprovisioning service documents incremental cycles on the order of tens of minutes, so state the bound for\nthe customer's IdP). On receipt we revoke all sessions and API\ntokens, pause their scheduled agents and invalidate permission caches, so access ends within that delay plus\nseconds. Retrieval filters are evaluated per request against current memberships, so no indexed content is\nreachable afterwards. Their data is retained or deleted per the contract's retention policy. We would verify\nthis with a test account and show the audit log."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do IdPs usually deprovision with PATCH active=false rather than DELETE?","options":["DELETE is not part of the SCIM protocol","Customers want the account recoverable and its history retained, so deactivation is the default","PATCH is faster to process than DELETE","DELETE would remove the user from the IdP as well"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SCIM defines DELETE, but deactivation keeps ownership, audit history and the option to reactivate; the app must still treat it as a full revocation event, not a cosmetic flag."},{"q":"How should a SCIM endpoint determine which tenant a request belongs to?","options":["From a tenant attribute in the JSON body","From the user's email domain","From the per-tenant bearer token or client credentials used to authenticate the request","From the IdP's IP address"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The credential is the trust anchor issued to one customer's IdP; payload fields and email domains can be wrong or spoofed, and IdP egress IPs are shared across many customers."},{"q":"What does SCIM provide that SSO with just-in-time provisioning does not?","options":["Multi-factor authentication","Signed login assertions","Notification of leavers and group changes even when the user never signs in again","Encryption of user passwords"],"answer":2,"explanation":"JIT only learns about users at login, so departures and moves are invisible; SCIM pushes lifecycle and membership changes from the directory as they happen."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","sources":["Ferraiolo & Kuhn, 1992 — Role-Based Access Controls","NIST, 2014 — SP 800-162: Guide to Attribute Based Access Control (ABAC)","Pang et al., 2019 — Zanzibar: Google's Consistent, Global Authorization System"],"why":"- **Nobody designed the two sides to fit.** A customer's Entra or Okta directory has groups like\n  `GRP-FIN-EMEA-RO`, `All Staff` and `Contractors-2023`, and attributes like `department=Legal` and\n  `country=DE`. Your product has roles such as `viewer`, `editor` and `admin`, plus documents synced with\n  their own ACLs.\n- **The naive approaches fail predictably.**\n  - Hard-coding group names (\"if 'Admins' in groups\") works for one customer, is wrong for the next, and\n    breaks on rename.\n  - Granting everyone in the tenant the same access ignores that source systems already restrict HR, M&A\n    and legal content.\n  - Re-implementing their whole permission model creates a second source of truth that drifts.\n- **Pure RBAC explodes on contextual rules.** \"Regional managers see deals in their region\" with six\n  functions, five regions and three seniority levels is 90 roles; adding one region adds 18 more, each bound\n  and reviewed by a customer admin. ABAC states the rule once, `subject.region == resource.region`, and\n  adding a region adds no policy.\n- **AI adds a new principal.** An agent acting for a user must not exceed that user's rights, nor be granted\n  broader rights because it runs as a service. A mapping layer gives each customer a reviewable, testable\n  statement of who can do what.","how":"### Three kinds of permission inputs\n| Input | Examples | Model | Why that model |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. **Product permissions** | configure workspace, run agent X, export | RBAC (your roles) | *Because* the action set is small and stable |\n| 2. **Data permissions** | who may see each document or record | Carried source ACLs (users and groups) | You carry them rather than invent them |\n| 3. **Contextual rules** | region, department, data classification, device, time | ABAC | *Because* these combinations would otherwise multiply roles |\n\n### The mapping record (per tenant)\n- **`role_bindings`:** IdP group *object ID* to product role, e.g. Entra group `3f2a…` to\n  `workspace_admin`. IDs, *because* display names are neither unique nor stable.\n- **`attribute_map`:** IdP claim name to your attribute, e.g. `extension_region` to `region`, with allowed values.\n- **`policies`:** ABAC rules over subject, resource and action attributes, e.g.\n  `resource.classification == \"confidential\" requires subject.clearance >= 2`.\n- **`default`:** what an authenticated user with no matching binding gets, usually nothing beyond basic access.\n\n### Translating source ACLs into the customer's identities\nCarried ACLs name principals in each source's vocabulary (SharePoint site groups, Confluence groups, Slack\nchannels, Salesforce public groups), not the IdP groups your role bindings use. Two ways to join them:\n\n| | Expand at index time | Resolve at query time |\n|---|---|---|\n| Stored on documents | ACL resolved to user IDs | Source principal IDs |\n| Query cost | Simple membership test | Look up the user's set of held source principals |\n| What membership churn costs | One person joining a 10,000-member group rewrites the ACL of every document that group can see | Documents change only when their own ACL changes |\n| Where effort goes | Index churn | Keeping the per-user principal set current and fast (crosswalk of source identities to IdP IDs plus synced source-group membership) |\n| Typical choice | Small groups | More common once groups are large |\n\n- **Either way, an unresolvable source principal grants nothing**, *because* guessing (by display name, say)\n  is how one person's access lands on another.\n\n### Resolving a principal\n1. **At login (OIDC/SAML) and on SCIM updates**, build the principal: stable user key, group IDs (including\n   nested groups resolved by the IdP or Graph), mapped roles and attributes.\n2. **Cache with a short TTL** and invalidate on SCIM events.\n3. **Hand the principal to the agent runtime, not to the model.** Tools read it from the session; the model\n   never supplies user, tenant or role as an argument.\n\n### Enforcing\n- **One policy decision point**, *because* scattered `if` statements cannot be audited.\n- **Explicit combining rules:** deny by default; an explicit deny overrides any allow; a policy whose\n  attribute is missing evaluates to deny (fail closed).\n- **Monitor the missing-attribute rate**, so fail-closed shows up as a data-quality alert rather than as\n  silent lockouts.\n- **Engine options:** a small in-process function; a policy engine (OPA/Rego, Cedar); or a\n  relationship-based system (Zanzibar-style, e.g. OpenFGA or SpiceDB) when permissions follow object graphs\n  like folders and shares.\n\n### AI-specific enforcement\n- **Retrieval:** filter candidate chunks by the principal's source groups and attributes *before* ranking\n  and generation, never by asking the model to withhold.\n- **Agents:** effective permission = user permission ∩ agent's granted scope. The agent never gains rights\n  the user lacks, and a broadly entitled user does not make a narrow agent broad.\n- **Tool exposure:** offer the model only tools the principal may call, *and* re-check at execution,\n  because the model can still emit a call to a tool name it saw elsewhere (or was told about by injected text).\n- **Resource-level checks inside tools:** a `read_ticket(id)` tool allowed by role must still check that\n  this ticket is visible to this user, because the model picks the ID.\n- **Audit:** log the decision inputs (principal, rule matched) so a customer can ask \"why could Ana see this?\"","example":"### In-process evaluator with the three layers\nTenant config is data, not code; agent scope intersects with user rights.\n\n```python\nTENANT_CFG = {\n    \"acme\": {\n        \"role_bindings\": {\"3f2a9c1e\": \"workspace_admin\", \"8b7d0e44\": \"analyst\"},  # Entra group IDs\n        \"attribute_map\": {\"extension_region\": \"region\", \"department\": \"department\"},\n    }\n}\nROLE_ACTIONS = {\"workspace_admin\": {\"configure\", \"query\", \"export\"}, \"analyst\": {\"query\"}}\n\ndef principal(tenant: str, claims: dict) -> dict:\n    cfg = TENANT_CFG[tenant]\n    groups = set(claims.get(\"groups\", []))\n    roles = {r for g, r in cfg[\"role_bindings\"].items() if g in groups}\n    attrs = {ours: claims.get(theirs) for theirs, ours in cfg[\"attribute_map\"].items()}\n    return {\"tenant\": tenant, \"groups\": groups, \"roles\": roles, \"attrs\": attrs}\n\ndef can_act(p: dict, action: str, agent_scope: set | None = None) -> bool:\n    allowed = set().union(*(ROLE_ACTIONS[r] for r in p[\"roles\"])) if p[\"roles\"] else set()\n    if agent_scope is not None:\n        allowed &= agent_scope                       # agent never exceeds the user, nor vice versa\n    return action in allowed\n\ndef can_read(p: dict, doc: dict) -> bool:\n    if doc[\"tenant\"] != p[\"tenant\"]:\n        return False                                 # tenant boundary first, always\n    if not (p[\"groups\"] & set(doc[\"acl_groups\"])):   # source-system ACL (RBAC on data)\n        return False\n    if doc.get(\"region\") and doc[\"region\"] != p[\"attrs\"].get(\"region\"):\n        return False                                 # ABAC: regional restriction\n    return True\n\nana = principal(\"acme\", {\"groups\": [\"8b7d0e44\", \"g-sales-emea\"], \"extension_region\": \"EMEA\"})\ndeal = {\"tenant\": \"acme\", \"acl_groups\": [\"g-sales-emea\", \"g-sales-amer\"], \"region\": \"AMER\"}\nprint(can_act(ana, \"query\"), can_act(ana, \"export\"), can_read(ana, deal))   # True False False\nprint(can_act(ana, \"query\", agent_scope={\"export\"}))                       # False\n```\n\n- **Ana is an analyst in the EMEA sales group.** She can query but not export.\n- **The ACL matches but ABAC denies.** She is in a group on the deal's ACL, yet the regional attribute rule\n  denies the AMER deal.\n- **The agent cannot amplify her.** An agent scoped only to `export` cannot query on her behalf.\n\n### Gating every agent tool call\nRunnable standard library, reusing the idea above: the runtime filters the tool list per principal and\nre-checks each call, including the resource the model chose.\n\n```python\nAGENT_SCOPE = {\"deal_assistant\": {\"query\", \"summarize\"}}\nTOOL_ACTION = {\"search_deals\": \"query\", \"summarize_deal\": \"summarize\", \"export_deals\": \"export\"}\nUSER_ACTIONS = {\"ana\": {\"query\", \"export\"}}            # from role bindings\nDEAL_REGION = {\"D-1\": \"EMEA\", \"D-2\": \"AMER\"}\nUSER_REGION = {\"ana\": \"EMEA\"}\n\ndef allowed_actions(user: str, agent: str) -> set:\n    return USER_ACTIONS.get(user, set()) & AGENT_SCOPE.get(agent, set())   # intersection\n\ndef tools_for_model(user: str, agent: str) -> list[str]:\n    return [t for t, a in TOOL_ACTION.items() if a in allowed_actions(user, agent)]\n\ndef execute(user: str, agent: str, tool: str, args: dict) -> dict:\n    action = TOOL_ACTION.get(tool)\n    if action not in allowed_actions(user, agent):\n        return {\"error\": f\"denied: {tool} not permitted\", \"rule\": \"user ∩ agent scope\"}\n    deal = args.get(\"deal_id\")\n    if deal and DEAL_REGION.get(deal) != USER_REGION.get(user):\n        return {\"error\": f\"denied: {deal} outside your region\", \"rule\": \"abac.region\"}\n    return {\"ok\": f\"{tool}({args})\"}\n\nprint(tools_for_model(\"ana\", \"deal_assistant\"))                    # ['search_deals'] only\nprint(execute(\"ana\", \"deal_assistant\", \"export_deals\", {}))        # denied: not in agent scope\nprint(execute(\"ana\", \"deal_assistant\", \"search_deals\", {\"deal_id\": \"D-2\"}))  # denied: ABAC\n```\n\n- **Denials go back to the model as tool results** with a reason, so the agent can explain instead of\n  retrying, and the rule name lands in the decision log.\n\n### Query-time resolution of source principals\nPure Python, runnable. Documents keep source principal IDs; each user gets a cached set of principals they\nhold, built from the crosswalk and synced source-group membership.\n\n```python\n# Synced from each source: source group -> member source identities (nested groups pre-expanded)\nSOURCE_GROUP_MEMBERS = {\n    \"confluence:group:legal\": {\"atl:5b10\"},                 # Atlassian account IDs\n    \"sharepoint:sitegroup:hr-owners\": {\"entra:9a1f\"},       # SharePoint uses Entra object IDs\n    \"slack:channel:C-deals\": {\"slack:U02ABC\", \"slack:U07XYZ\"},\n}\n# Crosswalk: source identity -> IdP object ID, joined on IdP ID or verified email\nCROSSWALK = {\"atl:5b10\": \"entra:9a1f\", \"slack:U02ABC\": \"entra:9a1f\", \"entra:9a1f\": \"entra:9a1f\"}\n\ndef principal_set(idp_user: str) -> set:\n    \"\"\"Every source principal this IdP user holds; computed per user, cached, invalidated on change.\"\"\"\n    mine = {src for src, idp in CROSSWALK.items() if idp == idp_user}\n    return mine | {g for g, members in SOURCE_GROUP_MEMBERS.items() if members & mine}\n\ndef visible(doc_acl: set, held: set) -> bool:\n    return bool(doc_acl & held)          # unresolved principals never match anyone: fail closed\n\nheld = principal_set(\"entra:9a1f\")\nprint(visible({\"confluence:group:legal\"}, held))          # True: via crosswalked Atlassian ID\nprint(visible({\"slack:channel:C-board\"}, held))           # False: not a member\nprint(visible({\"confluence:user:unknown-guest\"}, held))   # False: unresolved grants nothing\n```\n\n### Worked numbers: roles vs attributes, index vs query time\n| Change | RBAC-only or index-time cost | ABAC or query-time cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| Regional rule, 6 functions × 5 regions × 3 levels | 90 roles to bind and review | 1 rule |\n| Add a sixth region | 18 more roles | 0 new rules; tag deals with the new region |\n| One person joins a 10,000-member group that can see 200,000 documents (illustrative) | 200,000 document ACL rewrites | 1 user's principal set recomputed |","patterns":["**Mapping as versioned, reviewed config.** Store each tenant's `role_bindings`, `attribute_map` and policies as data with change history, edited through an admin UI or reviewed change, never in code. Monitor unmapped-user counts per tenant after each change.","**A tool gate in the agent runtime.** Wrap every tool with one check (user ∩ agent scope, then the resource-level rule) and filter the tool list shown to the model per principal. Monitor denied tool calls by tool and rule; a spike often means a prompt or injection is pushing the agent toward a forbidden tool.","**Fail closed with a data-quality alarm.** Deny when a required attribute is missing, and alert when the missing rate for `region` or `department` exceeds a threshold you agree with the customer. Monitor denied-request reasons.","**Externalised policies when rules grow.** Move rules to an engine such as Cedar, where the regional rule is `permit (principal, action == Action::\"read\", resource) when { principal.region == resource.region };`, or to OpenFGA/SpiceDB for folder-and-share graphs. Monitor decision latency on the request path, which is paid on every agent step.","**Decision logs that answer \"why\".** Log principal, resource, action and the rule that matched for every retrieval and agent decision, linked to the agent trace. Monitor by sampling: a customer admin reviews a set of decisions each quarter.","**Canary users per sensitive group.** Keep test users in HR, legal and each region, and run scheduled agent queries as them that must and must not return known documents. Alert on any wrong answer."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Retrieval and tool calls are where wrong mappings leak. An agent can\n  paraphrase a restricted document or run an action the user could never click, so the allow or deny must\n  happen in code before the model sees data or a tool executes.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The retrieval filter in front of the vector store, a gate wrapped\n  around every tool your agent runtime exposes, the list of tools offered to the model per user, and the\n  decision log next to your agent traces.\n- **What you control.** The rule that effective permission = user rights ∩ agent scope, fail-closed\n  defaults, which tools a principal is even shown, and per-tenant mapping config keyed by immutable group IDs.\n- **What you can skip.** Formal access-control theory and building your own policy language. Pick an\n  in-process function first, and Cedar, OPA or OpenFGA when rules or object graphs grow.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **ACL-aware RAG assistants.** Retrieval filters on source ACLs and region or classification attributes\n  before ranking, so answers never paraphrase restricted content.\n- **Tool-gated action agents.** A deal or support agent sees only the tools its scope and the user's role\n  allow, with resource checks inside each tool.\n- **Multi-tenant agent platforms.** Each customer's mapping config binds its own group IDs to your roles\n  without code changes.\n- **Access reviews and audits.** Decision logs linked to agent traces answer \"why could Ana see this?\"","mini_project":"**Permission gate for a three-tool agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** enforce user ∩ agent scope plus one ABAC rule on every tool call and retrieval of a small agent.\n- **Steps:** define two tenants' mapping configs keyed by fake group IDs; build `search_deals`,\n  `read_deal` and `export_deals` tools; filter the tool list per principal; add a region rule inside\n  `read_deal`; log each decision with the rule matched; try a prompt that asks the agent to export or to\n  read another region's deal.\n- **Done when:** forbidden tools never execute, cross-region reads return a denial the agent explains, a\n  renamed group display name changes nothing, and the decision log names the rule for every call."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"IdP claims and SCIM data\"] --> P[\"Resolve principal\"]\n    M[(\"Tenant mapping config\")] --> P\n    P --> PDP{\"Policy decision point\"}\n    PDP -->|\"role allows action\"| A[\"Product action\"]\n    PDP -->|\"ACL and attribute match\"| R[\"Retrieval filter before ranking\"]\n    PDP -->|\"user rights and agent scope\"| G[\"Agent tool call\"]\n    PDP -.->|\"principal and rule matched\"| L[(\"Decision audit log\")]\n    PDP -->|\"no match or missing attribute\"| D[\"Deny\"]","caption":"Identity claims become a principal once; every product action, retrieval and agent call asks the same\ndecision point."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between RBAC and ABAC, and why do enterprise AI products usually need both?","a":"- **RBAC** grants actions through roles; it fits product permissions because the action set is small and\n  stable and admins can review bindings.\n- **ABAC** evaluates rules over subject, resource and action attributes; it fits region, classification and\n  clearance rules that would otherwise multiply roles.\n- **Both, plus carried source ACLs**, because data visibility is already defined in the customer's systems."},{"q":"A user who changed teams still gets answers from the old team's documents. Where do you look?","a":"- **Principal cache:** was it invalidated on the SCIM group-removal event, or is it waiting out its TTL?\n- **Source-group sync:** has the source system's own group membership been resynced, since carried ACLs lag\n  by a sync cycle?\n- **Index-time expansion:** if ACLs were expanded to users, the rewrite for that group may be queued or failed."},{"q":"Would you expand document ACLs to users at index time or resolve them at query time?","a":"- **Index time** for small, stable groups: queries are a cheap membership test.\n- **Query time** once groups are large: one join to a 10,000-member group otherwise rewrites every document\n  that group can see.\n- **Either way**, unresolved principals grant nothing."},{"q":"How do agent permissions combine with user permissions, and why not a union?","a":"- **Effective permission is the intersection** of the user's rights and the agent's granted scope.\n- **A union lets either side amplify the other:** a narrow agent becomes broad for an admin, or a service\n  identity gives a junior user admin actions."},{"q":"A third of the customer's users have a blank `department`. What happens to your ABAC rules?","a":"- **With fail-closed evaluation**, those users are denied anything that depends on department, which looks\n  like an outage to them.\n- **With careless defaults**, they may be over-granted, which is worse.\n- **Fix it upstream:** alert on missing-attribute rate, agree a cleanup or fallback attribute with the IAM\n  admin, and test before rollout."},{"q":"Where exactly do you enforce permissions in a tool-calling agent, and why not in the system prompt?","a":"- **Three places in code:** the retrieval filter before ranking, the tool list offered per principal, and a\n  gate at execution that re-checks the action and the specific resource.\n- **Not the prompt**, because instructions can be overridden by injected content or ignored, and the model\n  chooses IDs and arguments you must not trust.\n- **Log each decision with the trace**, so a customer can see why the agent could or could not act."}],"tradeoffs":["**RBAC reviewability vs role explosion.** Role bindings are easy for customer admins to review, but encoding contextual rules as roles explodes their number. Switch to ABAC when roles start encoding region × function × level.","**ABAC compactness vs attribute quality.** Decisions depend on the customer's directory; if `department` is blank for a third of users, policies silently deny or over-grant.","**Carried ACLs vs live checks.** Carrying source ACLs keeps you consistent with existing permissions, but inherits their mistakes and lags by a sync cycle. Live checks against the source are exact but slow, and an agent may make several retrieval calls per answer.","**Central engine vs in-process library.** A central policy engine gives auditability and consistent decisions but adds a dependency on the request path. An in-process library is faster and enough while rules stay simple.","**Hiding tools vs showing and denying.** Hiding forbidden tools keeps the model from trying them and saves prompt tokens; showing them and returning denials lets the agent explain what the user cannot do. Either way the execution-time check is mandatory."],"pitfalls":["**Binding roles to group display names.** Symptom: a customer renames a group and all its members lose or gain access overnight.","**A permissive default for unmapped users.** Symptom: a newly onboarded department sees the whole workspace because no binding matched.","**Filtering after generation, or relying on the prompt to withhold.** Symptom: restricted facts appear in answers, paraphrased.","**Agents with a service identity broader than the user.** Symptom: an agent completes an action the user could not perform directly in the UI.","**Checking the tool but not the resource.** Symptom: a user allowed to `read_ticket` gets another team's ticket because the model guessed its ID. Fix: resource-level check inside the tool.","**Index-time expansion for large groups.** Symptom: index write volume and permission lag spike whenever HR moves people between big groups.","**Ignoring nested groups.** Symptom: users whose access comes through a parent group see nothing."],"when":"- **Use when** a second customer brings its own directory, and before any AI feature indexes customer content.\n- **Start with** role bindings by group ID plus carried source ACLs.\n- **Add ABAC** when customers ask for regional, classification or clearance restrictions.\n- **Gate every tool** from the first agent release; retrofitting permissions after tools ship is how\n  incidents happen.\n- **Measure:** unmapped-user counts per tenant, denied-request reasons, and periodic access reviews where a\n  customer admin checks a sample of decisions.\n- **Interviews:** explain how user rights, agent scope and document ACLs combine, and where the decision is\n  enforced.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements principal resolution, the policy decision function or engine integration, and pre-retrieval filters that use source ACL groups and attributes.","architect":"Defines the product permission model, chooses RBAC, ABAC or relationship-based enforcement, and sets the rule that agent permissions are the intersection with the user's.","fde":"Runs the mapping workshop with the customer's IAM admin, records group-ID bindings and attribute sources, and validates with test users from each sensitive group."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants: admins configure the workspace, everyone else queries, and deal documents are visible\nonly within the user's region unless they are in the global deals team. How do you model this?","a":"Product permissions are RBAC: bind the customer's admin group ID to `workspace_admin`, and a default or\nall-staff group ID to `analyst`. Document visibility starts from carried source ACLs. The regional rule is\nABAC: map the IdP region attribute and tag each deal with its region, then allow read when regions match or\nthe user is in the global-deals group ID. Enforce in one policy function used by the retrieval filter and by\nagent tools, and verify attribute coverage first, because users with no region would otherwise be denied\neverything."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should role bindings reference IdP group object IDs rather than names?","options":["IDs are shorter, so tokens are smaller","Names can be duplicated and renamed, while object IDs are unique and stable","SAML cannot transmit group names","Names leak information about the customer's org chart"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A rename or a second group with the same display name would silently change who holds a role; immutable IDs keep bindings correct, with names shown only for readability."},{"q":"An agent with scope {query, summarize} runs for a user who can {query, export}. What may it do?","options":["query, summarize and export","summarize only","query only","export only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Effective permissions are the intersection of the user's rights and the agent's scope, so only query is allowed; a union would let either party amplify the other."},{"q":"When does ABAC become preferable to adding more roles?","options":["When the product has fewer than five actions","When the customer uses SAML instead of OIDC","When access depends on combinations of attributes such as region, classification and department","When users belong to only one group each"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Expressing attribute combinations as roles multiplies them, one per combination; ABAC states the rule once over attributes, at the cost of depending on attribute quality."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/webhooks","sources":["Official docs: Slack Events API and request signing","Official docs: Microsoft Graph change notifications","Official docs: Stripe webhooks"],"why":"- **Freshness is the product.** An AI assistant indexed over Jira, Slack or SharePoint is only as fresh as\n  its last sync.\n- **Polling scales badly.** Calls multiply by tenants × containers × polls, most return \"nothing changed\",\n  and minutes of staleness remain. Illustratively, 200 tenants with 50 watched containers each, polled every\n  5 minutes, is 200 × 50 × 288 ≈ 2.9 million calls a day, and average staleness is still about half the\n  interval. Halving staleness doubles the calls.\n- **Agents feel the latency.** \"When a ticket is assigned to the AI queue, draft a reply\" waits on the poll.\n- **Webhooks invert the direction**: the source tells you when something happens.\n- **The naive receiver's three assumptions are false.** It works inside the handler, trusts the body, and\n  expects exactly-once, in-order delivery:\n  - the handler times out calling an LLM, the sender retries, and you process the event twice;\n  - anyone who discovers the URL can post fake events, e.g. a forged \"document updated\" injecting attacker\n    text into your index;\n  - events for one object arrive out of order, so an older \"updated\" overwrites newer state;\n  - senders drop events during outages on either side, or disable subscriptions that fail too often.","how":"### 1. Subscribe\n- **Register your URL per tenant for specific event types.** Subscribe only to events your agents act on,\n  because every extra type is extra queue load and extra triggers to reason about.\n- **Prove ownership where required.** Slack sends a `url_verification` challenge you must echo; Microsoft\n  Graph sends a `validationToken` you return as plain text.\n- **Renew expiring subscriptions.** Graph subscriptions expire, so renew them on a schedule. Store\n  subscription IDs and expiry, *because* an expired subscription fails silently: nothing arrives.\n- **Handle lifecycle notifications.** Graph sends them (for example a subscription needs reauthorization, or\n  notifications were missed); *because* \"missed\" is the sender telling you to run a catch-up sync.\n\n### 2. Verify\n- **Reject anything not provably from the sender.** Most use an HMAC of the raw body (often with a\n  timestamp) under a shared secret; some use a client-state token you chose at subscription time, or mutual TLS.\n- **Compare in constant time and reject old timestamps** to limit replay. Details are in the\n  signature-verification lesson.\n- **Verify the raw bytes before parsing.** That is the integration rule.\n- **Derive the tenant from which secret verified the request** (or from the subscription ID you stored),\n  never from a tenant field in the body, *because* the body is exactly what an attacker controls.\n\n### 3. Acknowledge fast, process later\n- **Return 2xx within the sender's timeout** after verifying and durably enqueueing (Slack, for example,\n  expects a response within three seconds), *because* slow work in the handler causes timeouts, retries and\n  duplicates. An agent run takes seconds to minutes, so it can never run inside the handler.\n- **Know what the sender does next.** Most retry with backoff a bounded number of times (Slack marks retries\n  with `X-Slack-Retry-Num` and `X-Slack-Retry-Reason` headers); some disable a subscription whose endpoint\n  keeps failing. Either way, your failure window becomes lost events.\n\n### 4. Process idempotently\n- **Deduplicate by the sender's event ID** (or a hash of the payload) in the consumer. Senders retry on\n  non-2xx and sometimes even after success, so duplicates are normal.\n- **A dedup table is an optimisation, not the guarantee.** Two workers can pick up the same event\n  concurrently and both see \"not seen yet\".\n- **The guarantee is idempotent processing:** re-fetch current state, upsert by key, so a duplicate that\n  slips through does no harm.\n- **Claim non-idempotent side effects** (posting a reply) with an atomic insert-if-absent before acting.\n- **Key agent runs by event.** Derive the run ID from the event ID, so a redelivered event resumes or\n  skips the existing run instead of starting a second agent that drafts a second reply.\n\n### 5. Treat payloads as hints\n- **Many events are thin** (an ID and a type); even fat payloads may be stale by processing time.\n- **Re-fetch the current object** and apply it, or compare a version or modified timestamp and discard older\n  ones, *because* ordering is not guaranteed.\n- **Payload text is untrusted data for the model.** Re-fetching through the source API with the right\n  identity confirms the object exists and is visible; even then, wrap its text as data, not instructions,\n  because ticket bodies and messages can carry prompt injections.\n\n### 6. Reconcile\n- **Run a periodic incremental sync** (delta query, `updated since` watermark) that catches whatever the\n  webhooks missed. Webhooks give latency; reconciliation gives correctness.\n- **Alert when reconciliation finds many undelivered changes**, since that usually means a broken subscription.","example":"### Slack Events API receiver in FastAPI\nVerify the signature over the raw body, answer the URL challenge, enqueue and return immediately.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, hmac, json, os, time\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, Request, Response, HTTPException\n\napp = FastAPI()\nSECRET = os.environ[\"SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET\"].encode()\n\ndef verify(raw: bytes, ts: str, sig: str) -> None:\n    if abs(time.time() - int(ts)) > 300:                     # 5-minute replay window\n        raise HTTPException(401, detail=\"stale timestamp\")\n    base = b\"v0:\" + ts.encode() + b\":\" + raw\n    expected = \"v0=\" + hmac.new(SECRET, base, hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()\n    if not hmac.compare_digest(expected, sig):               # constant-time compare\n        raise HTTPException(401, detail=\"bad signature\")\n\n@app.post(\"/hooks/slack\")\nasync def slack_events(req: Request):\n    raw = await req.body()                                   # raw bytes, before any JSON parsing\n    verify(raw, req.headers.get(\"X-Slack-Request-Timestamp\", \"0\"),\n           req.headers.get(\"X-Slack-Signature\", \"\"))\n    body = json.loads(raw)\n    if body.get(\"type\") == \"url_verification\":\n        return {\"challenge\": body[\"challenge\"]}\n    await queue.enqueue(\"slack-events\", key=body[\"event_id\"], payload=body)  # durable, then ack\n    return Response(status_code=200)\n\nasync def consume(msg):                                      # runs in a worker, not the handler\n    if await dedup.seen(msg.key):                            # event_id seen before: retry duplicate\n        return\n    ev = msg.payload[\"event\"]\n    if ev[\"type\"] == \"message\":\n        m = ev.get(\"message\") or ev.get(\"previous_message\") or ev   # edits/deletes nest the original\n        root = m.get(\"thread_ts\") or m[\"ts\"]\n        current = await slack.conversations_replies(ev[\"channel\"], root)\n        await indexer.upsert_thread(msg.payload[\"team_id\"], ev[\"channel\"], current)  # re-fetched state\n    await dedup.mark(msg.key)                                # mark only after success, so a crash retries\n```\n\n- **Whole-thread re-fetch.** The consumer re-fetches the thread rather than trusting the single message,\n  which also handles edits and out-of-order delivery for that thread.\n- **Nested messages on edits and deletes.** `message_changed` and `message_deleted` subtypes carry the\n  affected message nested inside the event, and the event's own `ts` is not a message, so the root timestamp\n  is read from the nested message.\n- **Deleted roots.** A deleted thread root makes the re-fetch return not-found, which the indexer must treat\n  as a delete.\n- **Mark after success.** Marking the dedup key first would turn a worker crash into a silently dropped event.\n\n### Event-triggered agent run that replies exactly once\nRunnable standard library: a ticket-assigned event starts at most one agent run per event, the run reads\nthe re-fetched ticket, and the reply is claimed before it is posted.\n\n```python\nimport sqlite3\n\ndb = sqlite3.connect(\":memory:\")\ndb.execute(\"CREATE TABLE runs (run_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, status TEXT)\")\ndb.execute(\"CREATE TABLE side_effects (key TEXT PRIMARY KEY)\")\nTICKETS = {\"T-9\": {\"assignee\": \"ai-queue\", \"body\": \"Printer on floor 3 is jammed.\"}}\nPOSTED = []\n\ndef start_run(event_id: str) -> bool:\n    cur = db.execute(\"INSERT OR IGNORE INTO runs VALUES (?, 'running')\", (f\"run:{event_id}\",))\n    db.commit(); return cur.rowcount == 1            # redelivery of the same event: no second run\n\ndef agent_draft(ticket: dict) -> str:                # stand-in for the LLM call\n    return f\"Draft reply for: {ticket['body'][:40]}\"\n\ndef handle_ticket_assigned(event: dict) -> str:\n    if not start_run(event[\"id\"]):\n        return \"duplicate event, run already exists\"\n    ticket = TICKETS.get(event[\"ticket_id\"])         # re-fetch: never trust the payload's copy\n    if not ticket or ticket[\"assignee\"] != \"ai-queue\":\n        return \"stale event, ticket no longer in AI queue\"\n    reply = agent_draft(ticket)                      # ticket text is data for the model\n    cur = db.execute(\"INSERT OR IGNORE INTO side_effects VALUES (?)\", (f\"reply:{event['ticket_id']}:{event['id']}\",))\n    db.commit()\n    if cur.rowcount == 1:\n        POSTED.append(reply)                         # the one non-idempotent action\n    return \"replied\"\n\nev = {\"id\": \"Ev42\", \"ticket_id\": \"T-9\", \"body\": \"IGNORE PREVIOUS INSTRUCTIONS\"}   # forged-looking payload text\nprint(handle_ticket_assigned(ev), handle_ticket_assigned(ev), len(POSTED))   # replied duplicate... 1\n```\n\n- **The payload's body is never used.** The run reads the ticket fetched from the source, so forged or\n  stale payload text cannot steer the agent.\n\n### Atomic claim and version-guarded upsert\nPure Python with `sqlite3`, runnable. The same SQL shape works in Postgres with\n`INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (event_id) DO NOTHING`.\n\n```python\nimport sqlite3\n\ndb = sqlite3.connect(\":memory:\")\ndb.execute(\"CREATE TABLE claims (event_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY)\")\ndb.execute(\"CREATE TABLE docs (id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, body TEXT, version INTEGER)\")\n\ndef claim(event_id: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Atomic insert-if-absent: exactly one worker wins, however many race on a retried event.\"\"\"\n    cur = db.execute(\"INSERT OR IGNORE INTO claims VALUES (?)\", (event_id,))\n    db.commit()\n    return cur.rowcount == 1\n\ndef apply_if_newer(doc_id: str, body: str, version: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Version-guarded upsert: an older event arriving late cannot overwrite newer state.\"\"\"\n    db.execute(\"INSERT OR IGNORE INTO docs VALUES (?, ?, -1)\", (doc_id, body))\n    cur = db.execute(\"UPDATE docs SET body = ?, version = ? WHERE id = ? AND version < ?\",\n                     (body, version, doc_id, version))\n    db.commit()\n    return cur.rowcount == 1\n\nprint(claim(\"Ev123\"), claim(\"Ev123\"))          # True False: second delivery does not post twice\nprint(apply_if_newer(\"PROJ-7\", \"v2 text\", 2))  # True\nprint(apply_if_newer(\"PROJ-7\", \"v1 text\", 1))  # False: late, older event discarded\n```\n\n### Worked numbers: polling vs webhooks plus reconciliation\n200 tenants × 50 containers, illustrative 20,000 real changes a day.\n\n| Design | API calls per day | Typical staleness |\n|---|---|---|\n| Poll every 5 minutes | 200 × 50 × 288 ≈ 2.9 million | about 2.5 minutes on average |\n| Poll every minute | ≈ 14.4 million | about 30 seconds |\n| Webhooks + one re-fetch per change | ≈ 20,000 | seconds |\n| Plus hourly reconciliation per container | + 200 × 50 × 24 = 240,000 | missed events bounded to 1 hour |\n\n- **For an event-triggered agent**, the difference is user-visible: a support agent drafting within seconds\n  of assignment versus an average 2.5-minute wait on 5-minute polling.","patterns":["**Durable enqueue before the 2xx.** Write the verified event to a durable queue or an outbox table and only then acknowledge; with Kafka, a producer configured with `acks=all` is the usual choice. Monitor enqueue latency against the sender's timeout and receiver error rate.","**One agent run per event, keyed by event ID.** Create the run record with an atomic insert keyed by the event ID, and claim each external side effect (reply, ticket update) the same way. Monitor duplicate-run rejections and side effects per event (should be at most one).","**Subscription renewal with a horizon alert.** A scheduled job renews expiring subscriptions (Graph, for example) well before expiry and re-creates any that were removed. Monitor each tenant's subscription expiry horizon and alert when it falls below one renewal interval.","**Lifecycle notifications trigger catch-up.** Set a lifecycle notification URL on Graph subscriptions and treat \"missed\" or \"reauthorization required\" as a signal to reauthorize and run reconciliation now. Monitor lifecycle events per tenant.","**Dead-letter queue with replay.** After a bounded number of worker attempts, move the event to a DLQ with the error, and give operators a replay command. Monitor DLQ depth and age of the oldest message.","**Dual secrets during rotation.** Accept signatures from the current and previous signing secret for a short overlap, then retire the old one. Monitor signature failures by tenant, which spike when a rotation goes wrong."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** \"When a ticket lands in the AI queue, draft a reply\" and \"when a Slack\n  thread mentions the bot, answer\" are webhook-triggered agent runs. The same events keep the agent's index\n  fresh. Get delivery semantics wrong and the agent replies twice, acts on forged events or answers from\n  stale data.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** A small receiver in front of a durable queue, workers that start\n  agent runs or re-index documents, the dedup and claim table that makes agent side effects happen once,\n  and a reconciliation job behind it all.\n- **What you control.** What the handler does before 2xx (verify and enqueue only), how an agent run is\n  keyed to an event so retries do not start a second run, whether payload text ever reaches the model\n  (re-fetch instead), and the reconciliation interval.\n- **What you can skip.** Each vendor's full event catalogue and HMAC theory. Know raw-body verification,\n  fast ack, idempotent consumers, re-fetch and reconciliation.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Ticket triage agents.** A Jira or ServiceNow event starts one agent run that drafts a reply or labels the\n  ticket within seconds.\n- **Slack and Teams bots.** Mentions arrive as events; the agent answers once per event and ignores its own\n  messages.\n- **Fresh RAG indexes.** Document-changed events trigger re-fetch and re-embedding, with hourly\n  reconciliation bounding misses.\n- **Workflow agents on business events.** A Stripe or CRM event (payment failed, deal closed) kicks off a\n  follow-up agent with an exactly-once side effect.","mini_project":"**Exactly-once Slack reply agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** build a Slack bot whose agent answers each mention exactly once, even under retries.\n- **Steps:** create a Slack app with the Events API; write a FastAPI receiver that verifies the signature on\n  raw bytes, enqueues by `event_id` and returns 200; in a worker, start a run keyed by event ID, re-fetch the\n  thread, call your LLM and claim the reply before posting; add a 5-second sleep in the handler once to force\n  Slack retries.\n- **Done when:** with forced retries the thread shows one reply, logs show the duplicate deliveries rejected,\n  the bot never answers its own messages, and a forged request with a bad signature gets 401."},"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant S as Source system\n    participant H as Webhook handler\n    participant Q as Durable queue\n    participant W as Worker or agent run\n    participant X as Index\n    S->>H: POST event (signed raw body)\n    Note over H: Verify HMAC on raw bytes, tenant from secret\n    H->>Q: Enqueue by event ID\n    H->>S: 200 within timeout\n    Q->>W: Deliver (maybe twice)\n    W->>S: Re-fetch current object\n    W->>X: Upsert by key, then mark event done\n    Note over W,X: Periodic reconciliation catches missed events","caption":"The handler only verifies and enqueues; all real work, including agent runs, happens in a worker that\nre-fetches state, and a reconciler covers what never arrives."},"interview":[{"q":"What delivery guarantees do webhooks give, and how does that shape the receiver?","a":"- **At-least-once, unordered, best-effort:** retries create duplicates, events can arrive out of order, and\n  some are lost for good.\n- **So the receiver** verifies, acks fast after a durable enqueue, processes idempotently, re-fetches current\n  state and relies on reconciliation for completeness."},{"q":"The assistant drafted the same Slack reply twice for one message. How do you debug and fix it?","a":"- **Check for retries:** `X-Slack-Retry-Num` on the second delivery usually means the handler was slow and\n  missed the timeout.\n- **Check the dedup:** a check-then-mark table lets two workers race on the same retried event.\n- **Fix:** ack after enqueue only, and claim the side effect with an atomic insert-if-absent on the event ID\n  before posting."},{"q":"When would you not use webhooks?","a":"- **Low-change sources**, where polling or delta sync is cheap and simpler.\n- **Sources without webhooks**, or customers who will not allow inbound internet traffic; use a relay or a\n  pull-based event API instead.\n- **Never as the only mechanism**, because a missed event stays wrong until the item changes again."},{"q":"How do you size the reconciliation interval?","a":"- **It is your worst-case staleness** for a missed event, so pick it from the freshness the customer needs.\n- **Cost:** tenants × containers × runs per day of incremental queries; 200 × 50 hourly is 240,000 calls a\n  day, far below 5-minute polling.\n- **Watch the catch rate:** many changes found by reconciliation means a subscription is broken, not that\n  the interval is wrong."},{"q":"How does a multi-tenant receiver know which tenant an event belongs to?","a":"- **From the credential:** the secret that verified the signature, or the stored subscription ID.\n- **Never from the body**, because a tenant field in the payload is exactly what a forger controls."},{"q":"Design a webhook-triggered support agent that drafts a reply when a ticket enters the AI queue.","a":"- **Receiver:** verify the signature, enqueue by event ID, return 2xx; no LLM work inline.\n- **Worker:** create the run keyed by event ID, re-fetch the ticket and confirm it is still in the AI queue,\n  run the agent with the ticket text marked as untrusted data, and claim the reply before posting.\n- **Safety nets:** ignore events authored by the bot, reconcile tickets in the queue that have no run, and\n  alert on runs per ticket above one."}],"tradeoffs":["**Freshness vs operational surface.** Webhooks cut lag to seconds and save API calls, but add a public endpoint, subscription lifecycle and secret management. Polling wins when changes are rare or the source has no webhooks.","**Fat vs thin payloads.** Fat payloads avoid an API call per event but can be stale or out of order; thin payloads plus re-fetch cost a call each but always apply current state, and give the agent verified input.","**Ack-before-process vs durability.** Acknowledging first keeps the sender happy but makes your queue the durability boundary; if the enqueue is not durable, an acknowledged event can be lost for good.","**Per-tenant vs shared subscriptions.** Per-tenant subscriptions isolate failures and secrets but multiply renewal jobs; one shared app-level subscription is simpler when the source supports routing by tenant ID.","**Agent per event vs batched runs.** One run per event gives the lowest latency; batching a burst of events for the same object (debouncing a few seconds) saves LLM cost and avoids several agents racing on one thread."],"pitfalls":["**Calling an LLM or indexer inside the handler.** Symptom: sender timeouts, retries, and the same document embedded or the same reply drafted twice.","**Verifying over re-serialized JSON instead of raw bytes.** Symptom: every request fails verification, after which someone disables the check.","**No reconciliation job.** Symptom: a few documents permanently stale after an outage, found only when a user complains.","**Letting subscriptions expire or get disabled after repeated failures.** Symptom: a tenant's freshness quietly drops to the reconciliation interval.","**Check-then-mark dedup for non-idempotent side effects.** Symptom: occasional double replies or double tickets under load, when two workers race on the same retried event.","**Trusting payload content as instructions or index text** without fetching from the source. Symptom: forged events inject attacker-controlled text into retrieval or straight into the agent's prompt.","**Agent replies triggering their own events.** Symptom: the bot's reply fires a new message event and the agent answers itself in a loop. Fix: ignore events authored by your own bot identity."],"when":"- **Use when** users or agents need changes within seconds and the source supports webhooks; keep a\n  reconciliation sync regardless.\n- **Prefer polling or delta sync alone** for low-change sources, sources without webhooks, or customers who\n  will not allow inbound internet traffic (then consider a relay or a pull-based event API).\n- **Measure:** end-to-end lag from source change to index or agent reply, duplicate rate, signature\n  failures, queue depth, subscription expiry horizon, and how many changes reconciliation catches that\n  webhooks missed.\n- **Interviews:** name at-least-once delivery, idempotency, fast ack and reconciliation.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes verified, fast-ack receivers, idempotent consumers keyed by event ID, re-fetch logic and subscription renewal jobs with alerting on expiry.","architect":"Decides webhook versus polling per source, where the durable queue sits, and the reconciliation cadence that bounds worst-case staleness.","fde":"Gets inbound endpoints allowed through the customer's network policy, configures subscriptions in their tenant and proves freshness with a live edit during acceptance testing."},"check":{"q":"After a two-hour outage of your queue, a customer reports that some Jira tickets edited during the\noutage still show old content in the assistant. Webhooks are back. Why, and how do you prevent it?","a":"The sender retried for a while, then gave up, or your endpoint returned errors and those events were\nnever redelivered; webhooks are not a durable log you can replay at will. Nothing will re-send those\nchanges, so they stay stale until the item changes again. Prevention is a reconciliation job: an incremental\nquery for items updated since the last confirmed watermark, run periodically and after any incident, which\nre-fetches and upserts them. Also alert on receiver error rate and queue health so outages trigger an\nimmediate catch-up sync."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should a webhook handler return 2xx quickly and process the event asynchronously?","options":["Senders reject responses that contain a body","Slow handlers hit the sender's timeout, which triggers retries and duplicate processing","Asynchronous processing makes the signature valid for longer","2xx responses guarantee exactly-once delivery"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Senders retry when they do not get a timely success; doing LLM or indexing work inline causes timeouts and duplicates. Fast ack after durable enqueue keeps delivery reliable, though still at-least-once."},{"q":"Two events for the same document arrive out of order. What handling is safest?","options":["Apply them in arrival order","Drop the second event as a duplicate","Re-fetch the current object, or compare versions and ignore older updates","Ask the sender to resend both in order"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Ordering is not guaranteed, so applying payloads as they arrive can let stale data win; fetching current state or version-checking makes the outcome independent of delivery order."},{"q":"Why keep a reconciliation sync if webhooks are working?","options":["Webhooks cannot deliver deletions","Reconciliation is required to verify signatures","Events can be lost during outages or expired subscriptions, and only a sync recovers them","Webhooks only work for the first tenant"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Push delivery gives low latency but no completeness guarantee; a periodic incremental sync bounds how long a missed change can stay wrong."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Official docs: Debezium","Official docs: PostgreSQL logical decoding and replication slots","Official docs: Microsoft Graph delta query"],"why":"- **The index is a derived copy of a moving source.** Tickets are edited, records reassigned, documents\n  deleted for legal reasons, permissions revoked. Reloading on a schedule is fine for small sources.\n- **Full reloads stop scaling.** Illustratively, a 2-million-record source where 0.5% of records change per\n  day has about 10,000 changes daily. A nightly reload re-reads and potentially re-embeds 2 million records\n  to apply them, about 200 times the necessary work, hammers source APIs, and still leaves up to 24 hours of\n  staleness.\n- **Reloads hide \"what changed?\"**, so downstream consumers cannot react to specific changes, such as\n  triggering an agent when a ticket's status flips.\n- **`updated_at > last_run` is where subtle bugs live:**\n  - hard-deleted rows never match the query;\n  - rows from transactions that commit after your run but carry an earlier timestamp are skipped forever;\n  - rows sharing the boundary timestamp are dropped or duplicated;\n  - bulk updates that bypass the application may not touch `updated_at` at all.\n- **Incremental sync and CDC get changes, deletes included, completely and in a defined order.** Delta\n  tokens move the bookkeeping to the source; log-based CDC sees every committed change, at the price of\n  operating a replication pipeline.","how":"### The spectrum of mechanisms\n| # | Mechanism | Deletes? | Cost and caveat |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Full snapshot plus diff**: reload and compare hashes | Yes | Correct, but cost scales with total size; fine for small or rarely changing sources |\n| 2 | **Watermark queries**: `WHERE updated_at > :hw` | Only with a soft-delete column or tombstone table | Needs a reliable, database-maintained (not app-maintained) modified column with an index |\n| 3 | **Source-provided change APIs** | Often reported | Tokens can expire, forcing a resync |\n| 4 | **Trigger-based CDC**: triggers write each change to a changelog table | Yes | Adds write cost to every transaction on the source |\n| 5 | **Log-based CDC**: read the write-ahead log | Yes | Every committed change, in commit order per table key, no polling load; you operate the pipeline |\n\nWatermark mitigations, each with its reason:\n- **Re-read an overlap window**, *because* transactions can commit after rows with later timestamps became visible.\n- **Page by the tuple `(updated_at, id)`**, *because* many rows can share one timestamp.\n- **Take the upper bound from the database clock**, not the worker's.\n\nSource change APIs:\n- **Examples:** Microsoft Graph delta tokens, Salesforce Change Data Capture events and\n  `getUpdated`/`getDeleted` calls, Jira and Confluence `updated` queries with JQL/CQL.\n- **Persist an opaque cursor per container**, *because* a token is only meaningful to the API that issued it.\n\nLog-based CDC sources:\n- **Logs:** PostgreSQL logical decoding via a replication slot, MySQL binlog, SQL Server CDC, Oracle redo via\n  a supported tool.\n- **Debezium** is the common open-source connector, publishing change events to Kafka with `before`, `after`\n  and `op` (`c` create, `u` update, `d` delete, `r` snapshot read).\n\n### What log-based CDC events actually contain\nThe log records what the database needs for replication, which is not always what a consumer expects:\n- **The `before` image depends on `REPLICA IDENTITY`.** With the PostgreSQL default, an update or delete\n  carries only the primary key as `before`. A consumer that needs old values (to remove a document from the\n  right shard, say) must set `REPLICA IDENTITY FULL` and pay for larger log records.\n- **Untouched TOASTed values may be placeholders.** Large out-of-line column values that an update did not\n  touch may arrive as a placeholder, so a consumer that blindly writes `after` can overwrite a document body\n  with it, and the agent will then retrieve and quote the placeholder.\n- **Transactions arrive as per-row events.** A consumer can observe a multi-row transaction half-applied\n  unless it groups by transaction ID.\n- **Schema changes flow through the same pipeline.** A renamed or retyped column changes the event shape,\n  which is why CDC deployments usually pair with a schema registry and a consumer that tolerates added fields.\n- **Delivery is at-least-once.** Every consumer is an idempotent upsert keyed by primary key, ideally guarded\n  by the log position (LSN) so a replayed older event cannot overwrite a newer one.\n\n### Snapshot, then stream\n1. **Every incremental system starts with an initial snapshot**, then follows changes.\n2. **The hand-off must not lose changes made during the snapshot.** Log-based tools record the log position\n   before snapshotting and stream from it, so overlap is resolved by idempotent upserts.\n3. **Avoid hours-long snapshot transactions.** A one-shot snapshot of a large table can hold a long-running\n   read transaction on the source for hours.\n4. **Use incremental snapshots.** Debezium reads the table in key-ordered chunks interleaved with the\n   stream, using watermarks in the log to resolve conflicts between a chunk and concurrent changes (the\n   approach described in Netflix's DBLog work), so a table can be re-snapshotted without pausing the stream.\n\n### Applying changes downstream\n- **Upsert by primary key; apply deletes as deletes** (remove chunks and embeddings).\n- **Make application idempotent**, *because* every mechanism above can redeliver after a crash.\n- **Hash-gate re-embedding**, *because* many updates touch only metadata, such as a status or assignee, not\n  the text. Update metadata in place; re-embed only when the text changed.\n- **Route permission changes through the same path**, or the index serves revoked content.\n- **Store `source_updated_at` and `indexed_at` on every chunk**, *because* the agent's retrieval tool can\n  then tell the model how old a fact is.\n\n### Freshness the agent can see\n- **Index lag is a fact the agent should know.** Return each hit's source modified time and the current\n  sync lag with retrieval results.\n- **Fall back to a live read for volatile fields.** If the question is about current status and the lag\n  exceeds the field's tolerance, the agent calls a live lookup tool instead of trusting the index.\n- **Read-your-writes for agent actions.** When an agent updates a record through a tool, the index will not\n  show it until the next sync or CDC event, so the agent should trust its own tool result, not a re-retrieval.\n\n### Operating it\n- **Persist cursors transactionally with the applied changes** (or after them, with idempotent replays).\n- **Monitor lag:** for watermarks, now minus high-water mark; for CDC, consumer offset behind the log head.\n- **Treat slot lag as a production alert.** A stalled PostgreSQL replication slot makes the server retain\n  WAL, which can fill the source's disk.\n- **Cap retained WAL where you can.** PostgreSQL 13+ offers `max_slot_wal_keep_size`, at the price of\n  invalidating a slot that falls too far behind, which then forces a re-snapshot.","example":"### Watermark sync with overlap, keyset paging and hash-gated re-embedding\nA PostgreSQL table with soft deletes. `conn` is a psycopg connection; `index` is your vector store.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib\nfrom datetime import timedelta\n\nOVERLAP = timedelta(minutes=10)  # illustrative; must exceed your longest write transaction\n\ndef sync_once(conn, state: dict, index, page: int = 500) -> None:\n    hi = conn.execute(\"SELECT clock_timestamp()\").fetchone()[0]  # DB clock; now() is frozen per txn\n    cursor = (state[\"high_water\"] - OVERLAP, \"\")              # overlap; \"\" sorts first for text ids\n    while True:\n        rows = conn.execute(\n            \"\"\"SELECT id, updated_at, deleted_at, title, body, acl FROM documents\n               WHERE (updated_at, id) > (%s, %s) AND updated_at <= %s\n               ORDER BY updated_at, id LIMIT %s\"\"\",\n            (*cursor, hi, page)).fetchall()\n        for doc_id, updated_at, deleted_at, title, body, acl in rows:\n            if deleted_at is not None:\n                index.delete(doc_id)                          # soft delete makes deletes visible\n                continue\n            digest = hashlib.sha256(body.encode()).hexdigest()\n            if index.content_hash(doc_id) == digest:\n                index.update_metadata(doc_id, title=title, acl=acl)  # no re-embedding needed\n            else:\n                index.upsert(doc_id, title=title, body=body, acl=acl, content_hash=digest)\n        if len(rows) < page:\n            break\n        cursor = (rows[-1][1], rows[-1][0])                   # keyset: last (updated_at, id)\n    state[\"high_water\"] = hi                                  # persist only after a full pass\n```\n\n- **`clock_timestamp()` rather than `now()`** matters if the connection is reused inside one open\n  transaction: `now()` returns the transaction's start time, so the upper bound would never advance.\n- **Cost at five-minute runs:** the illustrative 10,000 daily changes cost a few tens of thousands of row\n  reads (each changed row is re-read by every run whose 10-minute overlap window covers it, about three runs\n  here), plus re-embedding only the subset whose text changed. The hash gate keeps the overlap cheap.\n- **Starting id:** the `\"\"` assumes text ids; with integer or UUID keys start from 0 or the nil UUID instead.\n- **Still blind to hard deletes** that bypass `deleted_at`, which is the argument for log-based CDC or a\n  periodic full key comparison.\n\n### Applying Debezium-style change events safely\nPure Python, runnable. It guards by LSN, keeps a tombstone so a late update cannot resurrect a deleted row,\nand never writes the unchanged-TOAST placeholder into the index.\n\n```python\nPLACEHOLDER = \"__debezium_unavailable_value\"   # Debezium's default placeholder for unchanged TOAST\n\ndef apply_change(index: dict, ev: dict) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Apply one Debezium-style change event idempotently, guarded by log position (LSN).\"\"\"\n    op, lsn = ev[\"op\"], ev[\"source\"][\"lsn\"]\n    key = (ev[\"after\"] or ev[\"before\"])[\"id\"]          # default replica identity: before = key only\n    current = index.get(key)\n    if current and current[\"lsn\"] >= lsn:\n        return \"skip: replayed or older event\"\n    if op == \"d\":\n        index[key] = {\"row\": None, \"lsn\": lsn}          # tombstone keeps the LSN guard working\n        return \"delete chunks and embeddings\"\n    row = dict(ev[\"after\"])\n    for col, val in row.items():\n        if val == PLACEHOLDER:                          # column untouched by this update\n            if not (current and current[\"row\"] and col in current[\"row\"]):\n                return \"re-fetch row from source\"       # never saw the value: do not write the marker\n            row[col] = current[\"row\"][col]\n    index[key] = {\"row\": row, \"lsn\": lsn}\n    return \"upsert\"\n\nidx = {}\nprint(apply_change(idx, {\"op\": \"r\", \"before\": None, \"source\": {\"lsn\": 100},\n                         \"after\": {\"id\": 7, \"status\": \"open\", \"body\": \"long text\"}}))   # upsert\nprint(apply_change(idx, {\"op\": \"u\", \"before\": {\"id\": 7}, \"source\": {\"lsn\": 140},\n                         \"after\": {\"id\": 7, \"status\": \"closed\", \"body\": PLACEHOLDER}}))  # upsert\nprint(idx[7][\"row\"][\"body\"])                                                          # long text\nprint(apply_change(idx, {\"op\": \"u\", \"before\": {\"id\": 7}, \"source\": {\"lsn\": 120},\n                         \"after\": {\"id\": 7, \"status\": \"open\", \"body\": \"old\"}}))         # skip\n```\n\n### A freshness-aware retrieval tool for the agent\nRunnable standard library: each hit carries its age, and the tool tells the agent to use a live lookup\nwhen a volatile field is staler than its tolerance.\n\n```python\nimport time\n\nSYNC_STATE = {\"tickets\": {\"high_water\": time.time() - 900}}      # last full pass 15 minutes ago\nTOLERANCE_S = {\"status\": 300, \"body\": 86_400}                     # status must be < 5 min old\nINDEX = [{\"id\": \"T-12\", \"status\": \"open\", \"body\": \"VPN drops hourly\", \"source_updated_at\": time.time() - 3600}]\n\ndef search_tickets(query: str, field_needed: str = \"body\") -> dict:\n    lag = time.time() - SYNC_STATE[\"tickets\"][\"high_water\"]\n    hits = [h for h in INDEX if query.lower() in h[\"body\"].lower()]\n    advice = None\n    if lag > TOLERANCE_S.get(field_needed, 3600):\n        advice = f\"index is {lag/60:.0f} min behind; call get_ticket_live for current {field_needed}\"\n    return {\"hits\": hits, \"index_lag_s\": round(lag), \"advice\": advice}\n\nprint(search_tickets(\"vpn\")[\"advice\"])                   # None: body tolerates a day of lag\nprint(search_tickets(\"vpn\", field_needed=\"status\")[\"advice\"])   # index is 15 min behind; call get_ticket_live ...\n```\n\n- **The model decides with facts, not guesses:** lag and age are in the tool result, so the agent can\n  caveat an answer or make the live call.\n\n### Worked incident: a stalled replication slot\nA consumer was decommissioned but its slot was left behind. Figures are illustrative.\n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| WAL generated by the source | 2 GB per hour |\n| Free disk on the source volume | 50 GB |\n| Time until the disk fills with no cap | about 25 hours |\n| With `max_slot_wal_keep_size` set to 20 GB | slot invalidated after about 10 hours; the database is safe |\n| Cost of the cap | the consumer must re-snapshot before streaming again |\n\nThe fix was a slot-lag alert at a fraction of free disk, a runbook to drop orphaned slots, and the cap as a\nlast line of defence.\n\n### Worked scenario: embedding cost for a support agent's ticket index\nIllustrative: 2 million tickets, 10,000 changed rows a day, of which about 1,500 have text changes (most\nchanges are status or assignee updates).\n\n| Strategy | Re-embeds per day | Staleness |\n|---|---|---|\n| Nightly full reload | 2,000,000 | up to 24 hours |\n| Watermark sync, no hash gate | about 30,000 (10,000 changes × about 3 overlapping runs) | up to about 5 minutes; up to 15 for late commits caught by the overlap |\n| Watermark sync with hash gate | about 1,500 | same as above |\n| Log-based CDC with hash gate | about 1,500 | seconds |","patterns":["**Alert on retained WAL per slot.** Query `pg_replication_slots` with `pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), restart_lsn)` on a schedule and page well before it threatens free disk; agree the threshold with the customer's DBA. Monitor inactive slots as well as lagging ones.","**Heartbeats for quiet databases.** On low-traffic sources, configure the connector's heartbeat (Debezium has `heartbeat.interval.ms`) so the slot's confirmed position keeps advancing. Monitor slot position age.","**Freshness metadata in every retrieval result.** Store source modified time and index time per chunk, and return them plus current sync lag from the agent's search tool. Monitor sync lag per source against the freshness target agreed for the agent's use case.","**Schema registry plus tolerant consumers.** Register change-event schemas, ignore unknown added fields, and fail loudly on removed or retyped ones you depend on. Monitor consumer deserialization errors after every source migration.","**Hash-gated re-embedding with a ratio metric.** Store a content hash per document and re-embed only when it changes. Monitor the re-embed ratio; near 1 on a metadata-heavy source means the gate is missing.","**Periodic key and hash reconciliation.** Weekly, compare primary keys and content hashes between source and index, and repair drift. Monitor drift counts; non-zero trends point at a lost-delete or cursor bug."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent that cites a closed ticket, a deleted contract or a document whose\n  permissions were revoked is failing at sync, not at reasoning. Freshness and deletes in the index set the\n  ceiling on how trustworthy retrieval-backed answers can be.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The ingestion job that feeds your vector store, the cursor or\n  replication slot it reads from, the hash gate in front of the embedding model, and the \"as of\" metadata\n  your retrieval tool can return to the agent.\n- **What you control.** Mechanism per source, the overlap window, delete and permission propagation,\n  re-embedding only on text change, and whether the agent falls back to a live fetch when a record is newer\n  than the index.\n- **What you can skip.** Running Kafka and Debezium yourself for a pilot. Start with a watermark plus soft\n  deletes, learn the failure modes below, and move to log-based CDC when the numbers demand it.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Support and IT agents over ticket databases.** CDC or watermark sync keeps status, assignee and ACLs\n  current in the index, with hash-gated re-embedding.\n- **Contract and policy assistants.** Delete propagation removes documents withdrawn for legal reasons\n  before the agent can cite them.\n- **Agents over SaaS sources.** Graph delta tokens or Salesforce Change Data Capture feed the index without\n  database access.\n- **Event-driven agents.** A CDC stream of status changes triggers follow-up agents (\"ticket reopened,\n  summarise history for the new assignee\").","mini_project":"**Fresh, delete-aware ticket index for a RAG agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** keep a small agent's ticket index current with watermark sync, deletes and hash-gated\n  re-embedding, and expose freshness to the agent.\n- **Steps:** create a local PostgreSQL `tickets` table with `updated_at` and `deleted_at`; write the\n  watermark sync with overlap and keyset paging; store a content hash and timestamps per chunk; build a\n  `search_tickets` tool that returns index lag; run a script that edits statuses, edits bodies and deletes rows.\n- **Done when:** deleted tickets vanish from search within one run, status-only edits cause zero re-embeds,\n  a row from a transaction held open for a few minutes (shorter than the overlap) still appears after it\n  commits, and the agent caveats or calls a live lookup when\n  lag exceeds the status tolerance."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    DB[(\"Source database\")] --> WAL[\"Write-ahead log\"]\n    WAL --> SL[\"Replication slot\"]\n    SL --> DZ[\"CDC connector, e.g. Debezium\"]\n    DB -.->|\"initial or incremental snapshot\"| DZ\n    DZ --> K[(\"Change topic, e.g. Kafka\")]\n    K --> C[\"Consumer\"]\n    C --> G{\"Newer LSN than stored?\"}\n    G -->|\"no\"| SK[\"Skip replay\"]\n    G -->|\"yes\"| H{\"Text hash changed?\"}\n    H -->|\"yes\"| E[\"Re-embed and upsert\"]\n    H -->|\"no\"| MD[\"Update metadata and ACL\"]\n    SL -.->|\"stalled slot retains WAL\"| DB","caption":"Snapshot first, then stream from the recorded log position; the consumer's LSN guard and hash gate keep\nreplays and metadata churn cheap before anything reaches the agent's index."},"interview":[{"q":"Compare watermark sync, source delta APIs and log-based CDC.","a":"- **Watermarks** need no infrastructure but trust `updated_at`, miss hard deletes and late commits without\n  overlap and soft deletes.\n- **Delta APIs** are the only option for SaaS sources and often report deletes, but tokens expire and\n  semantics vary per vendor.\n- **Log-based CDC** sees every committed change including deletes, at the cost of operating slots,\n  connectors, a log and schema changes."},{"q":"After a metadata-only update, some indexed documents show a strange placeholder as their body. Why?","a":"- **Unchanged TOASTed columns** can arrive in Debezium `after` images as a placeholder rather than the value.\n- **The consumer wrote `after` blindly**, replacing the real body.\n- **Fix:** keep the stored value when a column carries the placeholder, re-fetch when you never had it, or set\n  `REPLICA IDENTITY FULL` if the larger log records are acceptable."},{"q":"When is log-based CDC not worth it?","a":"- **One small table**, or a source that changes rarely: a full reload or watermark is simpler.\n- **No one to operate it:** slots, connector upgrades and schema changes are ongoing work.\n- **No database access:** for SaaS systems the vendor's delta API is the only path anyway."},{"q":"How would you size and protect a replication slot on a customer's production database?","a":"- **Estimate WAL rate** and free disk to find how long a stalled consumer can survive.\n- **Alert on retained WAL** per slot well before that, and on inactive slots.\n- **Cap it** with `max_slot_wal_keep_size` on PostgreSQL 13+, accepting that an invalidated slot forces a\n  re-snapshot."},{"q":"Your support agent told a customer a ticket was still open, but it closed ten minutes earlier. How do you fix the system?","a":"- **Measure first:** sync lag for the ticket source at that moment, and whether the close event was\n  applied, delayed or lost.\n- **Make age visible:** return source modified time and index lag with each hit, so the agent can caveat.\n- **Route volatile fields live:** for status questions beyond the lag tolerance, the agent calls a live\n  lookup tool; for the pipeline, move toward CDC or webhooks if the target is seconds."},{"q":"Embedding spend for the agent's index doubled with no change in document volume. Where do you look?","a":"- **The re-embed ratio:** if it rose toward 1, the hash gate broke or a migration started touching text\n  columns.\n- **Overlap and replays:** a wider overlap window or a reset cursor re-processes rows, harmless only when\n  hash-gated.\n- **Metadata churn:** a new bulk job updating status or ACLs is cheap with in-place metadata updates and\n  expensive without them."}],"tradeoffs":["**Watermarks: no infrastructure vs trust.** They depend on a trustworthy `updated_at` and soft deletes. Move to log-based CDC when hard deletes, bulk updates or sub-minute freshness matter.","**Log-based CDC: completeness vs operations.** It captures every committed change with minimal query load, but adds Kafka or a similar log, connector operations, schema-change handling and slot monitoring. It rarely pays off for one small table.","**Source delta APIs: the only SaaS option vs vendor variance.** They handle deletes for you, but tokens expire and semantics vary per vendor, so you still need a full-resync path.","**Wider overlap vs re-reads.** A wider window catches later-committing transactions but re-reads more rows each run; cheap with idempotent, hash-gated upserts and expensive without them.","**Fresher index vs live tool calls.** Lower sync lag lets the agent answer from retrieval alone; a staler index is cheaper to run but pushes volatile questions to live lookups that cost source API calls per step."],"pitfalls":["**`updated_at > last_run` with the worker's clock and no overlap.** Symptom: a small, steady number of records never appear in the index.","**No delete path.** Symptom: the assistant cites documents deleted from the source weeks ago.","**Re-embedding on every update.** Symptom: embedding cost tracks metadata churn, like ticket status changes, rather than content changes.","**An abandoned PostgreSQL replication slot** after decommissioning a consumer. Symptom: the source database's disk usage climbs until the customer's DBA pages you.","**Applying Debezium `after` images blindly** on a PostgreSQL source with default replica identity. Symptom: document bodies become a placeholder string after an unrelated metadata update, or deletes cannot be routed because `before` holds only the key.","**Saving the cursor before changes are applied.** Symptom: changes lost permanently after a worker crash mid-batch.","**Hiding index age from the agent.** Symptom: the agent states a ticket is \"open\" minutes after it closed, with no caveat. Fix: return lag and modified time with hits, and a live lookup tool."],"when":"- **Full reloads:** small sources and early pilots.\n- **Watermark or source delta sync:** once reload cost or staleness hurts, insisting on a delete mechanism.\n- **Log-based CDC:** when you own or can access the database, need every change including hard deletes, or\n  need low lag, and when someone will operate the pipeline.\n- **Always:** keep a periodic full reconciliation.\n- **Measure:** sync lag, changes applied per run, re-embed ratio (re-embeds over changes; a ratio near 1 on a\n  metadata-heavy source means the hash gate is missing), deletes applied, replication-slot retained WAL for\n  log-based CDC, and drift found by reconciliation (keys or hashes that differ between source and index).\n- **Interviews:** explain why naive watermarks miss changes and how CDC avoids it; that is the expected depth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements cursors, overlap windows, keyset paging, idempotent upserts with hash-gated re-embedding, and delete propagation into the vector index.","architect":"Picks the mechanism per source (reload, watermark, delta API, log-based CDC), defines freshness targets and owns the snapshot-to-stream and full-resync strategies.","fde":"Negotiates with the customer's DBA for replica access or a replication slot, confirms how deletes are recorded, and agrees on monitoring so CDC never threatens their database."},"check":{"q":"A sync uses `WHERE updated_at > :last_run` every five minutes. A few records modified during busy periods\nnever appear in the index, and deleted records never disappear. Explain both and fix them.","a":"Missing records: `updated_at` is set when the write happens (with `DEFAULT now()`, at transaction start,\neven earlier), but the row becomes visible only when its transaction commits. A long transaction can commit after the sync advanced past that timestamp, so the row is\nskipped forever; worker clock skew and shared boundary timestamps make it worse. Fix with the database clock\nfor the upper bound, an overlap window longer than the longest transaction, keyset paging on `(updated_at,\nid)`, and idempotent upserts. Deleted records: hard deletes leave no row to match. Fix with soft deletes or a\ntombstone table, or move to log-based CDC, which emits delete events, plus periodic key reconciliation."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can a watermark query on updated_at miss rows even with a correct clock?","options":["Indexes on timestamps are approximate","A row can carry a timestamp earlier than the watermark but commit and become visible after the sync ran","PostgreSQL does not store microseconds","Rows with NULL ids are skipped"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The timestamp reflects when the write happened, not when it committed. A late commit lands behind an already-advanced watermark, which is why overlap windows or log-based CDC are needed."},{"q":"What does log-based CDC capture that a soft-delete-free watermark query cannot?","options":["Rows inserted in the last minute","Hard deletes, as explicit delete events from the transaction log","Changes to columns with indexes","Rows larger than one page"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Deleted rows leave nothing to match a query, but the log records the delete, so CDC emits an event (op d in Debezium) that the consumer applies to the index."},{"q":"An index re-embeds a document whenever its row changes. Most changes are status updates. What fixes the cost?","options":["Switch to a larger embedding model","Sync less often so changes are batched","Compare a content hash and update metadata in place unless the text changed","Delete and re-insert every changed document"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Embeddings depend on text, so metadata-only changes should update filters like status or ACL without calling the embedding model; batching reduces frequency but not the wasted re-embeds."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Hohpe & Woolf, 2003 — Enterprise Integration Patterns","Official docs: Stripe API (idempotent requests)"],"why":"Take a concrete integration: your assistant ingests ServiceNow incidents into a retrieval index through\nwebhooks and writes resolution notes back through the REST API. Three things go wrong in a normal week,\nnone of them your bug.\n- **Calls fail transiently.** The instance returns 429 because your sync shares the API quota with their\n  own users, a 503 during their maintenance window, or a connection reset through their proxy. These need\n  a careful retry, because synchronised retries from every worker become a thundering herd that throttles\n  the customer's own staff, the fastest way to get an integration switched off.\n- **Outcomes are ambiguous.** A write times out after the customer's server committed it but before the\n  response reached you. Retrying creates a second resolution note; not retrying may lose the first. No\n  retry policy fixes this; only a key that lets the target recognise the repeat does.\n- **Losses are silent.** A webhook delivery fails and the platform exhausts its own retry budget; a CDC\n  connector is down for forty minutes; a record changes between two pages of a paginated pull and lands\n  on neither; a deletion produces no event. You have no record of an event you never received, so only\n  comparing the source with your copy finds the gap.\n- **Each naive fix fails on one leg.** \"Retry until success\" creates duplicates and retry storms. \"Trust\n  the event feed\" accumulates drift that surfaces weeks later as a confident answer about a closed ticket.\n  \"Re-sync everything nightly\" is expensive, re-embeds unchanged documents, and does nothing about\n  duplicated writes.\n- **Three mechanisms, three failures.** The integration is only correct with all three, and the\n  reconciliation job's repair count becomes the health metric of the whole pipeline.","how":"### Retry: classify, then back off\nNot every error should be retried, because retrying a non-retryable error wastes quota and hides a bug\nbehind a delay.\n\n| Class | Codes and cases | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Retryable | 408, 429, 5xx, connection resets, timeouts | transient; a timed-out request may nevertheless have succeeded |\n| Not retryable | 400 and 422 | the payload is wrong and will be wrong again |\n| Not retryable | 401 once a token refresh has been tried | credentials, not load |\n| Not retryable | 403 | a permission the customer must grant |\n| Not retryable | 404 on a record deleted at source | it will never come back |\n\nFor the retryable class:\n1. **Grow the delay exponentially, with jitter, and cap it.**\n2. **Honour `Retry-After`** when the platform sends it.\n3. **Bound both attempts and total elapsed time.**\n\nThree additions specific to enterprise integrations:\n- **Retry budget per integration.** For example, retries may be at most a small percentage of calls over\n  a rolling window. This stops an outage at the customer turning your workers into a load generator\n  against the instance their employees share.\n- **Circuit breaker per endpoint.** Stop calling an instance that keeps failing and probe it\n  occasionally, *because* SaaS API quotas are commonly metered per customer instance per day, so a burst\n  of failed calls during an incident can lock the customer's own integrations out until the quota resets.\n- **Reads vs writes.** Reads can be retried freely. Writes can be retried only when the target can detect\n  a repeat, which is the next mechanism. The serving module's retry lesson covers the backoff arithmetic;\n  this lesson is about which calls are safe to retry at all.\n\n### Deduplication: make at-least-once look like once\n- **Why it is unavoidable.** A sender with no acknowledgement cannot tell \"the request was lost\" from \"the\n  request was applied and the reply was lost\". It must resend (at-least-once, possible duplicates) or give\n  up (at-most-once, possible loss).\n- **Exactly once is an effect, not delivery.** No protocol removes that ambiguity over an unreliable\n  network, so you build \"exactly once\" from at-least-once delivery plus a receiver that recognises\n  repeats. Every reliable feed duplicates; the only design question is where the repeat is detected.\n\nDuplicates arrive from three directions:\n\n**1. Inbound events** (webhooks, CDC, polled changes) are delivered at least once by every reliable\nmechanism, and sometimes out of order.\n- **Key on the version.** Use the delivery's own id or, better, `(tenant, record_id, source_version)`,\n  where the version is a sequence number or the source's last-modified stamp.\n- **Commit the version with the effect.** Store the last applied version per record *in the same\n  transaction* as the effect (the inbox pattern) and reject anything whose version is already applied or\n  exceeded.\n- **Why version beats delivery id.** It handles redelivery and reordering: a stale update arriving after\n  a newer one is dropped instead of overwriting it.\n- **Scope keys per tenant,** because two customers' systems can legitimately produce the same record id.\n\nThe key also decides how long dedupe state must live:\n\n| Dedupe state | Size | Lifetime | Late replay |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Seen *delivery ids* | grows with traffic | TTL longer than the sender's redelivery window (retry schedule plus any admin \"replay events\" button) | after the TTL, applied twice |\n| *Last-applied version per record* | bounded by number of records | never expires | rejected, however late |\n\nPrefer the version store whenever the source provides a version.\n\n**2. Outbound writes** need the target to recognise a repeat. In order of preference:\n1. **An idempotency key header** where the API supports one. Payment and messaging APIs usually do; most\n   enterprise SaaS APIs do not.\n2. **An *upsert on an external id* field you control,** so a repeated write with the same external id\n   updates instead of inserting.\n3. **Your own operation ledger** as the fallback: claim an operation id with status `unknown` before\n   calling, record the remote id after, and never issue a write the ledger does not know about. The ledger\n   has a race window (a crash between the remote commit and your record), which is exactly what\n   reconciliation closes.\n\n**3. Content duplicates:** the same policy document uploaded to two SharePoint sites, or the same\nattachment on three tickets. Deduplicate by a hash of the normalised content before chunking and\nembedding, otherwise retrieval returns the same passage three times and crowds out everything else.\n\n### Reconciliation: assume the first two leak\nA scheduled job compares source and target directly and repairs the difference. It runs in tiers\n*because* full comparison is expensive:\n1. **Compare counts** per partition (records by day of last modification).\n2. **Compare the set of ids and versions** for partitions that disagree or fall inside a recent window.\n3. **Compare field-level content hashes** for ids whose versions match but which are suspected of partial\n   writes.\n\nThe diff has three classes:\n\n| Class | Meaning | Repair |\n|---|---|---|\n| Missing in target | never arrived | fetch and apply |\n| Stale in target | version behind | re-fetch |\n| Extra in target | deleted at source without an event | tombstone and remove from the index |\n\n- **Deletes need a full-id sweep.** They only show up when every id is compared, so the job keeps a slow\n  background sweep over everything alongside the frequent window over the last few days.\n- **Repairs reuse the idempotent apply path.** That makes a reconciliation run safe to retry and safe to\n  overlap with the live feed.\n- **It closes the ledger.** Every operation still marked `unknown` is resolved by querying the target for\n  its external id, then recorded or re-issued.\n- **Its numbers are the health signal.** A healthy feed repairs a handful of records per run. A rising\n  repair count means the event path is broken (an expired webhook subscription, a filter that silently\n  excludes a record type, a stopped connector), usually well before any user notices.\n- **It is the customer artefact.** The reconciliation report is what you show the customer when they ask\n  whether your copy matches their system of record.\n\n### Inside the agent's tool loop\nA plain integration has one retry layer; an agent loop has three, and every one of them repeats writes.\n1. **Adapter retry.** The tool adapter retries transient errors (408, 429, 5xx) with backoff before the\n   model ever sees them, because a model shown a raw 503 often re-plans instead of waiting.\n2. **Model retry.** When the adapter gives up, the error goes back to the model as a tool result, and the\n   model may call the same tool again with slightly different arguments. Derive the operation id from the\n   run id plus the step's intent (for example `run_id + \"create_ticket\" + incident_id`), not from the\n   argument bytes, so a reworded retry still maps to the same ledger entry.\n3. **Resume retry.** A durable-execution runtime that replays a crashed run re-executes the step after the\n   last checkpoint. The ledger must already hold the operation id, or the replay writes again.\n- **Return outcomes, not exceptions.** Tell the model \"already created, ticket INC42\" or \"outcome unknown,\n  do not retry, reconciliation will resolve it\", because a model told only \"timeout\" will try again.\n- **Non-retryable errors go to the model.** A 400 or 422 is something the model can fix (bad arguments);\n  a 403 is something a human must grant, so it becomes an approval or escalation, not a loop.\n\n### How the three fit\n| Path | Steps |\n|---|---|\n| Live | event arrives → dedupe by `(tenant, record, version)` → apply and commit the version together |\n| Write | claim a ledger entry → call with retry → record the result |\n| Audit | compare source and target on a schedule → repair through the apply path → resolve unknown ledger entries → report drift |\n\nThe first two make the system fast and mostly right; the third makes it eventually exactly right and\ntells you when the first two have failed.","example":"### The three mechanisms around one integration\nThe stores (`versions`, `records`, `ledger`) stand in for tables committed in the same transaction as the\neffect; `client` wraps the customer-side API. Each function is safe to call twice, including `reconcile`\nitself.\n\n```python\nimport random, time\n\nclass ApiError(Exception):\n    def __init__(self, status, retry_after=None): self.status, self.retry_after = status, retry_after\n\nRETRYABLE = {408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504}\n\ndef call_with_retry(fn, max_attempts=5, base=0.5, cap=30.0):\n    for attempt in range(1, max_attempts + 1):\n        try:\n            return fn()\n        except (ApiError, TimeoutError, ConnectionError) as e:\n            status = getattr(e, \"status\", None)         # None: timeout/reset, outcome unknown\n            if (status is not None and status not in RETRYABLE) or attempt == max_attempts:\n                raise                                   # 4xx: wrong request, do not loop on it\n            delay = min(cap, base * 2 ** (attempt - 1)) * random.uniform(0.5, 1.5)\n            time.sleep(getattr(e, \"retry_after\", None) or delay)  # honour Retry-After if sent\n\ndef apply_event(db, tenant, event):\n    \"\"\"Inbound: idempotent by (tenant, record id, source version); version committed with the write.\"\"\"\n    key = (tenant, event[\"record_id\"])\n    seen = db.versions.get(key)\n    if seen is not None and event[\"version\"] <= seen:\n        return \"duplicate-or-stale\"                     # redelivery or out-of-order: no-op\n    db.records[key] = event[\"payload\"]\n    db.versions[key] = event[\"version\"]\n    return \"applied\"\n\ndef write_with_ledger(db, client, tenant, op_id, payload):\n    \"\"\"Outbound: never issue a write the ledger does not know about.\"\"\"\n    if db.ledger.get(op_id) not in (None, \"unknown\", \"needs-replay\"):\n        return db.ledger[op_id]                         # retry of a completed op returns the saved result\n    db.ledger[op_id] = \"unknown\"                        # claimed before the call; survives a crash mid-call\n    # Re-issuing an \"unknown\" op is safe only because upsert-by-external-id turns a repeat into an update.\n    # Against an insert-only API, resolve \"unknown\" by lookup (as reconcile does) before any re-issue.\n    remote_id = call_with_retry(lambda: client.upsert(external_id=op_id, body=payload))\n    db.ledger[op_id] = remote_id\n    return remote_id\n\ndef reconcile(db, client, tenant, since):\n    \"\"\"Catch what retry and dedupe cannot: events that never arrived, writes with unknown outcomes.\"\"\"\n    source = {r[\"id\"]: r[\"version\"] for r in client.list_changed(since=since)}   # paginated pull\n    target = {rid: v for (t, rid), v in db.versions.items() if t == tenant}\n    missing = [rid for rid in source if rid not in target]\n    stale = [rid for rid, v in source.items() if rid in target and v > target[rid]]\n    for rid in missing + stale:\n        apply_event(db, tenant, client.fetch(rid))      # repairs reuse the idempotent path\n    for op_id, status in list(db.ledger.items()):\n        if status == \"unknown\":\n            db.ledger[op_id] = client.find_by_external_id(op_id) or \"needs-replay\"\n    return {\"missing\": len(missing), \"stale\": len(stale)}  # alert when this stops trending to zero\n```\n\nDeletes are not in this sketch: `list_changed` only sees records that still exist, so a separate full-id\nsweep (or the platform's deleted-records endpoint) is what produces the \"extra in target\" class.\n\n### Version dedupe vs delivery-id dedupe on a reordered feed\nA runnable check of why the key matters. Two updates to one ticket arrive out of order, then the first is\nredelivered.\n\n```python\ndeliveries = [  # (delivery_id, record_id, version, status)\n    (\"d2\", \"INC1\", 2, \"closed\"),\n    (\"d1\", \"INC1\", 1, \"open\"),    # older update arrives late\n    (\"d1\", \"INC1\", 1, \"open\"),    # and is redelivered\n]\n\ndef by_delivery_id(feed):\n    seen, state = set(), {}\n    for did, rid, ver, status in feed:\n        if did in seen:\n            continue\n        seen.add(did); state[rid] = status\n    return state\n\ndef by_version(feed):\n    versions, state = {}, {}\n    for did, rid, ver, status in feed:\n        if versions.get(rid, -1) >= ver:\n            continue\n        versions[rid] = ver; state[rid] = status\n    return state\n\nprint(by_delivery_id(deliveries))  # {'INC1': 'open'}   wrong: closed ticket reopened\nprint(by_version(deliveries))      # {'INC1': 'closed'} right: stale update dropped\n```\n\n### Agent scenario: one \"create ticket\" call, three retries\nA support agent calls `create_ticket` during an incident. Timeline of one run (illustrative numbers).\n\n| Event | Without ledger and external id | With ledger and upsert by external id |\n|---|---|---|\n| Call 1 times out after 30 s; the server had committed | 1 ticket exists | 1 ticket, ledger entry `unknown` |\n| Adapter retry after backoff | 2 tickets | upsert updates the same ticket |\n| Model re-plans and calls the tool again | 3 tickets | same operation id, ledger returns the saved result |\n| Worker crashes, run resumes from checkpoint | 4 tickets | ledger hit, no call made |\n| Hourly reconciliation | 4 valid-looking tickets, nothing flagged | no `unknown` entries left (any that remain are resolved by external-id lookup) |\n\nFour tickets for one incident pages four on-call engineers at the customer; the ledger version makes one.\n\n### Worked scenario: a week of repair counts\nAn hourly reconciliation over the last 3 days of changes, plus a nightly full-id sweep (illustrative\nnumbers).\n\n| Day | Repairs per hourly run | Extra in target (nightly) | Reading |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Mon | 0-3 | 1 | healthy: occasional lost webhook |\n| Tue | 0-2 | 0 | healthy |\n| Wed | 40-60 | 2 | webhook subscription expired at 09:00 |\n| Thu | 0-2 | 0 | subscription renewed, backlog repaired |\n\nThe Wednesday spike paged on-call within the hour; no user had reported a stale answer yet.","patterns":["**Retry budget plus circuit breaker per tenant.** Use when many workers share one customer instance's quota. Track retries as a fraction of calls over a rolling window and stop retrying above it; open a breaker per endpoint after repeated failures and send occasional probe calls. Monitor retry ratio, breaker state and 429 rate per tenant.","**Inbox table keyed on source version.** Use for every inbound feed. A table with primary key `(tenant, record_id)` holding the last applied version, updated in the same transaction as the write, with the write skipped when the incoming version is not newer. Monitor the duplicate-or-stale rate; a sudden jump often means the source replayed events.","**Acknowledge after the durable write.** Use for webhooks. Return 200 only once the event is committed (or durably queued), so the sender's own retries cover your crashes. Monitor sender-side failed deliveries and your queue lag.","**Tiered reconciliation with an alerted repair count.** Use for any copy of customer data. Run a frequent window over recent changes and a slow full-id sweep for deletes; emit missing, stale and extra counts per run as metrics. Alert when repairs stop trending to zero or the same ids recur.","**Ledger plus upsert for outbound writes.** Use when an agent writes into a system of record. Claim an operation id as `unknown`, write with the operation id in an external-id field, and resolve every `unknown` entry by lookup in the reconciliation run. Monitor the age of the oldest `unknown` entry.","**Operation ids derived from intent, not arguments.** Use when the model can retry a tool on its own. Build the id from the run id, the tool name and the business key (incident id, order id), so a reworded second call hits the same ledger entry. Monitor duplicate-suppressed calls per run; a high count means the model is looping and needs a better tool result message."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent loop retries by nature: the framework retries the HTTP call, the\n  model re-plans and calls the tool again, a crashed run resumes from a checkpoint. Each one is a repeat\n  write unless the tool is idempotent, and a duplicate ticket or refund is the classic agent incident.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Tool adapters that wrap customer APIs, durable-execution\n  checkpoints, webhook receivers that feed the agent's RAG index, and the scheduled jobs that keep that\n  index in step with the system of record.\n- **What you control.** Which errors the adapter retries versus returns to the model, the operation id\n  you derive per tool call, the dedupe key on inbound events, and the reconciliation window and alert.\n- **What you can skip.** Proofs about distributed consensus. Know the two-generals ambiguity, the inbox\n  and ledger patterns and the three-class diff; that is enough to build and debug agent integrations.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Agents that file tickets or update records.** A ledger plus upsert by external id keeps a timed-out\n  `create_ticket` from becoming four tickets when the adapter, the model and a resume all retry.\n- **RAG assistants over a customer's ticketing system.** Version-keyed inbound dedupe stops a late webhook\n  from reopening a closed issue in the agent's answers.\n- **Index freshness guarantees.** Hourly reconciliation with an alerted repair count is the evidence that\n  the agent's knowledge matches the system of record.\n- **Durable agent runs that resume after crashes.** The ledger makes replayed steps no-ops instead of\n  repeated side effects.","mini_project":"**Make a write tool safe under every kind of retry (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** an agent with a `create_ticket` tool against a fake API that produces exactly one ticket per\n  intent, however often it is retried.\n- **Steps:** build a fake ticket API that randomly times out after committing; wrap it in an adapter with\n  classified retries and backoff; add a ledger keyed on run id, tool and incident id plus upsert by\n  external id; run a 10-step agent loop, kill the process mid-run and resume; finally write a\n  reconciliation function that resolves every `unknown` entry.\n- **Done when:** 50 runs (one ticket intent each) with injected timeouts and crashes leave exactly 50\n  tickets, the ledger has no `unknown` entries after reconciliation, and you can show the per-run count of\n  suppressed duplicates."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Customer system\"] -->|\"webhook or CDC event\"| D{\"Version newer than stored?\"}\n    D -->|\"no\"| X[\"Drop: duplicate or stale\"]\n    D -->|\"yes\"| A[\"Apply and commit version\"]\n    A --> T[(\"Target store and index\")]\n    W[\"Outbound write\"] --> L[(\"Operation ledger\")]\n    L -->|\"claim unknown, then call\"| R[\"Call with retry and backoff\"]\n    R -->|\"upsert by external id\"| S\n    C[\"Reconciliation job\"] -->|\"list ids and versions\"| S\n    C -->|\"compare\"| T\n    C -->|\"missing or stale\"| A\n    C -->|\"resolve unknown ops\"| L","caption":"Live events feeding the agent's index and the agent's outbound tool writes are both idempotent;\nreconciliation reads the source independently and feeds its repairs back through the same apply path."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is exactly-once delivery impossible, and what do you build instead?","a":"- **The two-generals ambiguity.** A sender with no acknowledgement cannot distinguish a lost request from\n  a lost reply, so it must choose possible duplicates (resend) or possible loss (give up).\n- **So build exactly-once effects.** Use at-least-once delivery plus a receiver that recognises repeats,\n  with the dedupe key committed in the same transaction as the effect so a crash cannot separate them."},{"q":"An agent's \"create ticket\" call to a customer's ServiceNow timed out. Walk me through making the retry safe.","a":"- **Check for an idempotency key first.** If the API supports one, send the same key on the retry.\n- **Otherwise upsert on an external id.** Put your operation id in a field you control so a repeat\n  updates instead of inserting.\n- **Fallback is a ledger.** Claim the op as `unknown` before the call, record the remote id after, and let\n  reconciliation look up any op still `unknown` before anything re-issues it against an insert-only API."},{"q":"When would you skip the operation ledger?","a":"- **When duplicates are cheap.** High-volume, low-value writes such as metrics or heartbeats do not\n  justify an extra database write before every call.\n- **When the target already dedupes.** An idempotency key or upsert-by-external-id gives the same safety\n  without your own bookkeeping, though reconciliation is still needed for silent losses."},{"q":"Your reconciliation job runs hourly. How do you decide its scope and what to alert on?","a":"- **Tier the comparison:** counts per partition, then ids and versions for disagreeing or recent\n  partitions, then content hashes only where partial writes are suspected, because full comparison is\n  expensive on the customer's API.\n- **Add a slow full-id sweep,** because deletes produce no event and only show up when every id is\n  compared.\n- **Alert on repair count per run** trending away from zero, and on the same ids recurring, which means\n  the repair does not converge."},{"q":"How long must a dedupe store keep delivery ids?","a":"- **Longer than the sender's redelivery window,** which includes its retry schedule and any admin\n  \"replay events\" button; a redelivery after the TTL is applied twice.\n- **Better: store last-applied version per record.** It is bounded by the number of records, never\n  expires and rejects arbitrarily late replays."},{"q":"Your agent's write tool returns an error and the model calls it again. How do you stop duplicates?","a":"- **Make the tool idempotent below the model.** Derive the operation id from run, tool and business key,\n  claim it in the ledger, and upsert by external id, so any number of repeats produce one effect.\n- **Change what the model sees.** Return \"already done, here is the id\" or \"outcome unknown, do not\n  retry\" as the tool result, and count suppressed duplicates per run as a loop signal."}],"tradeoffs":["**Aggressive retries vs the customer's quota.** More retries raise the chance a transient failure heals itself, but they consume the shared API quota. Past a modest fraction of calls (the Google SRE book's example per-client budget is retries below 10% of requests) extra retries mostly add load to an instance that is already struggling, which is why a retry budget exists.","**Version dedupe vs source quality.** It handles reordering and redelivery but needs a trustworthy version. When the source only offers a timestamp with second resolution, two edits in the same second collapse and you must fall back to comparing content hashes.","**Reconciliation certainty vs read load.** You pay with read load on the customer's system and a second code path. A narrow frequent window plus a slow full sweep is the usual balance; widen the window only when measured drift shows the event path is unreliable.","**Ledger safety vs a write per call.** A ledger makes outbound writes safe to retry but adds a database write before every call. For high-volume, low-value writes (metrics, heartbeats) the cost is not worth it and an occasional duplicate is accepted instead."],"pitfalls":["**Acknowledging a webhook with 200 before the internal write is durable.** Symptom: the customer's delivery logs show success, the feed looks healthy, and reconciliation keeps finding the same class of record missing. Fix: acknowledge after commit.","**Deduplicating by delivery id only.** Symptom: a stale update that arrived late overwrites a newer one and a ticket flips from closed back to open in your index. Fix: key on source version.","**Retrying 4xx errors.** Symptom: a validation error loops through the full backoff schedule, burning quota and delaying every other call in the queue, with a real bug hidden behind what looks like a slow dependency. Fix: classify before retrying.","**Treating reconciliation output as a log nobody reads.** Symptom: the repair count has climbed for weeks because a webhook subscription expired, and no alert was ever set on it. Fix: alert on it.","**A delivery-id dedupe TTL shorter than the source's redelivery window.** Symptom: duplicate notes or double-applied updates that cluster after a source outage or an admin's bulk \"resend events\", exactly when redeliveries arrive late. Fix: size the TTL from the window, or switch to versions.","**Hashing the tool arguments as the operation id.** Symptom: duplicates appear only when the model retries, because its second call words the description differently. Fix: derive the id from run, tool and business key, never from free-text arguments.","**Comparing source timestamps with timestamps generated on your side** (different clocks, different time zones). Symptom: records are perpetually judged stale and re-fetched every run, so the repair count never reaches zero. Fix: store and compare the source's own version."],"when":"All three apply whenever your system holds a copy of customer data or writes into a customer system,\nwhich is almost every enterprise AI deployment: a RAG index over their documents, an agent that files\ntickets, a sync of CRM records into a feature table. Size them to the stakes.\n- **Read-only index over a wiki:** a modest retry policy, content-hash dedupe and a nightly\n  reconciliation.\n- **Agent writing into a system of record:** an operation ledger, upsert-by-external-id and a\n  reconciliation job that resolves every unknown outcome within hours.\n- **Measure:** retry rate, duplicate rate and repair count per run; the last one is the one to alert on.\n- **Interviews:** explain why exactly-once delivery is impossible, why dedupe therefore has to be keyed and\n  committed with the effect, and why reconciliation is still required afterwards.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the error classifier, the inbox check keyed on source version, the ledger around every outbound write, and the tiered reconciliation job with its repair metrics and alerts.","architect":"Decides which writes need a ledger and which may tolerate duplicates, sets retry budgets per customer instance, and owns the reconciliation SLA that the parity promise to the customer rests on.","fde":"Negotiates with the customer's admin for an external-id field, a changes endpoint and a workable API quota, and uses the reconciliation report as the evidence that the integration is correct."},"check":{"q":"A customer's Jira webhook feed into your RAG index has run for six months with retries and dedupe\nin place. A user reports that an issue closed three weeks ago still appears open in the assistant's\nanswers. How did this happen, and what do you change?","a":"Retries and dedupe only act on events your system saw. The close event almost certainly never\narrived or was lost after acknowledgement: Jira's delivery to your endpoint failed and its own retries\nwere exhausted during an outage, or your endpoint returned 200 before the write was durable and then\ncrashed. Nothing in your system records an event it never processed, so the index drifted silently. The\nfix is reconciliation: an hourly job that pulls issues updated in the last few days from Jira's search\nAPI, compares ids and `updated` versions against the index, and repairs through the same apply path,\nplus a slow full sweep that catches deletions and anything older. Alert on the repair count per run, and\nmove the webhook acknowledgement to after the durable write."},"quiz":[{"q":"An integration already retries failed calls and deduplicates events by record id and version. Why does it still need a reconciliation job?","options":["To re-run retries that exceeded the attempt cap","Because events that were never delivered leave no trace in the target, and only comparing against the source reveals them","To compress the dedupe store once the TTL expires","Because retries are only safe for reads, so writes must be replayed nightly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Retry and dedupe act on events the system received. A lost webhook, a connector outage or a delete with no event produce nothing to retry or dedupe; a source-versus-target comparison is the only mechanism that can see the gap."},{"q":"A write to the customer's API timed out. Which approach is correct?","options":["Do not retry, because the write may have succeeded and a second attempt would duplicate it","Retry immediately, because timeouts almost always mean the request never arrived","Retry only through a path the target can recognise as a repeat: an idempotency key, an upsert on an external id, or a ledger entry claimed before the call","Retry after confirming by reading the record, which guarantees exactly one write"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A timeout is ambiguous, so the retry must be recognisable as a repeat by the target. A read-then-write check still has a race window between the read and the write; a ledger plus reconciliation closes it."},{"q":"Every nightly reconciliation run reports the same 40 records as stale in the target, and each run \"repairs\" them. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The customer's API is rate-limiting the reconciliation job, so it cannot read the source","The dedupe store's TTL is too short, so old events are being re-applied","The repair is not converging, for example the diff compares source versions against timestamps generated on your side, or the apply path rejects the re-fetched records","The 40 records are duplicates in the source system"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A healthy job's repair set changes between runs and trends to zero. The same set recurring means the diff is detected but the repair never makes source and target compare equal, typically because the two sides are not compared like for like; monitor the job's repair outcome, not just its diff."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/sandbox-vs-production-environments","sources":["Humble & Farley, 2010 — Continuous Delivery","Official docs: Salesforce (sandboxes)"],"why":"- **The customer has its own environments.** Software teams separate development, staging and\n  production; enterprise integration adds a second axis the customer creates on their schedule, under\n  their rules. A Salesforce org has sandboxes copied from production at a point in time; an SAP landscape\n  moves configuration through development and quality systems before production; ServiceNow customers\n  clone production into sub-production instances. Each of your environments must bind to one of theirs.\n- **You cannot develop against production.** An agent that creates cases or sends emails would have real\n  side effects on real customers on every test run, so you build against the sandbox.\n- **But the sandbox is not production.** Its data is a copy from weeks ago, may be a subset and may be\n  masked, so prompts, retrieval settings and extraction rules tuned on it meet a different distribution.\n  Ids drift apart, integrations disabled in the sandbox silently work in production, and API quota and\n  performance differ.\n- **Both naive fixes fail.** \"Test in production with a test account\" leaks test data into production\n  records, logs and the retrieval index, and the test account's permissions never match a real user's.\n  \"Assume the sandbox is production\" ships behaviour never exercised on real data, volume or permissions.\n- **The practice.** Use the sandbox for functional correctness, side effects, permissions and schema; use\n  production-safe methods (read-only checks, shadow mode, canaries) for what only production shows; and\n  make cross-wiring impossible rather than merely discouraged, because a system reading stale sandbox data\n  behaves differently and one writing to production by mistake is an incident.","how":"### The environment matrix\nPut your environments on one axis and the customer's on the other, with exactly one binding per row:\n\n| Yours | Theirs |\n|---|---|\n| development | developer sandbox |\n| staging or UAT | the customer's UAT or full-copy sandbox |\n| production | production |\n\n- **One-to-one.** No row ever points at two of theirs, and no two rows share one.\n- **A binding is a versioned configuration artefact** holding: base URL, the *name* of the credential in\n  the secret store (never the credential itself), whether writes are allowed, which index namespace\n  ingested data lands in, and which identity provider issues tokens.\n- **Credentials are per binding** *because* a customer's sandbox typically has its own connected app, API\n  client or service account, and because a production credential that exists in a non-production secret\n  store is the cross-wiring waiting to happen.\n\n### What differs, and what to do about each\n| Dimension | How it differs | What to do |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Data** | point-in-time copies, often partial, sometimes masked | functional tests with versioned fixtures you re-create after every refresh |\n| **Identifiers** | record, picklist, user and object ids diverge | reference by stable business keys or external ids; resolve at runtime |\n| **Configuration drift** | sandbox may be ahead of or behind production | introspect the schema at startup and fail fast |\n| **Integrations and side effects** | outbound email, callouts, scheduled jobs often disabled or redirected | keep an explicit list per binding |\n| **Limits and performance** | smaller capacity, different API quotas | verify throughput on a full-copy sandbox or a production canary |\n| **Identity** | users missing or with different roles | seed sandbox users that mirror production roles |\n\nThe details behind each row:\n- **Masked data misleads tuning.** Do not tune prompts or thresholds on masked data alone: a name replaced\n  by `Contact 00123` changes what an extraction prompt sees. Evaluate on a sample of real data under the\n  customer's data agreement, or in shadow mode in production.\n- **Ids diverge even after a copy.** Some platforms keep record ids when they copy production (Salesforce\n  full and partial copies do), but anything created afterwards on either side, and every record in a\n  developer sandbox, gets its own id. A hard-coded id may work in UAT by coincidence and point at nothing,\n  or at the wrong record, in production. Resolve ids through metadata or lookups, never in configuration.\n- **Drift comes from the customer's change process.** Custom fields, validation rules and workflows move\n  through change sets, transports or update sets. Failing fast at startup beats discovering a missing\n  field as a 400 response in production.\n- **Side-effect lists prevent surprises,** so a feature that \"worked in UAT\" is not sending its first real\n  email in production.\n- **Permission-sensitive behaviour** (what the assistant may retrieve for whom) is verified with the\n  seeded users, not with an admin account.\n\n### Guardrails against cross-wiring\nOutbound:\n1. **Every outbound request carries the binding pair** in its context and in its logs.\n2. **The HTTP client checks the destination host** against the binding's allow-list and refuses anything\n   else, so a mis-set environment variable produces a loud error, not a write to production.\n3. **Non-production bindings have no path to production secrets;** production bindings reject sandbox\n   hosts, so sandbox data cannot be ingested into the production index.\n4. **Ingested records are tagged with their binding,** which is what lets you prove later that no sandbox\n   fixture sits behind a production answer.\n\nInbound needs its own guard, because the customer's side can cross-wire too:\n- **The problem.** A sandbox cloned from production inherits its configuration, including any webhook\n  subscription, outbound message or notification rule that points at *your production receiver*. After\n  the next refresh, sandbox test edits arrive at your production ingestion endpoint looking like real\n  events. An outbound host allow-list cannot see this.\n- **The fix.** Authenticate the sender per binding: a separate webhook signing secret or receiver URL per\n  customer environment, plus a check of the source instance identifier that most platforms put in the\n  payload or headers, so events from an unbound instance are rejected and counted rather than indexed.\n\n### The promotion path\n- **Same artefact, different bindings.** Your changes (prompts, mappings, flags, connector settings) move\n  development → staging → production unchanged, aligned with the customer's release calendar.\n- **Plan for sandbox refreshes.** A refresh replaces the sandbox's data and typically invalidates\n  credentials and seeded fixtures, so staging breaks on the customer's schedule unless re-seeding is\n  automated.\n- **Negotiate full-copy refreshes.** They are rare (a Salesforce full sandbox, for example, can be\n  refreshed at most once every 29 days), so a refresh is negotiated, not assumed.\n\n### What only production can show\nBehaviour on the real data distribution, real volume, real permissions and real integrations. The\nproduction-safe tools:\n- **Read-only smoke tests** after deployment.\n- **Shadow mode:** the system runs on live inputs without acting.\n- **A canary** to a small user slice with rollback criteria agreed in advance.\n\nThe sandbox proves the integration is *correct*; these prove it is *right for this customer*.\n\n### Environments for an agent\nAn agent adds several things the binding must cover, because the model decides at runtime which tools to\ncall and with what.\n- **The tool registry is per binding.** Each tool's adapter resolves its host and secret from the binding,\n  so no prompt or tool argument can redirect a call to another environment. Never let the model supply a\n  base URL or instance name.\n- **Shadow mode means write tools return a dry-run result.** The agent runs on live production inputs, read\n  tools work normally, and write tools record the proposed call (tool, arguments, target record) and\n  return \"recorded, not executed\". You compare proposed actions with what humans did.\n- **Watch how the model reacts to a dry run.** If it retries or changes plan because the write \"did not\n  happen\", the shadow trajectory stops matching a live one; return a success-shaped result and mark the\n  dry run only in the trace.\n- **Evals run against the sandbox, quality evals against real data.** Functional agent evals (does the\n  agent call `update_case` with valid fields, does it respect permissions) run in the sandbox; task-quality\n  evals need real text, gathered under the data agreement or from shadow logs.\n- **Memory and retrieval are namespaced too.** An agent's long-term memory or retrieval index written in\n  staging must never be readable by the production agent, for the same reason as the index namespace.","example":"### A guarded binding per environment\nA binding resolved from a single environment variable with no default, and a guard that fails loudly on\nany mismatch between the binding and the host it points at. `http` and `secrets` and `index` stand for\nyour HTTP client, secret-store client and retrieval index.\n\n```python\nimport os\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom urllib.parse import urlparse\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Binding:\n    ours: str              # dev | staging | prod\n    theirs: str            # the customer's environment name\n    base_url: str\n    secret_ref: str        # name in the secret store; the value never lives in config\n    writes_allowed: bool\n    index_namespace: str   # where ingested data lands; never shared across bindings\n\nBINDINGS = {\n    \"dev\":     Binding(\"dev\", \"dev-sandbox\", \"https://dev-sandbox.crm.acme.example\", \"crm/dev\", True, \"acme-dev\"),\n    \"staging\": Binding(\"staging\", \"uat-sandbox\", \"https://uat-sandbox.crm.acme.example\", \"crm/uat\", True, \"acme-uat\"),\n    \"prod\":    Binding(\"prod\", \"production\", \"https://crm.acme.example\", \"crm/prod\", True, \"acme-prod\"),\n}\nPROD_HOSTS = {\"crm.acme.example\"}\n\ndef binding() -> Binding:\n    b = BINDINGS[os.environ[\"APP_ENV\"]]       # KeyError if unset: there is no default, least of all prod\n    host = urlparse(b.base_url).hostname\n    if (host in PROD_HOSTS) != (b.ours == \"prod\"):\n        raise RuntimeError(f\"cross-wired: {b.ours} is bound to {host}\")\n    return b\n\ndef request(method: str, path: str, body=None):\n    b = binding()\n    if method != \"GET\" and not b.writes_allowed:\n        raise PermissionError(f\"writes are disabled in {b.ours}\")\n    return http(method, b.base_url + path, token=secrets.get(b.secret_ref), json=body,\n                headers={\"X-Env-Pair\": f\"{b.ours}->{b.theirs}\"})   # tags your logs (and theirs, if logged)\n\ndef ingest(record: dict):\n    b = binding()\n    index.upsert(namespace=b.index_namespace, doc={**record, \"env_pair\": f\"{b.ours}->{b.theirs}\"})\n```\n\nThe guard is deliberately dumb: it does not try to be clever about hostnames, it only refuses to run\nwhen the two facts it knows (which binding, which host) disagree.\n\n### Inbound guard: reject events from an unbound instance\nA webhook receiver that verifies a per-binding HMAC signature and the source instance id before indexing.\nThe header names and the `instance_id` field are placeholders; use whatever your platform actually sends.\n\n```python\nimport hmac, hashlib\n\nEXPECTED = {  # one entry per customer environment bound to THIS deployment\n    \"prod\": {\"secret\": b\"from-secret-store\", \"instance_id\": \"acme-prod-instance\"},\n}\nrejected = {\"bad_signature\": 0, \"unbound_instance\": 0}\n\ndef receive(env: str, raw_body: bytes, signature_hex: str, payload: dict) -> bool:\n    cfg = EXPECTED[env]\n    mac = hmac.new(cfg[\"secret\"], raw_body, hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()\n    if not hmac.compare_digest(mac, signature_hex):\n        rejected[\"bad_signature\"] += 1          # a sandbox clone signs with a different secret\n        return False\n    if payload.get(\"instance_id\") != cfg[\"instance_id\"]:\n        rejected[\"unbound_instance\"] += 1       # refreshed sandbox still pointing at prod receiver\n        return False\n    return True                                  # safe to queue for ingestion\n```\n\n### Agent shadow mode: write tools that record instead of acting\nThe same tool registry serves both modes; in shadow, write tools log the proposal and tell the model it\nwas not executed, so the run continues without side effects. `request` is the guarded client above.\n\n```python\nSHADOW = os.environ.get(\"AGENT_MODE\") == \"shadow\"\nproposals = []   # compared later with what human agents actually did\n\ndef tool_update_case(case_id: str, category: str) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Write tool exposed to the model. Host and secret come from the binding, never from arguments.\"\"\"\n    if SHADOW:\n        proposals.append({\"tool\": \"update_case\", \"case_id\": case_id, \"category\": category})\n        return {\"status\": \"recorded_not_executed\", \"case_id\": case_id}\n    return request(\"PATCH\", f\"/cases/{case_id}\", body={\"category\": category})\n\ndef tool_get_case(case_id: str) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Read tool: runs normally in shadow mode.\"\"\"\n    return request(\"GET\", f\"/cases/{case_id}\")\n\nTOOLS = {\"update_case\": tool_update_case, \"get_case\": tool_get_case}\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: what the sandbox proved and what it missed\nA ticket-triage feature (illustrative numbers) passed UAT on a partial-copy sandbox.\n\n| Check | Sandbox result | Production reality | Caught by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Field mapping and writes | pass | pass | sandbox |\n| Validation rule on category | absent in sandbox | 400 on 3% of writes | startup schema introspection |\n| Classification accuracy | 91% on masked text | 84% on real text | shadow mode, 1 week |\n| Notification email on update | disabled | would email requesters | side-effect list per binding |\n\nTwo of four problems were invisible in the sandbox by construction; shadow mode and the per-binding list\nfound them before the canary.","patterns":["**No-default environment resolution.** Use for every service and job that talks to a customer system. Read the environment from one variable and crash if it is unset, as `os.environ[\"APP_ENV\"]` does above. Monitor startup failures in CI, which surface misconfiguration before it reaches a customer.","**Host allow-list in the HTTP client.** Use wherever a write path exists. Compare the request host with the binding's allowed hosts before sending and raise on mismatch. Monitor refused-request counts by binding; any non-zero value is a configuration bug to fix, not noise.","**Automated re-seed after refresh.** Use when the customer refreshes sandboxes on a schedule. A job that re-creates fixture records, test users and the sandbox credential, triggered from the customer's refresh calendar. Monitor time to a green staging run after each refresh.","**Per-environment webhook secrets and instance checks.** Use for every inbound feed. A distinct signing secret and receiver URL per customer environment, plus a source-instance check. Monitor rejected events by reason; a spike after a refresh means a cloned subscription.","**Shadow before canary.** Use for any AI behaviour whose quality depends on real text. Run on live inputs without writing, compare with human decisions, then canary one queue with an agreed rollback threshold. Monitor agreement rate and canary error rate.","**Binding-scoped tool registry.** Use for every agent with write tools. Build the agent's tools from the resolved binding at startup, keep base URLs and instance names out of tool schemas, and tag every tool-call trace with the binding pair. Monitor traces whose host does not match the binding; the count must be zero."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Every tool call an agent makes in a test run is a real call somewhere. An\n  agent pointed at production during development creates cases and sends emails; one evaluated only on a\n  masked sandbox ships prompts tuned on the wrong text.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The tool adapter's base URL and secret reference, the vector-store\n  namespace the agent retrieves from, the eval harness that replays tasks, and the webhook receiver feeding\n  the agent's index.\n- **What you control.** One binding per environment resolved with no default, a host allow-list inside\n  every tool's HTTP client, a writes flag per binding, and shadow mode where the agent proposes actions\n  but its write tools are disabled.\n- **What you can skip.** The details of each vendor's sandbox types and refresh rules beyond what your\n  customer uses; learn the matrix, the guards and the promotion path, and look up the platform specifics.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Developing a CRM or ticketing agent.** Every tool call during development and evals goes to the\n  customer's developer sandbox through a binding that cannot reach production.\n- **Proving agent quality before go-live.** Shadow mode runs the agent on live production inputs with\n  write tools recording proposals, so you measure agreement with humans without side effects.\n- **Canary release of an agent's write tools.** One queue gets live writes with a rollback threshold\n  agreed in advance, while other queues stay in shadow.\n- **Keeping agent memory and retrieval clean.** Namespaces per binding stop sandbox fixtures from\n  appearing in a production agent's answers.","mini_project":"**Build a binding-guarded agent with a shadow mode (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** one agent codebase that runs against a fake sandbox, a fake production API and in shadow mode,\n  with cross-wiring impossible.\n- **Steps:** create two fake HTTP APIs on different local ports standing in for sandbox and production;\n  define bindings resolved from `APP_ENV` with no default, and make the guard compare host *and* port,\n  since both fakes run on localhost; build a read tool and a write tool through the guarded client; add an `AGENT_MODE=shadow` path where writes are recorded; run a 5-task agent loop in\n  each mode.\n- **Done when:** a deliberately mis-set binding fails at startup, shadow runs leave the production API\n  untouched while producing a proposals log, and every trace line shows its binding pair."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    E[\"APP_ENV, no default\"] --> B[\"Resolve binding\"]\n    B --> G{\"Host matches binding?\"}\n    G -->|\"no\"| F[\"Fail loudly\"]\n    G -->|\"yes\"| W{\"Write and writes allowed?\"}\n    W -->|\"no\"| P[\"PermissionError\"]\n    W -->|\"yes\"| C[\"Call customer env with binding secret\"]\n    C --> I[(\"Index namespace per binding\")]\n    S[\"Sandbox: correctness, schema, permissions\"] --> R[\"Release gate\"]\n    SH[\"Shadow mode on prod inputs\"] --> R\n    R --> K[\"Canary with rollback criteria\"]","caption":"Each of your environments binds to exactly one customer environment, and the guard inside every agent\ntool refuses any request whose host does not match its binding; production agent behaviour is proven only\nthrough shadow and canary."},"interview":[{"q":"Why can't you treat a customer's full-copy sandbox as equivalent to production for an AI feature?","a":"- **The data is a stale, sometimes masked snapshot.** Prompts and thresholds tuned on it meet a different\n  text distribution in production.\n- **Configuration and side effects differ.** Validation rules drift through the customer's change process,\n  and outbound email or callouts are often disabled, so behaviour that passed UAT may differ live.\n- **Limits and identity differ too,** so throughput and permission-sensitive retrieval need production-safe\n  checks such as shadow mode and a canary."},{"q":"Design the guard that stops a staging agent's tools from writing to a customer's production CRM.","a":"- **Bind each environment explicitly** with a base URL, a secret reference and a writes flag, resolved\n  from a variable with no default.\n- **Check the destination host in the client** against the binding and refuse on mismatch, so a typo\n  fails loudly before any call.\n- **Keep hosts out of tool schemas,** so neither the model nor a prompt-injected document can pass a URL\n  or instance name that redirects a call.\n- **Keep production credentials out of non-production secret stores,** so even a bypassed check has\n  nothing to authenticate with."},{"q":"After a customer refreshed their sandbox, fake records started appearing in your production index. What happened and how do you fix it?","a":"- **Diagnosis:** the sandbox cloned production's webhook subscription, which still points at your\n  production receiver, so test edits arrive looking like real events.\n- **Fix:** a signing secret and receiver URL per customer environment, plus a source instance id check,\n  with rejected events counted by reason.\n- **Clean-up:** use the binding tag on ingested records to find and delete what came from the sandbox."},{"q":"How would you run an agent with write tools in shadow mode against production?","a":"- **Same agent, same read tools, dry-run write tools.** Write tools record the proposed call and return a\n  \"recorded, not executed\" result, so the loop continues without side effects.\n- **Compare proposals with human actions** per task over a fixed period, and gate the canary on an agreed\n  agreement rate. Keep hosts bound by configuration so shadow cannot become live by a prompt."},{"q":"When is a lighter environment setup acceptable?","a":"- **Read-only pilots over an exported snapshot** have no side effects and no live credentials, so a full\n  binding matrix adds cost without removing risk.\n- **The flip point is the first connector or write path:** from then on, one wrong environment variable\n  can write to a customer's live system, so the matrix and guards become mandatory."},{"q":"What would you measure to know your environment practice is healthy?","a":"- **Time to green staging after a sandbox refresh,** which shows whether re-seeding is automated.\n- **Count of ids and settings that differ** between sandbox and production, each a latent failure.\n- **Share of behaviour verified only in sandbox** versus shadow or canary, which should fall before\n  release."}],"tradeoffs":["**Full-copy realism vs cost and exposure.** It gives the most realistic testing but costs the customer money, refreshes slowly, and holds a copy of production data that needs the same protection, which is why many customers only grant partial or developer sandboxes.","**Masking vs signal.** Masking protects PII but changes text distributions; the more a feature depends on free text (extraction, summarisation), the less a masked sandbox tells you and the more you need shadow mode on real traffic.","**Strict allow-lists vs friction.** They make cross-wiring impossible but add friction whenever the customer renames an instance or adds a sandbox; the friction is worth it wherever a write path exists.","**Per-binding credentials vs onboarding steps.** They multiply steps with the customer's admin; the alternative, one credential reused across environments, removes the only hard boundary you have."],"pitfalls":["**Hard-coding sandbox record ids in prompts, filters or fixtures.** Symptom: everything passes UAT, then production returns empty results or \"record not found\" on the first real request. Fix: resolve by business key at runtime.","**Defaulting the environment to production when the variable is unset.** Symptom: a developer's laptop or a misconfigured CI job writes into the customer's live system. Fix: no default.","**Forgetting that a sandbox refresh wipes seeded data and sandbox-only credentials.** Symptom: staging tests fail the morning after the customer's scheduled refresh with authentication errors that nobody's change explains. Fix: automate re-seeding.","**Letting sandbox-ingested documents into the production index namespace.** Symptom: a production answer cites a test fixture or a months-old version of a policy. Fix: one namespace per binding.","**Sharing one webhook receiver and signing secret across the customer's environments.** Symptom: after a sandbox refresh, records with test names or obviously fake values appear in the production index, and their source instance id is the sandbox's. Fix: per-environment secrets and instance checks.","**Letting the model choose the environment.** Symptom: a tool schema exposes an `instance` or `url` argument and a prompt-injected document steers the agent to another host. Fix: resolve hosts only from the binding, never from tool arguments.","**Treating \"works in sandbox\" as the release gate.** Symptom: the first production run hits a validation rule, an email trigger or an API limit that the sandbox did not have. Fix: shadow and canary."],"when":"- **Use the full practice** (a binding per environment, hard guards and a promotion path) for any\n  integration that touches a customer system with side effects or permissions.\n- **Start lighter** for a read-only pilot over an exported snapshot, but build the matrix the moment a\n  connector or a write path appears.\n- **Measure:** time to re-establish staging after a sandbox refresh, the number of ids or settings that\n  differ between sandbox and production (each one is a latent production failure), and the share of\n  behaviour verified only in the sandbox versus in shadow or canary.\n- **Day to day:** \"which environment is this pointed at?\" must be answerable from any log line.\n- **Interviews:** expect to be asked how you would prevent a development deployment from writing to a\n  customer's production system, and why sandbox parity cannot be assumed for an AI feature.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements per-binding configuration, the host allow-list guard, environment tagging on ingested data, and automated re-seeding of fixtures after a sandbox refresh.","architect":"Owns the environment matrix, the rule that credentials never cross bindings, and the decision of what must be verified in production through shadow mode or canary rather than in sandbox.","fde":"Negotiates sandbox type, refresh schedule and credentials with the customer's admin, and explains why masked sandbox data cannot be the only evidence that the assistant works."},"check":{"q":"A customer offers only a developer sandbox with synthetic data for your ticket-triage assistant,\nwhich writes a suggested category onto each new ticket. How do you reach a confident production release?","a":"Use the sandbox for what it can prove: schema and field mappings, write permissions, validation\nrules, the full create-read-update path and failure handling, with seeded tickets covering every\ncategory and edge case. It cannot prove classification quality or behaviour on real text and volume.\nFor that, negotiate a read-only sample of real tickets under the data agreement and build an evaluation\nset with the customer's SMEs, then run in shadow mode in production: the assistant reads live tickets and\nrecords its suggested category without writing it, and you compare against what agents chose. Release\nas a canary to one queue with writes enabled and a rollback criterion agreed in advance, with hard guards\nensuring the development binding can never reach the production host."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must record ids captured in a sandbox never be hard-coded into prompts, filters or configuration?","options":["Because sandbox ids are encrypted and cannot be read by the integration","Because ids of records and metadata created independently in each environment diverge, so a hard-coded value can resolve in UAT and point at nothing, or at the wrong record, after promotion","Because ids change every time the sandbox is queried through the API","Because production ids are longer and would overflow the prompt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Even where a copy preserves the ids that existed at copy time, anything created later on either side gets its own id. Anything resolved by id must be looked up at runtime through stable business keys or metadata, otherwise it can break on the first production call."},{"q":"Which measure most reliably prevents a non-production deployment from writing to a customer's production system?","options":["A code review checklist item reminding engineers to check the environment variable","Logging the target host on every request so mistakes can be found afterwards","A per-binding host allow-list enforced in the client, with production credentials absent from non-production secret stores, so a mismatch fails loudly before any call","Running all tests against the sandbox before each deployment"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Review and logging detect mistakes after the fact. A guard that refuses to run when binding and host disagree, combined with credentials that physically do not exist outside production, prevents the write from happening at all."},{"q":"A feature passed every UAT test in a full-copy sandbox, but its first production run sent several hundred emails to customers. What was most likely missed?","options":["The production API quota was higher, so more records were processed","Outbound email was disabled in the sandbox, and the per-binding list of side-effect differences was never written down or tested for","The sandbox's data was older, so fewer records matched","Production users had more permissions than sandbox users"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sandboxes commonly disable or redirect outbound integrations. A side effect that silently does nothing in the sandbox must be listed explicitly per binding and verified in shadow or canary mode, not inferred from a green UAT run."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags","sources":["Hodgson, 2017 — Feature Toggles (aka Feature Flags)","Official docs: OpenFeature"],"why":"- **Every customer wants something different.** The first gets the product as built. The second needs\n  answers in German, Confluence rather than SharePoint, and a refusal policy for HR questions. The third\n  is regulated and cannot use one of your model providers at all.\n- **Hard-coded checks decay.** An `if customer == \"acme\":` ships fastest but scatters policy across the\n  codebase, so nobody can answer \"what is different about Acme?\" without grepping, and every change must\n  consider every special case by hand.\n- **Per-customer branches are worse.** A security fix or model upgrade must be merged into N branches,\n  testing multiplies by N, and branches drift until some customers run code months old. It works for the\n  first three customers and becomes unmaintainable by the tenth.\n- **One configuration for everyone fails the other way,** because enterprise deals are often won on\n  exactly these differences.\n- **What survives: two separations.** *What varies per tenant* (data, resolved at runtime) is separate\n  from *the code that reads it* (one version, deployed everywhere); *configuration* (a durable statement\n  about how this tenant works) is separate from *feature flags* (a usually temporary decision about who\n  sees a code path). Differences become inspectable, testable and auditable, and change without a deploy,\n  which in an AI product is frequent because prompts, models and thresholds change far more often than\n  code.","how":"### What goes in tenant configuration\nTypical per-tenant settings in an AI product:\n- **Models:** allowed model providers and the default model.\n- **Behaviour:** prompt template version, guardrail policy and thresholds, language and tone.\n- **Data:** retrieval sources and their connector settings, data residency region.\n- **Limits:** rate limits and token budgets.\n- **Capabilities:** enabled integrations and which tools an agent may call.\n- **Secrets are referenced, never stored.** API keys and connector credentials are *referenced* by name\n  and stored in a secrets manager, because configuration is read widely, logged and diffed, and secrets\n  must not be.\n\n### Layered resolution\nConfiguration is resolved by merging layers from general to specific, with the most specific winning:\n\n```\nglobal defaults  <  plan tier  <  region / deployment  <  tenant  <  environment override\n```\n\n- **Why layers.** Most tenants differ in a handful of keys. Full configurations per tenant mean a new\n  default must be copied into every tenant record; overrides alone mean a new default reaches everyone\n  who has not opted out.\n- **Validate the resolved result** against a typed schema before use, because a typo in a key or an\n  out-of-range threshold should fail at write time in an admin tool, not at 2 a.m. inside a request.\n\n### Versioning and audit\n1. **Every change creates a new immutable version** with author, timestamp and reason.\n2. **Every request logs the configuration version** it ran under. When a customer reports answers got\n   worse on Tuesday, the first question is what changed, and a prompt version or retrieval threshold\n   changed by an admin is as likely a cause as a deploy.\n3. **Behaviour-changing config goes through the eval gate.** Run the tenant's regression suite against the\n   new version before activating it, as you would for code.\n\n### Feature flags, and their four kinds\nA flag is a runtime decision about which code path runs, evaluated against a context (tenant, user,\nenvironment, percentage bucket). Hodgson's taxonomy separates them by lifetime and owner, which is the\nuseful distinction:\n\n| Kind | Purpose | Lifetime |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Release flags** | hide unfinished or unproven work | short; removed once on for everyone |\n| **Experiment flags** | split traffic between variants (two prompts, two rerankers) | removed when the experiment concludes |\n| **Ops flags / kill switches** | let on-call turn off an expensive or misbehaving path (a costly model, an external tool) without a deploy | long-lived by design |\n| **Permission or entitlement flags** | gate features by plan or contract | effectively long-lived tenant configuration; better modelled as such |\n\nTreating them the same causes trouble: release flags never removed become permanent branching, and\nentitlements implemented as ad hoc flags get lost when someone cleans up \"old flags\".\n\n### Evaluation mechanics\n- **Serve from an in-process cache.** Lookups sit on the request path, so they come from a cache refreshed\n  by polling or streaming from the flag service. During an outage it keeps serving last-known values.\n- **The coded default must be conservative.** It is what a process uses with no value at all, typically a\n  pod started while the service was unreachable. Make it the conservative path (feature off, stricter\n  guardrail) *because* a config-service outage should degrade capability, not remove protections.\n- **Hash a stable key for percentage rollouts.** Use tenant ID, or user ID within a tenant, so a user stays\n  in the same bucket across requests, and salt it with the flag name so the same users are not first in\n  line for every rollout.\n- **Use a vendor-neutral interface.** SDK interfaces such as OpenFeature let application code evaluate\n  flags without binding to one flag vendor.\n- **Resolve once per unit of work and pin it.** An agent run or conversation that re-reads config at every\n  step can switch prompt version, model or tool list halfway through when an admin saves a change,\n  producing a run no single version explains and nobody can reproduce. Snapshot the resolved config and\n  flag decisions at the start of the request, job or conversation, carry its version in the trace, and\n  apply changes at the next unit.\n- **Kill switches are the exception to pinning.** A switch exists to stop harm now, so the tool adapter\n  checks it on every call and returns \"tool unavailable\" to the model, even inside a pinned run, because\n  waiting for long runs to finish defeats the switch.\n- **Key the cache by tenant,** *because* a cache keyed by flag name alone is a cross-tenant leak: the\n  first tenant to resolve a key sets it for everyone.\n\n### Assembling an agent from tenant config\nAt the start of each run the resolved, pinned config builds the agent, so the model never sees a tool or\nprompt the tenant is not entitled to.\n1. **Pick the prompt** by `prompt_version`, never by editing prompt text in place, so every run names the\n   exact prompt it used.\n2. **Filter the tool registry** to the tenant's enabled tools, then remove any tool whose kill switch is\n   on. A tool that is not registered cannot be called, which is stronger than telling the model not to;\n   a switch flipped mid-run is caught by the per-call check above.\n3. **Apply limits** from config: step budget, token budget per run, allowed models and providers.\n4. **Stamp the trace** with config version, prompt version, tool list and flag decisions, so an eval or\n   incident can replay the run under the same settings.\n- **Experiment flags for agents** split runs, not requests: bucket by conversation or run id so one run\n  never mixes two prompts or two tool sets.\n\n### Keeping the matrix testable\nEach independent boolean doubles the possible combinations, so you cannot test them all:\n- **Test what tenants actually run:** pull the resolved configs of live tenants into CI.\n- **Keep the number of live flags small.**\n- **Give each release flag an owner and a removal date.**","example":"### A layered resolver with validation and stable rollout buckets\nA minimal layered resolver with validation, versioning and a conservative fallback. The schema is the\ncontract; overrides are partial dictionaries.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, replace, fields\nimport hashlib\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass TenantConfig:\n    model: str = \"default-large\"\n    prompt_version: str = \"support-v7\"\n    retrieval_top_k: int = 8\n    pii_redaction: bool = True\n    allowed_providers: tuple = (\"provider-a\", \"provider-b\")\n    new_reranker_pct: int = 0           # release flag: % of users on the new reranker\n\n    def validate(self):   # explicit raises, not assert: asserts vanish under python -O\n        if not 1 <= self.retrieval_top_k <= 50: raise ValueError(\"retrieval_top_k out of range\")\n        if not 0 <= self.new_reranker_pct <= 100: raise ValueError(\"new_reranker_pct out of range\")\n        if self.model_provider() not in self.allowed_providers:\n            raise ValueError(\"model not allowed for tenant\")\n\n    def model_provider(self):\n        return \"provider-b\" if self.model.startswith(\"b-\") else \"provider-a\"\n\nLAYERS = {  # in production: rows in a versioned config store\n    \"plan:enterprise\": {\"retrieval_top_k\": 12},\n    \"tenant:acme\": {\"prompt_version\": \"support-v7-de\", \"allowed_providers\": (\"provider-a\",)},\n    \"tenant:globex\": {\"new_reranker_pct\": 25},\n}\n\ndef resolve(tenant: str, plan: str) -> TenantConfig:\n    cfg, known = TenantConfig(), {f.name for f in fields(TenantConfig)}\n    for layer in (f\"plan:{plan}\", f\"tenant:{tenant}\"):\n        override = LAYERS.get(layer, {})\n        unknown = set(override) - known\n        if unknown: raise ValueError(f\"{layer}: unknown keys {unknown}\")   # typo caught here\n        cfg = replace(cfg, **override)\n    cfg.validate()\n    return cfg\n\ndef in_rollout(flag: str, user_id: str, pct: int) -> bool:   # stable bucket per user and flag\n    return int(hashlib.sha256(f\"{flag}:{user_id}\".encode()).hexdigest(), 16) % 100 < pct\n\ncfg = resolve(\"globex\", \"enterprise\")\nprint(cfg.retrieval_top_k, in_rollout(\"new_reranker\", \"globex:u-1842\", cfg.new_reranker_pct))\n```\n\n- **What each tenant gets.** Acme gets a German prompt and a single allowed provider; Globex gets a quarter\n  of users on the new reranker; both inherit the enterprise `top_k`.\n- **Where the rollout belongs.** The percentage sits in the config only to keep the sketch short; in\n  practice it lives in the flag service with an owner and a removal date, the separation argued above.\n- **Validation as contract.** Setting `model=\"b-large\"` for Acme would fail validation before any request\n  runs, because the contract forbids that provider for that tenant.\n\n### Pinning the config for a conversation\nA runnable sketch of why snapshots matter: an admin saves a new prompt version mid-conversation, and only\nthe pinned path stays consistent.\n\n```python\nstore = {\"acme\": {\"version\": 41, \"prompt_version\": \"support-v7-de\"}}\n\ndef live_lookup(tenant):\n    return store[tenant]\n\ndef run_conversation(tenant, turns, pin, admin_edit_at):\n    snapshot = dict(live_lookup(tenant)) if pin else None\n    used = []\n    for turn in range(turns):\n        if turn == admin_edit_at:   # admin saves a change mid-run\n            store[tenant] = {\"version\": 42, \"prompt_version\": \"support-v8-de\"}\n        cfg = snapshot if pin else live_lookup(tenant)\n        used.append(cfg[\"version\"])\n    store[tenant] = {\"version\": 41, \"prompt_version\": \"support-v7-de\"}   # reset for the demo\n    return used\n\nprint(run_conversation(\"acme\", 4, pin=False, admin_edit_at=2))  # [41, 41, 42, 42] mixed run\nprint(run_conversation(\"acme\", 4, pin=True, admin_edit_at=2))   # [41, 41, 41, 41] reproducible\n```\n\n### Building a tenant's agent with a kill switch\nA sketch that turns the pinned config into the tool list the model sees. Tool names and the `KILL`\nset are illustrative; in production the kill switches come from the flag service.\n\n```python\nALL_TOOLS = {\"search_kb\": ..., \"create_ticket\": ..., \"web_search\": ..., \"refund_order\": ...}\nTENANT_TOOLS = {\"acme\": [\"search_kb\", \"create_ticket\"], \"globex\": [\"search_kb\", \"web_search\", \"refund_order\"]}\nKILL = {\"web_search\"}            # ops flag flipped by on-call: external tool misbehaving\n\ndef build_agent(tenant: str, plan: str, run_id: str) -> dict:\n    cfg = resolve(tenant, plan)                     # typed, validated, pinned for this run\n    tools = [t for t in TENANT_TOOLS.get(tenant, []) if t not in KILL]   # unknown tenant: no tools\n    return {\"run_id\": run_id, \"prompt_version\": cfg.prompt_version, \"model\": cfg.model,\n            \"tools\": tools, \"trace_attrs\": {\"tenant\": tenant, \"tools\": \",\".join(tools)}}\n\nprint(build_agent(\"globex\", \"enterprise\", \"run-77\")[\"tools\"])   # ['search_kb', 'refund_order']\n```\n\nThe model cannot call `web_search` while the switch is on, because the tool is absent from the request,\nnot merely discouraged in the prompt.\n\n### Worked scenario: rolling a new model to fifty tenants\nIllustrative plan using tenant config plus a release flag.\n\n| Wave | Tenants | Gate before enabling | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | 3 barred by contract | validation rejects the provider | never switched |\n| 1 | 10 low-risk | regression suite per tenant | all pass, enabled |\n| 2 | 35 standard | suite plus a week of feedback metrics on wave 1 | 33 pass; 2 held on a threshold tweak |\n| 3 | 2 with past complaints | shadow traffic, then suite | enabled last |\n\nAfter wave 3 the flag is removed and the new model becomes the default; the old one stays only as a kill\nswitch target.","patterns":["**Resolved-config viewer with provenance.** Use as soon as layers exist. An admin view or CLI that prints each key's resolved value and the layer that set it. Monitor how many tenants override each key; a key everyone overrides should become the default.","**Config changes through the eval gate.** Use for any key that changes model behaviour (prompt version, model, thresholds). Saving a new version triggers the tenant's regression suite, and activation waits on a pass. Monitor gate failures and time from save to activation.","**Flag hygiene with owner and expiry.** Use for every release and experiment flag. Record an owner and a removal date at creation and fail CI or open a ticket when a flag passes its date. Monitor live flag count and median flag age.","**Kill switches for expensive or external paths.** Use for costly models and third-party tools. A long-lived ops flag, default off-path when unknown, that on-call can flip without a deploy. Monitor how often it is flipped and time to recovery.","**Config version in every trace.** Use always. Stamp the pinned config version and flag decisions on each request, job or conversation span. Monitor quality metrics broken down by config version so a regression is attributable to a save, not just a deploy.","**Tool lists as entitlements, tool kill switches as ops flags.** Use for every agent with external or side-effecting tools. Store which tools a tenant may use in typed config, and give each external tool a long-lived kill switch that removes it from the registry. Monitor tool-call error rate per tool and how often each switch flips."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent's behaviour is mostly data: system prompt version, model, which\n  tools are registered, retrieval sources, guardrail thresholds and step budgets. Each customer needs a\n  different mix, and each of those changes more often than the agent's code.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The step where the agent is assembled for a run (prompt, model,\n  tool registry), the trace attributes of each run, the eval gate in front of prompt changes, and the kill\n  switch on-call uses to disable an external tool.\n- **What you control.** The typed schema of what may vary, the layering order, pinning the resolved config\n  at run start, and conservative defaults so a config outage removes tools rather than guardrails.\n- **What you can skip.** Comparing flag vendors in depth; a vendor-neutral interface and a typed table you\n  own cover most agent teams until flags multiply.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Multi-tenant support agents.** Each customer gets its own prompt version, language, retrieval sources\n  and tool list from one codebase.\n- **Rolling a new model or prompt to an agent fleet.** A release flag targeted by tenant, gated by each\n  tenant's regression suite, moves agents over in waves.\n- **Incident response on agent tools.** A kill switch, checked on every tool call, takes a failing\n  external tool away from every agent, in-flight runs included, without a deploy.\n- **Prompt A/B tests on agents.** Experiment flags bucketed by run id compare two prompts without mixing\n  them inside one run.","mini_project":"**Config-driven agent assembly with pinning (2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** one agent factory that builds a different agent per tenant from layered config and keeps each\n  run on one config version.\n- **Steps:** define a typed config with prompt version, model, tool list and step budget; add global, plan\n  and tenant layers; build the agent from the resolved config; add a kill switch for one tool; change the\n  tenant's prompt version halfway through a 6-step run and log the version at every step.\n- **Done when:** two tenants get different tools and prompts from the same code, the kill switch removes a\n  tool on the next run, and every step of the edited run logs the same config version."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Request or agent run starts\"] --> C{\"Tenant-keyed cache has value?\"}\n    C -->|\"no and service down\"| D[\"Conservative coded default\"]\n    C -->|\"yes\"| M[\"Merge: global, plan, region, tenant, env\"]\n    C -->|\"no and service up\"| S[\"Fetch layers from service\"]\n    S --> M\n    M --> V{\"Schema valid?\"}\n    V -->|\"no\"| E[\"Reject and alert\"]\n    V -->|\"yes\"| P[\"Pin snapshot and config version\"]\n    D --> P\n    P --> F[\"Evaluate flags: hash of flag and user\"]\n    F --> X[\"Run code path\"]\n    X --> T[\"Trace logs config version and flag decisions\"]","caption":"A request resolves its tenant's layered config once, validates it, pins it for the whole unit of work and\nevaluates flags against it; an unreachable service falls back to the conservative default."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between tenant configuration and a feature flag?","a":"- **Configuration is a durable statement** about how a tenant works (allowed providers, residency region,\n  prompt version), typed, versioned and audited.\n- **A flag is a usually temporary decision** about who sees a code path; release and experiment flags\n  should be deleted once their job is done.\n- **Entitlements blur the line,** so model them as configuration; ad hoc entitlement flags get lost in\n  flag clean-ups."},{"q":"A customer says their support agent got worse on Tuesday and there was no deploy. How do you investigate?","a":"- **Pull the config and prompt versions from the agent's run traces** for that tenant before and after\n  Tuesday; an admin change to a prompt version, tool list or retrieval threshold is as likely as a deploy.\n- **Diff the resolved configs,** including inherited layers, since a global default edit also changes\n  every non-overriding tenant.\n- **Re-run the tenant's regression suite** on both versions to confirm, then roll back by activating the\n  previous immutable version."},{"q":"What should a flag client do when the flag service is down, and why?","a":"- **Serve last-known values from the in-process cache,** so most processes keep their current behaviour.\n- **With no value at all, use the conservative coded default** (feature off, stricter guardrail), because\n  an outage should reduce capability, not remove protections."},{"q":"When would you not use a managed feature-flag service?","a":"- **When flags are few and long-lived.** A handful of entitlements fits a typed config table you own,\n  without adding a dependency on the request path.\n- **The service earns its place** when you run many short-lived release and experiment flags that need\n  targeting, percentage rollouts, audit and a UI for non-engineers."},{"q":"An external tool your agent uses starts returning garbage. How do you contain it across all tenants?","a":"- **Flip the tool's kill switch,** which removes it from every tenant's tool registry at the next run\n  without a deploy; runs already in progress see it too, because the adapter checks the switch on every\n  call and returns \"tool unavailable\" to the model.\n- **Confirm from traces** that tool-call counts for it drop to zero, then check task success per tenant\n  to see which agents depended on it and need a fallback."},{"q":"How do you keep the flag and config matrix testable as tenants grow?","a":"- **Test the combinations tenants actually run** by pulling live resolved configs into CI, since each\n  independent boolean doubles the theoretical matrix.\n- **Cap live flags and expire them** with an owner and removal date.\n- **Watch override counts per key** and promote universal overrides to defaults, shrinking variation."}],"tradeoffs":["**No-deploy changes vs config-caused breakage.** Runtime configuration changes tenant behaviour without a deploy, but a config change can break a tenant as surely as a code change, so it needs the same review and evaluation gate once it touches prompts, models or thresholds.","**Small override records vs computed answers.** Layered overrides let new defaults propagate, but make \"what does this tenant actually run?\" a computed answer, so you need a tool showing the resolved config and which layer set each key.","**Propagation cuts both ways.** Editing a global default changes every tenant that has not overridden it, so once a key affects model behaviour a default change needs a staged rollout (a release flag over the new default) rather than a single save.","**Decoupled release vs test matrix.** Flags decouple deploy from release and give on-call kill switches, but each live flag doubles the theoretical test matrix; the benefit flips to a cost once flags outlive their purpose.","**Managed flag service vs owned table.** A service gives targeting, audit and UI for free but adds a dependency on the request path; for a handful of long-lived entitlements a typed config table you own is simpler.","**Deep customization vs N products.** It wins deals, but past some point you are running N products; the trade flips when requests start requiring code paths no other tenant uses."],"pitfalls":["**Customer names in code** (`if tenant == \"acme\"`). Symptom: nobody can list a tenant's differences without grepping, and a refactor silently drops one customer's behaviour. Fix: move the difference into a typed config key.","**Release flags that are never removed.** Symptom: dozens of stale flags, dead code paths that still need testing, and an incident when someone flips one nobody remembers. Fix: owner and removal date.","**A fallback default that is the permissive path.** Symptom: during a flag-service outage, guardrails or redaction quietly switch off for every tenant. Fix: make the coded default the conservative path.","**Not logging the config version per request.** Symptom: a quality regression report you cannot attribute, because a prompt version changed by an admin left no trace in the trace. Fix: stamp it on every span.","**Secrets or connector credentials directly in tenant configuration.** Symptom: API keys appearing in admin UIs, config diffs and debug logs. Fix: store a secret-manager reference only.","**Re-reading configuration at every step of a long agent run or conversation.** Symptom: a trace in which the prompt version or model changes mid-run, and a bug report nobody can reproduce because no single config version produced it. Fix: pin a snapshot at run start.","**Disabling a tool by prompt instead of by registry.** Symptom: on-call adds \"do not use web search\" to the prompt during an incident and the agent still calls it on some runs. Fix: remove the tool from the request through a kill switch.","**Unvalidated free-form JSON config.** Symptom: a typoed key is silently ignored, so the tenant runs the default and nobody notices until the customer does."],"when":"- **Introduce tenant configuration** as soon as a second customer needs something the first does not, and\n  before the first customer-specific `if` lands.\n- **Type and validate from day one;** retrofitting a schema onto a pile of free-form overrides is painful.\n- **Use release flags for every risky AI change** (a new model, prompt or retriever), enabling it tenant\n  by tenant while watching each tenant's eval and feedback metrics. Use kill switches for anything\n  expensive or external.\n- **Measure:** the number of live flags, their age, and how many tenants run non-default values for each\n  key; a key that every tenant overrides should become the new default.\n- **Interviews:** expect to be asked how you would roll out a new model to fifty enterprise tenants\n  without a fork per customer.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the typed resolver, caches lookups on the request path, logs config version and flag decisions in every trace, and deletes release flags when the rollout completes.","architect":"Decides what may vary per tenant and what may not, sets the layering model, and requires config changes affecting model behaviour to pass the same eval gate as code.","fde":"Translates each customer requirement into configuration rather than custom code, and pushes back when a request would need a tenant-only code path the product team must then maintain."},"check":{"q":"You must move fifty enterprise tenants from one LLM to another. Three are contractually barred\nfrom the new provider, and two have had quality complaints in the past. How do you structure the rollout?","a":"Make the model a tenant configuration key constrained by each tenant's allowed-providers list, so\nthe three barred tenants cannot be switched even by mistake: validation rejects it. Gate the switch with\na release flag targeted by tenant. Run each tenant's regression suite against the new model before\nenabling it for them, start with low-risk tenants, and leave the two sensitive ones until last, ideally\nwith shadow traffic first. Log model and config version per request so any regression is attributable,\nkeep the old model as the conservative fallback and a kill switch, and remove the flag once every\neligible tenant is migrated."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should a feature-flag client fall back to the conservative path when the flag service is unreachable?","options":["Because the conservative path is always faster to execute","Because an outage of the config service should reduce capability, not remove protections such as guardrails","Because flag services guarantee the last value is stale after a timeout","Because percentage rollouts cannot be computed without the service"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If the default is permissive, a flag-service outage silently disables redaction or guardrails for every tenant. A conservative default turns the same outage into a temporary loss of new features instead."},{"q":"What is the main advantage of storing tenant overrides rather than a full configuration per tenant?","options":["Overrides are cheaper to store in the database","Overrides remove the need for schema validation","A new global default reaches every tenant that has not explicitly overridden that key","Overrides make the resolved configuration easier to see at a glance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With full copies, every new default must be backfilled into each tenant record. Overrides keep records small and let defaults propagate, at the cost of needing a tool to show the resolved result."},{"q":"Which flag is expected to be long-lived by design?","options":["A release flag for a new reranker","An experiment flag comparing two prompts","An ops kill switch that disables an expensive external tool","A flag hiding a half-built admin page"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Kill switches exist for incidents that can happen at any time, so they stay. Release and experiment flags should be removed once the rollout or experiment ends, or they become permanent branching."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/data-onboarding","sources":[],"why":"- **The data is the per-customer cost.** An AI product is only as good as the data it retrieves or learns\n  from, and every customer differs in systems, field meanings, quality, permission models and legal\n  constraints. Model and pipeline code are shared; the data is not, so onboarding dominates time to value.\n- **Big-bang imports fail late.** \"Get a full export, load it, see what happens\" takes weeks to arrange\n  and transfer, so the first real feedback arrives late, and then all at once at production volume: a\n  third of documents are scanned images, the \"status\" field has forty undocumented values, timestamps are\n  local time with no zone, half the records are duplicates from a past migration.\n- **No agreed \"correct\" means no sign-off.** Without acceptance criteria agreed in advance there is no\n  basis for sign-off, and the customer's first view is answers built on bad data, which damages trust\n  that is hard to rebuild.\n- **One-off onboardings repeat discovery.** The tenth customer goes through the same discovery as the\n  first, with findings in someone's notes rather than in reusable checks and mappings.\n- **A staged, gated, documented process fixes both.** Problems surface on a sample when they are cheap to\n  fix, each stage has an exit criterion the customer agrees to, and findings become reusable mappings,\n  checks and connector settings.","how":"This lesson orients the whole sequence; each stage has its own lesson in this module.\n\n| Stage | Output | Exit criterion |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Inventory and scope | source list, legal basis, permission models | in-scope slice agreed |\n| 2. Access and sample | least-privilege access, stratified sample | sample covers time, type, state |\n| 3. Profile and assess | written quality assessment | blockers known and owned |\n| 4. Map to canonical schema | recorded mapping decisions, quarantine | open questions with the data owner |\n| 5. Pilot load and SME validation | pilot tenant, reviewed answers, permission checks | SMEs accept the sample |\n| 6. Backfill, then incremental sync | full load, live change feed | counts reconcile per batch |\n| 7. Sign-off and handover | acceptance record, versioned artifacts | customer signs off |\n\n### 1. Inventory and scope\n- **List each needed source** (from discovery) with owner, system, approximate volume, change rate,\n  sensitivity classification, and how it can be reached.\n- **Scope the first subset** to the slice that serves the pilot use case, *because* onboarding everything\n  before proving value multiplies cost and approval work.\n- **Confirm the legal basis:** data processing agreement, residency constraints, whether PII may leave the\n  customer boundary, retention rules.\n- **Record each source's permission model** (document ACLs, sharing groups, record-level visibility\n  rules), because an assistant that ingests content without its permissions will show a user what the\n  source would have hidden, and permissions are much harder to retrofit than to ingest from the first\n  load.\n\n### 2. Access and a representative sample\n- **Use a dedicated, least-privilege service identity** for read access, ideally against a sandbox or\n  replica first.\n- **Sample representatively, not conveniently:** across time periods, departments, document types and\n  record states. The first 1,000 rows of a table are often the oldest and least like current data, so\n  stratify deliberately.\n\n### 3. Profile and assess quality\n- **Profile the sample:** null rates, cardinalities, value distributions, formats, duplicates, and for\n  documents the mix of born-digital and scanned files, languages and sizes.\n- **Write the assessment** of what is usable, what needs cleaning and what blocks the use case, and share\n  it with the customer.\n\n### 4. Map to the canonical schema\n- **Map source fields and value sets** to your canonical model and record each decision.\n- **Quarantine values you cannot map** rather than defaulting them, and send them to the customer's data\n  owner as questions, because only they know what `STATUS=7` means.\n\n### 5. Pilot load and SME validation\n1. **Load the sample end to end** (parse, map, chunk, embed or index) into a non-production tenant.\n2. **Have the customer's subject-matter experts check it:** are these records right, are answers grounded\n   in the right documents, is anything missing. A small golden set often starts here, *because* the same\n   experts who validate the data can write the first evaluation questions.\n3. **Validate permissions in the same pass:** log in as two or three seeded users with different roles\n   and confirm each sees only what the source shows them.\n\n### 6. Backfill, then incremental sync\n- **Run the historical load safely:** batches with throttling (the source is someone's production\n  system), checkpointing so a failure resumes rather than restarts, and count reconciliation per batch.\n- **Order the hand-over correctly.** A large backfill takes days and the source keeps changing. Start the\n  change feed only after the backfill finishes and every record edited during the backfill, after its\n  batch was read, is lost: the backfill saw the old version, the feed starts too late for the new one.\n\nThe safe sequence:\n1. **Record the change-feed position first:** a CDC log position, or a `modified_at` watermark taken\n   *before* the first batch.\n2. **Run the backfill.**\n3. **Replay changes from the recorded position.** Records touched in both arrive twice; that is safe as\n   long as the apply step is idempotent and keeps the higher source version.\n4. **Keep periodic reconciliation running,** because the backfill is a snapshot and the change feed can\n   still miss deletes and lost events.\n\n### What changes when the consumer is an agent\n- **Validate through the agent, not only the tables.** In the pilot, SMEs ask the agent real questions and\n  check both the answer and the tool calls behind it (which records it fetched, which chunks it cited),\n  because a correct table can still be retrieved wrongly.\n- **Permissions are a tool-level filter.** Every retrieval or record tool call carries the end user's\n  identity and filters on the ACL metadata ingested with each item, so the seeded-role check runs through\n  the agent's own tools.\n- **The golden set becomes the agent's regression suite.** The SME questions written in stage 5 are\n  replayed against the agent after every mapping change, backfill and prompt change.\n- **Freshness is part of acceptance.** A tool that returns a stale claim status is a wrong answer, so sync\n  lag is an acceptance metric, not an ops detail.\n\n### 7. Sign-off and handover\n- **Exit with an agreed acceptance record:** coverage (records and documents loaded versus in scope),\n  reject and quarantine counts with reasons, quality findings and their owners, sync freshness, and the\n  runbook for re-onboarding a source.\n- **Encode mappings, checks and connector config as versioned artifacts** so the next customer on the\n  same system starts from them.","example":"### Worked scenario: an insurer's claims assistant\nAn insurer wants an assistant for claims handlers over two sources: a claims system (a relational\ndatabase, roughly 2 million claim records) and a SharePoint document library (roughly 300,000 files).\nFigures are illustrative.\n\n| When | Stage | What happened |\n|---|---|---|\n| Week 1 | Inventory | scope narrowed to motor claims from the last three years: about 400,000 records and about 90,000 files; legal confirms claimant PII may be processed only in the customer's cloud region, which fixes the deployment target before any code |\n| Week 2 | Sample and profile | stratified 2,000 claims (by year, region, status) and 1,500 documents (by folder, file type); 38% of documents are scanned PDFs with no text layer, 6% are password-protected, `loss_date` is 11% null for one region, `status` has 27 distinct codes where documentation lists 9 |\n| Week 3 | Mapping and fixes | 18 status codes mapped with the claims operations lead; the remaining 9 are legacy migration codes, mapped to \"closed, legacy\"; scans routed to OCR; protected files reported back, because only the customer can supply passwords or exclude them |\n| Week 4 | Pilot | three claims handlers review 50 claim summaries and 40 question-answer pairs; adjuster notes attached to the wrong claim for one region, traced to a join on a non-unique key; fixed and re-run |\n| Weeks 5 to 6 | Backfill and sync | CDC connector started and log position recorded before the first batch; nightly batches throttled off peak hours, with per-batch count reconciliation; changes since the position applied at the end, and the version check keeps the later edit for claims edited in both paths |\n\nThe sign-off record lists coverage of 99.1% of in-scope claim records, the password-protected files\n(counted on the full library, not extrapolated from the sample's 6%) as excluded with a named owner, and\na nightly reconciliation job.\n\n### Agent scenario: the pilot review that caught the join bug\nIn the insurer pilot above, the claims agent has two tools, `search_claim_docs` and `get_claim`. The 40\nSME questions were scored by how the agent answered and which tool results it used (illustrative split).\n\n| Finding in review | Questions | Root cause | Fixed in stage |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Correct answer, correct citations | 29 | none | none |\n| Cited an adjuster note from another claim | 6 | join on a non-unique key | 4, mapping |\n| \"No information found\" | 3 | answer sat in a scan loaded before its OCR pass finished | 5, re-run pilot load |\n| Showed a document a handler should not see | 2 | folder ACL not ingested | 1, permission model |\n\nRe-run after fixes: 38 of 40 pass and zero permission leaks; the 40 questions stay as the agent's\nregression suite for every later backfill.\n\n### Stratified sampling and the watermark-first hand-over\nA runnable sketch of two stage rules: sample across strata instead of taking the first rows, and replay\nchanges from a position recorded before the backfill.\n\n```python\nimport random\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nrandom.seed(7)\nrows = [{\"id\": i, \"year\": 2018 + i * 7 // 10_000, \"status\": random.choice(\"ABCDEFG\"), \"ver\": 1}\n        for i in range(10_000)]                   # ordered by insert time, like a real table\n\ndef stratified(rows, keys, per_stratum):\n    groups = defaultdict(list)\n    for r in rows:\n        groups[tuple(r[k] for k in keys)].append(r)\n    return [r for g in groups.values() for r in random.sample(g, min(per_stratum, len(g)))]\n\nfirst_n = rows[:1000]\nsample = stratified(rows, (\"year\", \"status\"), 20)\nprint(len({(r[\"year\"], r[\"status\"]) for r in first_n}),     # 7: first 1,000 rows are all 2018\n      len({(r[\"year\"], r[\"status\"]) for r in sample}))      # 49: every year x status stratum\n\n# Hand-over: record the position BEFORE the backfill, then replay from it.\nchanges, target = [], {}\nposition = len(changes)                      # watermark taken first\nfor r in rows[:5000]:                        # backfill batch 1 read\n    target[r[\"id\"]] = r[\"ver\"]\nchanges.append({\"id\": 42, \"ver\": 2})         # edit lands mid-backfill, after its batch was read\nfor r in rows[5000:]:\n    target[r[\"id\"]] = r[\"ver\"]\nfor c in changes[position:]:                 # replay; keep the higher version\n    target[c[\"id\"]] = max(target.get(c[\"id\"], 0), c[\"ver\"])\nprint(target[42])                            # 2: the mid-backfill edit survived\n```\n\nBecause the table is ordered by insert time, the first 1,000 rows see only the oldest year. Starting the\nfeed after the backfill would have left record 42 at version 1.","patterns":["**Stage gates as commands, not notebooks.** Use when onboarding more than one customer. Each stage (sample, profile, map, pilot load) is a repeatable job with its output saved per tenant and source. Monitor time from access granted to first validated pilot load.","**Watermark-first backfill.** Use for any source that changes during the load. Record the CDC log position or a `modified_at` watermark before batch 1, backfill with checkpoints, then replay from the position through the idempotent apply. Monitor feed lag after hand-over and records whose indexed version is older than the source's.","**Throttled, off-peak batches.** Use against the customer's production database. Cap concurrent queries and batch size, schedule outside business hours, and back off on rising source latency. Monitor source response times alongside your batch throughput.","**Quarantine with an owner.** Use for unmappable values and unreadable files. Route them to a quarantine table with a reason and a named customer owner instead of defaulting them. Monitor quarantine counts and outstanding mapping questions per source.","**Seeded-role permission checks.** Use in every pilot. Two or three seeded users with different roles run the same queries and the results are compared with what the source shows them. Monitor any result a user should not see as a release blocker.","**Golden set from the pilot, replayed on every change.** Use for any agent over onboarded data. Store the SME questions with expected answers and expected source records, and run the agent against them after each mapping update or backfill. Monitor pass rate and citation accuracy per source."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A RAG agent or a tool-calling agent that reads records is only as right\n  as the index and tables behind its tools. Most \"the agent is wrong\" reports in a new deployment trace to\n  onboarding: unmapped codes, missing permissions, scanned files with no text, stale snapshots.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The ingestion jobs that fill the agent's vector store and record\n  tables, the ACL metadata the retrieval tool filters on, the golden set the agent is evaluated against,\n  and the change feed that keeps tool results fresh.\n- **What you control.** The pilot slice, the stratified sample, quarantine rules, the seeded-role checks\n  run through the agent itself, and the acceptance criteria that include agent eval scores.\n- **What you can skip.** Building a general data platform first; onboard the slice the pilot agent needs\n  and turn each stage into a reusable job as you go.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Standing up a RAG agent for a new enterprise customer.** The staged process turns their document\n  library and records into an index the agent can cite, with permissions attached.\n- **Adding a new source to an existing agent.** A new system goes through sample, profile, map and pilot\n  before its tool is registered with the agent.\n- **Agent acceptance with the customer.** The sign-off record pairs data coverage with the agent's pass\n  rate on the SME golden set.\n- **Re-onboarding after a customer migration.** Versioned mappings and checks make the second load a\n  rerun, not a new project.","mini_project":"**Onboard a messy dataset for a small retrieval agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** take a deliberately messy CSV plus a folder of documents to a validated agent with a golden\n  set.\n- **Steps:** draw a stratified sample; profile nulls and value sets; map one status field and quarantine\n  unknown codes; load the sample into a vector store with an ACL field per document; build an agent with a\n  search tool that filters by user role; write 15 questions with expected sources and run them as two\n  different roles.\n- **Done when:** a profile report, a quarantine list and a per-question table show pass or fail with the\n  cause, and neither role ever sees a document outside its ACL."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    I[\"Inventory, scope, legal basis\"] --> S[\"Stratified sample\"]\n    S --> P[\"Profile and assess\"]\n    P --> M[\"Map to canonical schema\"]\n    M -->|\"unmappable values\"| Q[\"Quarantine, ask data owner\"]\n    Q --> M\n    M --> PL[\"Pilot load, SME and permission review\"]\n    PL -->|\"defects found\"| M\n    PL --> W[\"Record change-feed position\"]\n    W --> B[\"Throttled, checkpointed backfill\"]\n    B --> R[\"Replay changes since position\"]\n    R --> O[\"Sign-off, reconciliation keeps running\"]","caption":"Each stage gates the next, and problems found at a gate loop back to mapping or the data owner instead\nof flowing into the agent's index; the pilot is reviewed through the agent, and the change-feed position\nis recorded before the backfill starts."},"interview":[{"q":"Walk me through how you would onboard a new customer's data for a RAG assistant.","a":"- **Inventory and scope first:** sources, owners, volumes, sensitivity, legal basis and permission models,\n  narrowed to the pilot slice.\n- **Then sample, profile and map** a stratified sample, quarantining what cannot be mapped and sending it\n  to the data owner.\n- **Pilot with SMEs and seeded users** to check correctness and permissions, then backfill with a\n  recorded change-feed position, replay, and reconcile; close with an agreed acceptance record."},{"q":"Why must the change-feed position be recorded before the backfill starts?","a":"- **The source keeps changing during a multi-day backfill.** A record edited after its batch was read is\n  missed by the backfill, and a feed started afterwards begins too late to see the edit.\n- **Recording first and replaying closes the gap.** Records touched in both paths arrive twice, which is\n  safe because the apply is idempotent and keeps the higher source version."},{"q":"The pilot looked clean but the full load is full of formats you never saw. What went wrong?","a":"- **The sample was convenient, not representative,** such as the first N rows (often the oldest) or one\n  folder.\n- **Fix:** re-sample stratified by time, department, document type and record state, re-profile, and\n  update the mappings before resuming the load."},{"q":"When would you skip stages of this process?","a":"- **For a second tenant on an already-mapped system,** reuse mappings and connector templates and move\n  quickly from sample to backfill.\n- **Never skip profiling,** because two customers on the same software use its fields differently, and\n  never skip permission checks."},{"q":"Pilot users say the agent is wrong on a third of questions. How do you tell a data problem from an agent problem?","a":"- **Inspect the traces per failed question:** did the right record or chunk come back from the tool, and\n  did the model use it? Wrong or missing tool results point to onboarding; right results misused point\n  to the prompt or model.\n- **Group data failures by cause** (mapping, parsing, permissions, freshness) and fix them at their stage,\n  then replay the same questions."},{"q":"What goes in the sign-off record, and how would you set the numbers?","a":"- **Coverage** of records and documents loaded versus in scope (for example 99.1% of claim records in the\n  worked scenario), with exclusions named and owned.\n- **Reject and quarantine counts with reasons,** quality findings with owners, and sync freshness.\n- **The re-onboarding runbook,** so the acceptance record is also the operating manual."}],"tradeoffs":["**Staged vs fast.** Staged onboarding finds problems on a sample when they are cheap but takes more calendar time before \"all the data\" is in. It pays off whenever the data is unfamiliar, and is overkill for a second tenant on an identical, already-mapped system.","**Narrow scope vs generality.** Narrowing to the pilot slice gets to value faster but risks a design that does not generalize; include at least one awkward source in the first slice to keep the design honest.","**SME validation vs SME time.** Experts catch meaning errors no automated check can see, but their time is scarce and expensive; ask for short, structured reviews of specific samples rather than open-ended exploration.","**Reusable artifacts vs rot.** Mappings, checks and connector templates make later customers faster, but only if kept in version control and tested; otherwise they rot into misleading defaults."],"pitfalls":["**Loading the full export before profiling a sample.** Symptom: a multi-week load followed by the discovery that a key field is unusable, and a restart. Fix: profile a sample first.","**Sampling the first N rows or the most convenient folder.** Symptom: a pilot that looks clean and a production load full of formats and states the sample never contained. Fix: stratify.","**Defaulting unmappable values to a catch-all bucket.** Symptom: answers and analytics that are quietly wrong for a whole category of records. Fix: quarantine with an owner.","**No agreed acceptance criteria.** Symptom: a sign-off meeting that turns into a debate, with the customer judging success by individual bad answers. Fix: agree the acceptance record up front.","**An unthrottled backfill against the customer's production database.** Symptom: a call from their operations team about slow screens during business hours. Fix: throttled, off-peak batches.","**Starting the incremental feed only after the backfill completes.** Symptom: records whose indexed version is older than the source's, all with source edit times inside the backfill window. Fix: record the feed position first.","**Ingesting content without its permissions.** Symptom: a pilot user sees a document or record in an answer that the source system would not have shown them. Fix: ingest ACLs with the first load.","**Validating tables but not the agent's tool results.** Symptom: every count reconciles, yet the agent cites the wrong claim's notes. Fix: review answers and the tool calls behind them in the pilot.","**Treating onboarding as finished at backfill.** Symptom: a slowly diverging index because deletes and late updates after the snapshot were never reconciled. Fix: keep reconciliation running."],"when":"- **Run every stage** for an unfamiliar system or a regulated dataset.\n- **Go faster on known systems:** a new tenant on a system you have already mapped can reuse mappings and\n  go from sample to backfill quickly, but still needs profiling, because two customers on the same\n  software use its fields differently.\n- **Measure:** time from access granted to first validated pilot load, coverage against scope, quarantine\n  and reject rates, and the number of mapping questions outstanding.\n- **Interviews and customer conversations:** the strong signal is describing onboarding as a gated\n  sequence with exit criteria rather than as \"we import their data\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the sample, profile and pilot-load tooling so each stage is a command rather than a notebook, with checkpointed backfills and per-batch count reconciliation.","architect":"Defines the canonical schema and onboarding stages, decides what is reusable across customers, and sets the acceptance criteria every onboarding must meet before production.","fde":"Runs onboarding inside the customer's environment, chases access and data-owner answers, and turns each finding into an agreed decision recorded with a named owner."},"check":{"q":"A customer offers to send a full database dump \"so you have everything\" on day one. Why might you\nstill insist on a staged onboarding, and what would you do with the dump?","a":"The dump does not remove the need for profiling, mapping and SME validation; it only changes where\nthe sample comes from. Loading it all first means finding unusable fields, unmapped codes and\nduplicates at full volume, after weeks of work, with no agreed definition of correct. I would accept the\ndump if legal and residency allow it, but pull a stratified sample from it, profile and map that, run a\npilot load reviewed by their experts, and only then backfill the rest. I would also note a dump is a\nsnapshot: I would ask exactly when it was taken (ideally a log position or transaction timestamp), start\nincremental sync from that point rather than from the day we finish loading, and keep reconciliation\nrunning, or every change made in between is silently missing and the system drifts from the source."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should the onboarding sample be stratified rather than the first N rows of each table?","options":["Stratified samples are smaller and faster to load","The first rows are often old or atypical, so a convenient sample can miss formats and states common in current data","Database exports always return rows in random order","Stratification is required by most data processing agreements"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Convenient samples are biased toward whatever the source returns first, often legacy records. Sampling across time, region, type and state surfaces the variety the production load will actually contain."},{"q":"What is the right handling of source values that cannot be mapped to the canonical schema during onboarding?","options":["Map them to the most common canonical value to keep coverage high","Drop the records silently so they do not pollute the index","Quarantine them and send them to the customer's data owner as questions","Let the LLM infer the meaning at query time"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Only the data owner knows what an undocumented code means. Defaulting or dropping makes results quietly wrong; quarantining keeps the record and makes the gap visible and assignable."},{"q":"Why does onboarding not end when the historical backfill completes?","options":["The embedding model must be retrained after each backfill","Backfills always miss a fixed percentage of records","The backfill is a snapshot, and later changes and deletes need incremental sync and reconciliation","Customers require a second full import before sign-off"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The source keeps changing after the snapshot. Without incremental sync and periodic reconciliation, the system drifts from the source and serves deleted or outdated content."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/data-ingestion-from-client-systems","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Hohpe & Woolf, 2003 — Enterprise Integration Patterns"],"why":"- **Ingestion at a client is a negotiation.** The data sits in their CRM, ERP, ticketing system or file\n  shares; their security team decides which network paths exist; their platform team decides whether you\n  may touch a database; their operations team notices if your job slows the system staff use all day.\n- **You get what they can approve,** rarely the interface you would design: often a REST API with rate\n  limits, a nightly CSV on SFTP, a read replica, or an on-premises system reachable only from an agent\n  inside their network.\n- **The naive script breaks in a characteristic order.** Call the API, paginate through everything, write\n  straight into the index on a schedule:\n  1. Rate limits return 429s and the script dies halfway, so the next run starts over.\n  2. A full nightly re-pull takes longer than the night as volume grows.\n  3. Records updated during the run are skipped or duplicated because pagination shifted under it.\n  4. Deleted records stay in the index forever, because a pull of \"what exists\" says nothing about what\n     stopped existing unless you diff it.\n  5. Raw responses were never kept, so a parser bug found a month later means re-pulling everything from\n     a source that took weeks to get access to.\n- **What the pattern buys:** ingestion that is resumable, incremental, gentle on the source, and\n  replayable without going back to the client.","how":"For the generic landing-zone and pipeline design, see the data-architecture ingestion lesson; this lesson\ncovers what changes when the source belongs to a client.\n\n### 1. Choose the channel from what the client can offer\n| Channel | Strength | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Vendor API pull** (Salesforce, ServiceNow, Zendesk, Graph for SharePoint) | the usual default: respects the vendor's permission model and is supported | rate limits, pagination quirks, per-object API shapes |\n| **Database read replica or read-only views** | fastest for bulk data, allows SQL filtering | couples you to an internal schema that can change without notice; ask for views as a stable contract |\n| **File drop** (SFTP, shared object-storage bucket) | common for legacy systems and teams that cannot grant live access; simple and auditable | batch-only, prone to format drift |\n| **Events or webhooks** | low latency | at-least-once and can be lost, so needs a periodic pull or reconciliation behind it |\n| **CDC from the database log** | inserts, updates and deletes in order, low source load | needs DBA cooperation and connector infrastructure, so usually a phase-two upgrade |\n\n### 2. Decide where the connector runs\n- **Inside the client network when needed.** If the source is only reachable there, run a small agent that\n  pushes outbound to you, *because* outbound connections usually pass security review far more easily\n  than inbound ones.\n- **Otherwise private connectivity** (private endpoints, VPN).\n- **Credentials:** a dedicated service account or OAuth client with read-only scope, stored in a secrets\n  manager and rotated.\n\n### 3. Backfill, then incremental\n- **Backfill once:** in bounded batches, off-peak, resumable per batch.\n- **Then incremental:** fetch only what changed since a **watermark**, typically a `modified_at` timestamp\n  or a sequence number from the source.\n\n### 4. Make the watermark safe\nTimestamps lie at the boundaries: several records can share the watermark's exact timestamp, clocks skew,\nand long transactions commit with earlier timestamps. So:\n1. **Query from `watermark - overlap`** and accept that some records arrive twice.\n2. **Deduplicate by `(source_id, version or content_hash)`.**\n3. **Advance the watermark only *after* the batch is durably written,** so a crash re-reads rather than\n   skips. This is the whole reason ingestion can be at-least-once without being wrong.\n\n### 5. Land raw, immutably\n- **Write each record as received** to a raw store, keyed by source system, object type, ID and content\n  hash, before any parsing or mapping.\n- **Why:** everything downstream (mapping, parsing, chunking, embedding) can be re-run from raw without\n  re-contacting the client, which matters because access is slow to get and downstream logic will change.\n- **Hash only content-bearing fields.** Many APIs return volatile fields (a computed \"last viewed\", a\n  signed download URL that expires, a request timestamp), and hashing those makes every re-read look like\n  a new version.\n- **Land permissions with content** (ACLs, sharing, owning group), because retrieval must enforce them at\n  query time and a raw zone without them cannot be replayed into a permission-aware index.\n\n### 6. Protect the source\n- **Honour rate limits and `Retry-After`,** and cap concurrency.\n- **Schedule heavy work outside business hours** and prefer replicas over primaries.\n- **Expose a client-side switch to pause ingestion.** Being a good neighbour is what keeps your access\n  approved.\n\n### 7. Catch what the channel misses\n- **Polling by `modified_at` misses hard deletes** and some permission changes.\n- **It only sees what bumps the polled field.** Salesforce, for example, updates `SystemModstamp` on\n  system and automated changes that leave `LastModifiedDate` untouched, so a sync keyed on the latter\n  misses them.\n- **Fill the gap** with the source's deleted-items endpoint or tombstones where it has one, CDC where\n  available, and otherwise a periodic ID reconciliation comparing source and target key sets.\n\n### 8. Ingest ahead, or let the agent call live\nAn agent can reach client data two ways, and most deployments use both.\n\n| Approach | Good for | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ingest into an index** (this lesson) | semantic search over documents, large history, cross-record questions | freshness lag, a second copy, permissions to mirror |\n| **Live tool call** to the client API at question time | current status of one record, actions | rate limits shared with the client's users, latency per step, no semantic search |\n\n- **Rule of thumb:** ingest what the agent searches, call live for what must be current, such as a ticket's\n  status before the agent writes to it.\n- **Live tools share the ingestion quota.** Agent tool calls and sync jobs hit the same rate limit, so\n  budget them together and give the agent's calls priority during business hours.\n- **Re-embedding comes from raw.** Changing chunking or the embedding model re-runs from the raw zone,\n  never from the client API.","example":"### Incremental pull with a safe watermark\nAn incremental pull from a paginated REST API with a safe watermark, backoff on rate limits, raw landing\nand deduplication. `api`, `raw_store` and `state` stand in for your HTTP client, object store and\ncheckpoint table.\n\n```python\nimport time, hashlib, json\nfrom datetime import datetime, timedelta\n\nOVERLAP = timedelta(minutes=10)\n\ndef get_with_backoff(api, path, params, max_tries=6):\n    for attempt in range(max_tries):\n        r = api.get(path, params=params, timeout=30)\n        if r.status_code == 429 or r.status_code >= 500:\n            ra = r.headers.get(\"Retry-After\", \"\")      # seconds, or an HTTP-date we ignore here\n            wait = float(ra) if ra.isdigit() else 2 ** attempt\n            time.sleep(min(wait, 120)); continue\n        r.raise_for_status()\n        return r.json()\n    raise RuntimeError(f\"gave up on {path} after {max_tries} tries\")\n\ndef sync_tickets(api, raw_store, state, tenant):\n    wm = datetime.fromisoformat(state.get(tenant, \"tickets_wm\", \"1970-01-01T00:00:00+00:00\"))\n    since, newest, cursor = (wm - OVERLAP).isoformat(), wm, None\n    while True:\n        params = {\"updated_since\": since, \"page_size\": 200, \"order\": \"updated_at\"}\n        if cursor: params[\"cursor\"] = cursor\n        page = get_with_backoff(api, \"/tickets\", params)\n        for rec in page[\"items\"]:\n            body = json.dumps(rec, sort_keys=True).encode()   # assumes rec has no volatile fields\n            key = f\"{tenant}/tickets/{rec['id']}/{hashlib.sha256(body).hexdigest()[:16]}.json\"\n            if not raw_store.exists(key):          # dedupe the overlap window\n                raw_store.put(key, body)\n            newest = max(newest, datetime.fromisoformat(rec[\"updated_at\"]))\n        cursor = page.get(\"next_cursor\")\n        if not cursor: break\n    state.set(tenant, \"tickets_wm\", newest.isoformat())  # advance only after all writes succeeded\n```\n\n- **Overlap re-reads are no-ops.** The run re-reads ten minutes of records each time; the content-hash key\n  makes those re-reads no-ops, and an unchanged record never produces a second raw object.\n- **The watermark comes from the data.** It is the largest `updated_at` *seen in the data*, never your own\n  clock at the end of the run, because the two clocks disagree and a watermark from your clock can jump\n  past records the source stamped slightly earlier.\n- **Sizing the overlap.** It should exceed the worst clock skew plus the longest transaction you expect at\n  the source; ten minutes is a starting value, not a derived one. Content-hash dedupe makes a generous\n  overlap cheap, so err on the large side.\n- **Crashes resume.** A crash mid-run leaves the watermark where it was, so the next run resumes.\n- **What it cannot see.** A ticket hard-deleted in the source never appears in `updated_since`, which is\n  why a separate reconciliation job compares ID sets.\n\n### Why a strict `>` with no overlap loses records\nA runnable simulation: a long transaction commits after the previous run, but with an earlier\ntimestamp than the watermark.\n\n```python\nsource = [  # (id, updated_at as minutes since midnight)\n    (\"T1\", 100), (\"T2\", 105), (\"T3\", 105),\n]\nwatermark = 105                        # run 1 saw T1, T2, T3 and stopped at 105\nsource.append((\"T4\", 104))             # long transaction commits late, stamped 104\nsource.append((\"T5\", 110))             # ordinary later edit\n\nstrict = [i for i, ts in source if ts > watermark]\noverlap = [i for i, ts in source if ts >= watermark - 10]\nalready = {\"T1\", \"T2\", \"T3\"}\n\nprint(strict)                               # ['T5']        T4 is lost forever\nprint([i for i in overlap if i not in already])  # ['T4', 'T5'] overlap catches it\n```\n\nThe overlap also re-reads T1 to T3; in the real pipeline the content-hash key turns those into no-ops.\n\n### Agent scenario: a stale answer traced to the sync\nA support agent tells a user that ticket INC-2207 is \"awaiting customer\" though it closed two hours ago.\nTrace of the investigation (illustrative numbers).\n\n| Question | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| What did the agent retrieve? | the chunk for INC-2207 indexed at 06:00, status \"awaiting customer\" |\n| When did the source change? | closed and stamped 09:12, inside a long transaction that committed at 09:20 |\n| Did the hourly syncs see it? | no: the 09:15 run had already set the watermark to 09:14 from other records, and the 10:15 run queried strictly `updated_at > 09:14` |\n| Fix for freshness | query from the watermark minus an overlap, and add a live `get_ticket_status` tool the agent calls before stating a status |\n| Fix for trust | show the chunk's indexed-at time in the tool result so the model can say how current it is |\n\n### Worked scenario: choosing channels for one client\nA client with three sources and different constraints (illustrative).\n\n| Source | What the client could approve | Channel chosen | Delete handling |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ServiceNow tickets | API service account, shared quota | API pull with watermark overlap | nightly ID reconciliation |\n| On-premises ERP | no inbound connections | agent inside their network, outbound push | CDC requested for phase two |\n| Policy PDFs | nightly export only | SFTP file drop, validated per file | full listing diff per drop |","patterns":["**Overlap watermark with content-hash dedupe.** Use for every polling sync. Query from the watermark minus an overlap larger than skew plus the longest transaction, key raw objects by ID and content hash, advance after writes. Monitor duplicate-skip counts; if they equal the whole overlap window every run, volatile fields are leaking into the hash.","**Outbound-only agent for on-premises sources.** Use when security forbids inbound connections. A small connector inside the client network pushes over a monitored outbound channel with read-only credentials. Monitor agent heartbeat, version and push lag, since you cannot log in freely.","**Source-protection controls.** Use against any production system. Cap concurrency, honour `Retry-After`, schedule backfills off-peak, and give the client a pause switch. Monitor 429 rate per tenant and the source's latency during your runs.","**Validate file drops before loading.** Use for SFTP or bucket exports. Check expected columns and order, row counts against the previous drop and an end-of-file marker or manifest, and quarantine failures. Monitor missed nights and rejected files.","**ID-set reconciliation for deletes.** Use whenever the channel cannot report deletes. Periodically list source IDs, diff with target IDs and tombstone the difference through to the index. Monitor tombstones per run and raw-versus-target counts.","**Freshness metadata in every retrieval result.** Use for agents that state facts from indexed records. Store the source's `updated_at` and your indexed-at time on each chunk and return both in the tool result, so the agent can prefer a live tool call when data is old. Monitor index lag p95 per source."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Ingestion decides what the agent can know and how stale it is. A missed\n  update means the agent confidently cites an old ticket status; a missed delete means it cites a document\n  the customer removed; missing ACLs mean it shows users what the source would hide.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The sync jobs behind the agent's vector store and record tables,\n  the raw zone you re-chunk from when you change embedding models, and the choice between pre-ingesting\n  data and letting the agent call the live API as a tool.\n- **What you control.** Channel per source, watermark overlap, what goes into the content hash, which\n  permission fields are landed, and a freshness target the agent's answers depend on.\n- **What you can skip.** Building a general streaming platform. A watermark poll with overlap, a raw zone\n  and an ID reconciliation cover most agent knowledge bases.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Knowledge bases for support and IT agents.** Ticketing and wiki content is synced incrementally into\n  the agent's vector store with ACLs attached.\n- **Re-embedding without re-pulling.** Changing the chunker or embedding model re-runs from the raw zone in\n  hours instead of weeks of rate-limited API calls.\n- **Agents over on-premises systems.** An outbound-only connector inside the client network feeds an agent\n  hosted in your cloud.\n- **Hybrid agents.** Indexed history for search plus live tools for current status, sharing one quota\n  budget.","mini_project":"**Incremental sync feeding a retrieval agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** a fake ticket API synced into a small vector index that a question-answering agent searches,\n  with freshness and deletes handled.\n- **Steps:** build a fake paginated API with `updated_at` and random 429s; write the overlap-watermark sync\n  with a content-hash raw zone; chunk and index into any local vector store; add a reconciliation that\n  tombstones deleted ids; build an agent with `search_tickets` and a live `get_ticket_status` tool; edit\n  and delete tickets between runs.\n- **Done when:** after edits and deletes the agent never cites a deleted ticket, states current status via\n  the live tool, and the sync survives a mid-run crash without losing or duplicating records."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    W[(\"Checkpoint: watermark\")] --> Q[\"Query updated_since = watermark - overlap\"]\n    Q --> A[\"Client API page\"]\n    A -->|\"429 or 5xx\"| B[\"Back off, honour Retry-After\"]\n    B --> A\n    A --> H{\"Content hash already landed?\"}\n    H -->|\"yes\"| N[\"Skip: overlap re-read\"]\n    H -->|\"no\"| R[(\"Immutable raw store\")]\n    R --> D[\"Downstream: map, parse, chunk, embed\"]\n    A -->|\"last page written\"| W2[\"Advance watermark to max updated_at seen\"]\n    W2 --> W\n    C[\"ID reconciliation job\"] -->|\"tombstone deletes\"| D","caption":"An incremental run reads from the watermark minus an overlap, lands raw records keyed by content hash,\nand moves the watermark only after every write succeeds before chunks reach the agent's index;\nreconciliation covers deletes separately."},"interview":[{"q":"Why land an immutable raw copy before parsing or mapping?","a":"- **Replay without the client.** Every downstream fix (mapping, parsing, chunking, embedding) can be re-run\n  from raw, which matters because access took weeks to get and downstream logic will change.\n- **The cost** is storing sensitive client data a second time, so retention, encryption and deletion\n  propagation must cover the raw zone."},{"q":"Your raw zone grows by thousands of objects every run though nobody edits records. What is wrong?","a":"- **The content hash includes volatile fields,** such as a signed download URL or a request timestamp, so\n  every overlap re-read looks like a new version.\n- **Fix:** hash only content-bearing fields and keep permissions alongside; expect duplicate skips to\n  roughly match the overlap window once fixed."},{"q":"The client offers either API access or a read replica. How do you choose?","a":"- **API pull** respects the vendor's permission model and is supported, but rate limits make large\n  backfills slow.\n- **A replica** is fastest for bulk history and allows SQL filtering, but couples you to an internal\n  schema; ask for read-only views as a stable contract.\n- **Often both:** a replica or views for the backfill and the API for incremental sync and permissions."},{"q":"Security will not open any inbound port to the on-premises system. What do you propose?","a":"- **An outbound-only agent** inside their network, on a host they control, pushing to you over a channel\n  their security team can monitor.\n- **The trade-off** is operating software you cannot log into freely, so build heartbeat, version reporting\n  and remote diagnostics in from the start."},{"q":"Should your agent read ticket data from an index or call the ticketing API live?","a":"- **Index what the agent searches:** semantic search over descriptions and comments needs ingested,\n  chunked and embedded data with permissions attached.\n- **Call live for what must be current,** such as status before the agent updates a ticket, because the\n  index lags by up to one sync interval. Budget live calls inside the same API quota as the sync."},{"q":"Your agent states a ticket status that changed two hours ago. How do you find the cause?","a":"- **Start from the trace:** which chunk the retrieval tool returned and its indexed-at time, against the\n  source's change time, so you know whether the sync or the agent is at fault.\n- **Then check the sync runs after the change:** did the query window cover the change's timestamp\n  (watermark overlap, late commits), and does the polled field move on this kind of change?\n- **Fix both layers:** an overlap sized above skew plus the longest transaction (ten minutes is a starting\n  value, not a derived one), plus a live status tool and indexed-at time in the tool result."}],"tradeoffs":["**API pull vs replica.** API pull respects the vendor's permission model and is supported, but rate limits make large backfills slow; a read replica flips the balance for bulk history when the client's DBAs will provide one.","**Polling vs events or CDC.** Watermark polling is simple and works with almost any API, but adds latency of up to one polling interval and misses deletes; events or CDC win once freshness or delete propagation becomes a requirement.","**In-network agent vs operability.** An agent inside the client network reaches on-premises systems and passes security review more easily, but you now operate software in an environment you cannot log into freely, so upgrades, monitoring and support get harder.","**Immutable raw copy vs a second copy of sensitive data.** It makes every downstream fix replayable, but stores client data twice; retention, encryption and deletion propagation must cover the raw zone too.","**File drops vs inherited failures.** They are the easiest channel to get approved, but you inherit the export job's failures, such as partial files, changed column orders and missed nights, so validate each file before loading it."],"pitfalls":["**Advancing the watermark before writes are durable.** Symptom: a small, permanent gap of records that were fetched during a crash and never re-fetched. Fix: advance after the write.","**A strict `>` comparison on a timestamp watermark with no overlap.** Symptom: occasional missing records that share a timestamp with the previous run's last record, or committed late with an earlier stamp. Fix: query from the watermark minus an overlap.","**Offset pagination over a set that changes during the run.** Symptom: records skipped or seen twice when inserts shift page boundaries. Fix: cursor or keyset pagination ordered on a stable key.","**Ignoring `Retry-After` and retrying immediately.** Symptom: the client's API gateway throttles or blocks your service account, sometimes for the whole tenant's integrations. Fix: honour it and cap concurrency.","**Hashing the whole API response, volatile fields included, for the dedupe key.** Symptom: a raw zone that grows by roughly the full overlap window on every run and \"changed\" counts that never fall, although nobody edited those records. Fix: hash only content-bearing fields.","**Writing straight to the index with no raw copy.** Symptom: a parser or mapping fix requires a full re-pull, which can take days against a rate-limited API. Fix: land raw first.","**Giving agent tool calls and sync jobs separate, uncoordinated rate budgets.** Symptom: during a backfill the agent's live lookups start failing with 429s and users see \"tool unavailable\". Fix: one quota budget per client instance with priority for interactive calls.","**Assuming polling sees deletes.** Symptom: answers citing documents or records the customer deleted weeks ago, often found by the customer. Fix: deleted-items endpoint, CDC or ID reconciliation."],"when":"- **Use for** any recurring pull from a system you do not own.\n- **Ask what the client can approve first,** not your ideal: an API with a service account is usually\n  fastest to get, a file drop is the fallback for legacy systems, and CDC is worth requesting once\n  freshness or deletes matter.\n- **Measure:** lag (source change to landed), records per run, retry and 429 rates, raw-versus-target\n  counts from reconciliation, and run duration against the window you have.\n- **Interviews:** the tell of experience is mentioning the watermark overlap, advancing the checkpoint\n  after the write, and deletes.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes connectors with cursor pagination, backoff that honours `Retry-After`, overlap watermarks and content-hash deduplication into an immutable raw zone.","architect":"Chooses the channel per source, where connectors run relative to the client network, and how raw data is retained, encrypted and deleted across the boundary.","fde":"Negotiates service accounts, network paths and off-peak windows with the client's security and platform teams, and is the first to hear when ingestion load annoys their operations staff."},"check":{"q":"Your nightly sync uses `updated_at > last_watermark` against a client API. Users report a few\nmissing tickets each week and some deleted tickets still being cited. Diagnose both and fix them.","a":"Missing tickets come from the strict comparison at the boundary: records sharing the watermark\ntimestamp, clock skew, or late-committing transactions fall behind it and are never re-read. Fix by\nquerying from the watermark minus an overlap, deduplicating by ID plus version or content hash, and\nadvancing the watermark only after writes succeed. Deleted tickets persist because a `updated_at`\nquery cannot return records that no longer exist. Fix with the API's deleted-records endpoint if it has\none, CDC if the client can provide it, or a scheduled reconciliation that diffs source and target ID sets\nand tombstones the difference through to the index."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should the watermark be advanced only after the batch has been durably written?","options":["Because APIs reject requests whose watermark is in the future","Because a crash between fetch and write would otherwise skip those records permanently","Because writing first makes the batch smaller","Because the watermark must equal the earliest record in the batch"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If the checkpoint moves first and the write then fails, the next run starts after records that were never stored. Advancing after the write makes failures re-read instead of skip."},{"q":"A client's security team will not allow any inbound connection to their on-premises database. What is the usual design?","options":["Ask for a nightly full database dump emailed to you","Use the vendor's public API from your cloud regardless of the policy","Run a connector agent inside their network that makes outbound connections to your service","Wait for them to migrate the database to the cloud"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Outbound-only agents are a common, reviewable compromise: no inbound ports open, the client controls the host, and data is pushed to you over a channel their security team can monitor."},{"q":"What does a timestamp-watermark poll fundamentally fail to capture?","options":["Records created after the last run","Records whose fields were edited in the source since the last run","Records returned on the second page of results","Records that were hard-deleted in the source"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A query for recently modified records can only return records that still exist. Hard deletes need a deleted-items endpoint, CDC, or a key-set reconciliation between source and target."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","sources":["Hohpe & Woolf, 2003 — Enterprise Integration Patterns"],"why":"- **Every client represents the same concepts differently.** One CRM's \"Account\" is another's\n  \"Customer\", dates arrive in three formats, and a status of \"Closed\" may mean won, lost or cancelled.\n- **Point-to-point puts every source in every consumer.** Say your product consumes tickets from Zendesk,\n  ServiceNow and Jira Service Management across twelve customers, feeding a retriever, a summarizer,\n  analytics and an eval harness. Read natively, the summarizer must know ServiceNow's `priority` is an\n  integer from 1 (critical) to 5, derived from impact and urgency, while Zendesk uses strings such as\n  `urgent` and `low`; the retrieval filter must know which field holds the product name in each system.\n- **That is the N × M problem.** Adding a source means editing every consumer; changing a consumer means\n  testing it against every source.\n- **\"Let the LLM cope\" fails three ways.** Dumping raw JSON into the prompt works in a demo, but:\n  1. Retrieval filters, access control and analytics are not LLM calls; they need a queryable field, so\n     they still need the mapping.\n  2. Model interpretation is inconsistent across records and model versions, so the same code can be read\n     two ways on two days.\n  3. Meaning is often not in the data: whether `STATUS=7` means \"escalated\" or \"awaiting customer\" is held\n     by the client's data owner, and no inference recovers it reliably.\n- **The canonical data model puts that knowledge in one place per source.** It is a named pattern in Hohpe\n  and Woolf's catalogue: a mapping, reviewed with the people who know the data, tested, and versioned.","how":"### Designing the canonical schema\n- **Model *your* domain,** the concepts your product reasons about (case, party, document, interaction),\n  not a union of every source's fields.\n- **Keep it small and stable,** *because* each field is a promise every mapping must keep.\n- **Keep its scope to your product.** At enterprise scale, a single company-wide canonical model tries to\n  make \"customer\" mean the same to sales, billing and support, needs every team's agreement to change, and\n  becomes the bottleneck. Domain-driven design's answer is a model per bounded context with translation at\n  the edges. Your product's schema is one such context; it need not be the client's enterprise data model,\n  and should not try.\n\nTypical choices:\n\n| Choice | Rule | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Normalized types | timestamps in UTC with zone, money as amount plus ISO currency, explicit units | consumers never guess formats |\n| Closed enumerations | documented, e.g. `status ∈ {open, pending, resolved, closed}` | downstream logic branches on them |\n| Provenance on every record | source system, source ID, source version, mapping version | any canonical value can be traced back |\n| **Extensions** bag | source fields with no canonical home | mapping is lossless; a field can be promoted later without re-ingesting |\n| Additive, versioned evolution | new optional fields cheap; renames and meaning changes are a new version with a migration | as with any data contract |\n\n### Layers of a mapping\nMapping is more than renaming, and the layers fail in different ways:\n1. **Structural:** field renames, flattening or nesting, splitting one field into two or joining two.\n2. **Type and format:** parse dates and numbers, normalize time zones, convert units and currencies.\n3. **Value or semantic mapping:** crosswalks from source codes to canonical enums, agreed with the data\n   owner. This is where meaning lives and where most real errors happen.\n4. **Identity:** resolve source keys to canonical IDs and link entities across sources (the same customer\n   in the CRM and in billing), which may need matching rules rather than equality.\n5. **Derivation:** computed fields such as a canonical `is_escalated` from several source flags.\n\n### Mappings as declarative, versioned config\n- **Express each mapping as data** (a field table and value crosswalks) interpreted by one engine, not\n  hand-written code per customer, *because* data can be reviewed by non-engineers, diffed, validated and\n  reused for the next tenant on the same system.\n- **Layer tenant overrides on a per-system base mapping,** since two customers on the same CRM often\n  customize the same fields differently.\n\n### Mapping back: write paths need the reverse direction\n- **Read-only needs only inbound.** An assistant that writes back (updating a ticket's status, filing a\n  case) needs the reverse too.\n- **The reverse is not automatic,** *because* crosswalks are often many-to-one. If source codes `W` and\n  `K` both map to canonical `pending`, then `pending` has no single source code to write.\n- **Two fixes:** keep the original source value on the canonical record (provenance makes this cheap) and\n  write it back unchanged when the meaning has not changed; or define an explicit reverse crosswalk agreed\n  with the data owner (`pending` writes `W`).\n- **Test the round trip,** source → canonical → source, as a fixture.\n\n### Unmapped values are errors, not defaults\n- **Quarantine with the reason** when a value has no crosswalk entry, and report it to the data owner.\n- **Never default** to `other` or the most common value: every downstream result for that category\n  becomes silently wrong, which is worse than a visible gap.\n\n### Testing and validating\n- **Fixture tests per mapping:** source record in, expected canonical record out, built from profiled\n  samples.\n- **Validate every mapped record** against the canonical schema before it is written.\n- **Track per-field null and quarantine rates per source** after each load, because a sudden rise is\n  usually a source-side change rather than a data trend.\n\n### The canonical schema is the agent's tool contract\n- **Tool schemas use canonical types.** A write tool's JSON schema declares `status` as the canonical enum\n  (`open`, `pending`, `resolved`, `closed`), so the model never sees or invents a tenant's source codes,\n  and argument validation rejects anything else before the adapter runs.\n- **Tool results return canonical records,** trimmed to the fields the agent needs, with source detail\n  left in extensions. The same prompt then works across Zendesk, ServiceNow and Jira tenants.\n- **The adapter does both directions.** Inbound it maps source to canonical for results; outbound it uses\n  the reverse crosswalk and the kept source value, so the agent's write changes only what it meant to.\n- **Retrieval filters are canonical fields.** A filter such as `status = open AND product = X` works for\n  every tenant only because mapping produced queryable canonical values at ingestion.\n\n### LLM-assisted mapping\n- **Good for *proposing*.** Given source field names, descriptions and sample values, an LLM drafts a field\n  table and crosswalk quickly.\n- **A human confirms with the data owner, then it is frozen as config.** The model accelerates authoring;\n  it should not be the runtime mapping.","example":"### A declarative mapping with quarantine\nA declarative mapping for one customer's ticket export with a structural rename, type normalization, a\nvalue crosswalk, an extensions bag and quarantine on unknown values.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom zoneinfo import ZoneInfo\n\nMAPPING = {  # versioned config for tenant \"acme\", source \"legacy_helpdesk\"\n    \"version\": \"acme-helpdesk-v3\",\n    \"fields\": {\"TicketNo\": \"source_id\", \"Subj\": \"title\", \"Opened\": \"opened_at\", \"Stat\": \"status\"},\n    \"tz\": \"Europe/Berlin\",                       # source writes naive local time\n    \"status_crosswalk\": {\"N\": \"open\", \"W\": \"pending\", \"K\": \"pending\", \"E\": \"resolved\", \"Z\": \"closed\"},\n}\n\nclass Unmapped(Exception): pass                     # caller sends the record to quarantine\n\ndef map_ticket(src: dict, m=MAPPING) -> dict:\n    out = {\"source_system\": \"legacy_helpdesk\", \"mapping_version\": m[\"version\"]}\n    out.update({m[\"fields\"][k]: v for k, v in src.items() if k in m[\"fields\"]})\n    ext = {k: v for k, v in src.items() if k not in m[\"fields\"]}   # lossless: nothing is dropped\n    missing = {\"source_id\", \"opened_at\", \"status\"} - out.keys()\n    if missing:\n        raise Unmapped(f\"missing {missing} in {src.get('TicketNo')!r}\")\n    local = datetime.strptime(out[\"opened_at\"], \"%d.%m.%Y %H:%M\").replace(tzinfo=ZoneInfo(m[\"tz\"]))\n    out[\"opened_at\"] = local.astimezone(timezone.utc).isoformat()\n    code = out[\"status\"]\n    if code not in m[\"status_crosswalk\"]:\n        raise Unmapped(f\"status={code!r} for ticket {out['source_id']}\")\n    out[\"status\"], out[\"source_status\"] = m[\"status_crosswalk\"][code], code   # keep original for write-back\n    out[\"extensions\"] = ext\n    return out\n\nprint(map_ticket({\"TicketNo\": \"T-1042\", \"Subj\": \"VPN drops\", \"Opened\": \"03.11.2025 09:15\",\n                  \"Stat\": \"W\", \"Kostenstelle\": \"4711\"}))\n# opened_at becomes 2025-11-03T08:15:00+00:00 (CET is UTC+1 in November); \"Kostenstelle\" is kept in extensions.\n```\n\n- **The crosswalk is a human decision.** It maps two source codes, `W` and `K`, to one canonical\n  `pending`; that came from the client's helpdesk lead, not from the data.\n- **New codes become visible.** A code `X` appearing next quarter raises `Unmapped` and lands in quarantine\n  with a message the FDE can forward, instead of becoming a silently wrong status.\n- **Write-back stays exact.** Keeping `source_status` means a later write-back can restore `K` rather than\n  guessing between `W` and `K`.\n- **One edge it does not handle.** Naive local times are ambiguous for one hour each autumn (the clock\n  repeats 02:00 to 03:00) and nonexistent for one hour each spring, and `replace(tzinfo=...)` silently\n  picks one reading. If ordering within that hour matters, flag those values rather than trusting the\n  default.\n\n### Round-trip fixture for the write path\nA runnable test that the reverse direction never rewrites a code the user did not change.\n\n```python\nCROSSWALK = {\"N\": \"open\", \"W\": \"pending\", \"K\": \"pending\", \"E\": \"resolved\", \"Z\": \"closed\"}\nREVERSE = {\"open\": \"N\", \"pending\": \"W\", \"resolved\": \"E\", \"closed\": \"Z\"}   # agreed with data owner\n\ndef to_source(canonical: dict) -> str:\n    # unchanged meaning: write back the original code; changed meaning: use the reverse crosswalk\n    if CROSSWALK.get(canonical[\"source_status\"]) == canonical[\"status\"]:\n        return canonical[\"source_status\"]\n    return REVERSE[canonical[\"status\"]]\n\nfor code in CROSSWALK:\n    rec = {\"status\": CROSSWALK[code], \"source_status\": code}\n    assert to_source(rec) == code, code                     # round trip is exact\nrec = {\"status\": \"resolved\", \"source_status\": \"K\"}           # user resolved a K ticket\nprint(to_source(rec))                                        # E\nnaive = {v: k for k, v in CROSSWALK.items()}                 # inverted many-to-one map\nprint(naive[\"pending\"])                                      # K: every W ticket would become K\n```\n\n### A canonical write tool for an agent\nThe model sees one tool schema for every tenant; the adapter validates against the canonical enum and\nwrites back through `to_source` from the fixture above. `client` stands for the tenant's API wrapper.\n\n```python\nUPDATE_STATUS_TOOL = {\n    \"name\": \"update_ticket_status\",\n    \"description\": \"Set a ticket's status. Use only the listed values.\",\n    \"input_schema\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"properties\": {\"ticket_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n                       \"status\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"open\", \"pending\", \"resolved\", \"closed\"]}},\n        \"required\": [\"ticket_id\", \"status\"],\n    },\n}\n\ndef run_update_status(args: dict, canonical_store: dict, client) -> dict:\n    if args[\"status\"] not in REVERSE:                       # validate before any write\n        return {\"error\": f\"status must be one of {sorted(REVERSE)}\"}\n    rec = dict(canonical_store[args[\"ticket_id\"]], status=args[\"status\"])\n    code = to_source(rec)                                    # exact round trip when meaning unchanged\n    client.patch(args[\"ticket_id\"], {\"Stat\": code})\n    return {\"ticket_id\": args[\"ticket_id\"], \"status\": args[\"status\"]}   # canonical result to the model\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: counting translations\n| Sources × consumers | Point-to-point (N × M) | Canonical (N + M) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 3 × 4 (Zendesk, ServiceNow, Jira; retriever, summarizer, analytics, eval) | 12 | 7 |\n| 6 × 4 | 24 | 10 |\n| 10 × 6 | 60 | 16 |\n\nWith one source and one consumer both columns are 1 versus 2, which is why mapping directly is fine there.","patterns":["**Base mapping per system, overrides per tenant.** Use when several customers run the same SaaS. Store a versioned base field table and crosswalk per source system and layer each tenant's customizations on top. Monitor how many tenants override each crosswalk entry.","**Quarantine queue routed to the data owner.** Use for every value crosswalk. Unmapped values go to a table with record id, field, value and mapping version, and a weekly digest goes to the named owner. Monitor quarantine rate per source and age of the oldest open question.","**Mapping version stamped on every record.** Use always. After a crosswalk fix, select records by the old version and remap them from raw instead of remapping everything. Monitor the count of records still on superseded versions.","**Fixture tests from profiled samples.** Use for every mapping change. Source-in, canonical-out fixtures, plus a round-trip fixture for any field on a write path, run in CI. Monitor per-field null rate after each load against the previous load.","**LLM-drafted, human-approved crosswalks.** Use when onboarding a large unfamiliar schema. Prompt with field names, descriptions and sample values, then have the data owner approve each value mapping before it is frozen as config. Monitor the share of proposals the owner changes.","**Canonical enums in tool schemas.** Use for every agent tool that takes a categorical argument. Declare the canonical values as an enum in the tool's input schema and validate in the adapter before mapping back. Monitor rejected tool arguments per tool; a rise means the model or the prompt drifted."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Without a canonical model every tool schema, retrieval filter and prompt\n  has to know each customer's field names and codes. With one, the agent's `update_ticket_status(status=...)` tool\n  takes the same closed enum for every tenant, and the adapter maps it to that tenant's source code.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Tool argument and result schemas, metadata filters in the vector\n  store, the mapping layer inside each tool adapter, and the eval harness that compares agent outputs\n  field by field.\n- **What you control.** Which concepts are canonical, the closed enums the model may emit, the reverse\n  crosswalk for write tools, and quarantine of values the mapping does not know.\n- **What you can skip.** Enterprise-wide data modelling. Model only the concepts your agent reasons about\n  and keep the rest in extensions.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **One support agent across many helpdesks.** Canonical ticket tools let the same prompt and evals run\n  on Zendesk, ServiceNow and Jira tenants.\n- **Agents that write back to systems of record.** Reverse crosswalks and kept source values stop the\n  agent from silently rewriting statuses it did not mean to change.\n- **Filtered retrieval for RAG agents.** Canonical metadata fields make filters such as status or product\n  work identically for every tenant.\n- **Fast onboarding of a new tenant.** An LLM drafts the crosswalk, the data owner approves it, and the\n  agent's tools work unchanged.","mini_project":"**Canonical tools over two fake helpdesks (2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** one agent with `get_ticket` and `update_ticket_status` tools that works against two sources\n  with different field names and status codes.\n- **Steps:** define a canonical ticket schema; write declarative mappings for two fake sources, one with a\n  many-to-one crosswalk; build adapters that map results inbound and writes back through a reverse\n  crosswalk; add quarantine for unknown codes; run the same 8-task agent script against both sources.\n- **Done when:** both sources pass the same tasks with one prompt, round-trip fixtures pass, and an\n  injected unknown code lands in quarantine instead of reaching the model."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Source record\"] --> ST[\"Structural: rename, flatten\"]\n    ST --> TY[\"Type: parse dates, UTC, units\"]\n    TY --> V{\"Value in crosswalk?\"}\n    V -->|\"no\"| Q[\"Quarantine with reason\"]\n    Q --> O[\"Data owner decides mapping\"]\n    O -->|\"new mapping version\"| V\n    V -->|\"yes\"| ID[\"Identity: resolve canonical ID\"]\n    ID --> DV[\"Derive fields\"]\n    DV --> VAL{\"Valid against schema?\"}\n    VAL -->|\"yes\"| C[(\"Canonical store with provenance\")]\n    VAL -->|\"no\"| Q\n    C -->|\"source_status kept\"| WB[\"Write-back via reverse crosswalk\"]","caption":"A source record passes through the mapping layers in order before the agent's tools can read it;\nanything without a crosswalk entry goes to quarantine and back to the data owner, and the original value\nis kept for the agent's write-back."},"interview":[{"q":"What problem does a canonical schema solve, and what does it cost?","a":"- **It decouples sources from consumers.** Each source maps in once and each consumer reads one shape, so\n  translations grow as N + M rather than N × M.\n- **The cost** is upfront modelling, an extra hop, and governance of a shared model; with one source and\n  one consumer it is not worth it."},{"q":"Two customers on the same CRM use the same status field differently. How do you model the mapping?","a":"- **A per-system base mapping** holds the shared field table and default crosswalk.\n- **Tenant overrides layer on top** for each customer's customized picklist values, agreed with that\n  customer's data owner.\n- **Stamp the mapping version** that includes the tenant override, so fixes can be replayed precisely."},{"q":"Your assistant now writes status back to the source, and the client says statuses are changing on their own. What happened?","a":"- **A many-to-one crosswalk was inverted.** Canonical `pending` came from both `W` and `K`, and the naive\n  reverse writes one of them for every record.\n- **Fix:** keep the original source value on the canonical record and write it back when meaning is\n  unchanged, use an agreed reverse crosswalk otherwise, and add a round-trip fixture."},{"q":"When would you not quarantine an unmapped value?","a":"- **When the field is free text only shown to users,** strict quarantine lowers coverage for no\n  correctness gain.\n- **Always quarantine** when the value drives filters, routing or access, because a defaulted value makes\n  every downstream result for that category silently wrong."},{"q":"How do you design an agent's ticket tools so one prompt works for every customer's helpdesk?","a":"- **Canonical schema as the tool contract:** tool inputs and results use canonical fields and closed\n  enums, never source field names or codes.\n- **Mapping lives in the per-tenant adapter,** inbound for results and reverse crosswalk for writes, with\n  round-trip fixtures so the agent's writes change only what it intended."},{"q":"What do you monitor on mappings in production?","a":"- **Per-field null rate and quarantine rate per source after each load;** a sudden rise usually means a\n  source-side change, not a data trend.\n- **Outstanding unmapped values and their age,** which measure how fast data owners are answering.\n- **Records on superseded mapping versions,** which should drain to zero after a remap from raw."}],"tradeoffs":["**N + M vs upfront modelling.** A canonical schema turns N × M translations into roughly N + M, but costs modelling effort and an extra hop; with one source and one consumer, mapping directly is simpler and fine.","**Small schema vs useful detail.** A small schema is stable and easy to map to, but pushes useful source detail into extensions; promote a field once several consumers need it, not before.","**Declarative vs complex logic.** Declarative mappings are reviewable and reusable, but awkward for genuinely complex logic such as multi-record identity resolution; allow a code escape hatch for those cases, tested like any code.","**Strict quarantine vs coverage.** It keeps results correct but lowers coverage and creates support work. It is the right default when values drive filters, routing or access, and too strict for a free-text field only shown to users.","**LLM-proposed mappings vs hidden errors.** They speed authoring considerably, but a confident wrong crosswalk is hard to spot; the saving holds only if a data owner reviews every proposed value mapping."],"pitfalls":["**Designing the canonical schema as the union of every source's fields.** Symptom: hundreds of mostly null columns and no consumer able to tell which fields are reliably populated. Fix: model your domain and keep the rest in extensions.","**Mapping by field name alone without checking values.** Symptom: two sources with a `priority` field whose scales run in opposite directions, so high-priority tickets from one source sort to the bottom. Fix: profile values and crosswalk them.","**Dropping naive timestamps into UTC without the source zone.** Symptom: events shifted by hours and off-by-one-day errors around midnight and daylight-saving changes. Fix: record the source zone in the mapping.","**Defaulting unknown codes.** Symptom: a canonical category that grows quietly and contains records that belong elsewhere. Fix: quarantine them.","**No mapping version on records.** Symptom: after a crosswalk fix you cannot tell which records were mapped under the old rule and need remapping from raw. Fix: stamp the version on every record.","**Writing back through an inverted many-to-one crosswalk.** Symptom: source records whose status changes from `K` to `W` when the assistant updates an unrelated field, and the client's reports shifting for no visible reason. Fix: write back the kept source value, plus a round-trip fixture.","**Exposing source codes in tool schemas.** Symptom: the agent writes `W` for one tenant and `pending` for another, and a prompt tuned on one customer fails on the next. Fix: canonical enums in the tool, mapping inside the adapter.","**Letting the LLM interpret raw source records at query time instead of mapping.** Symptom: filters and permissions that do not work and answers that vary across model versions. Fix: map the fields used for filtering, routing and permissions."],"when":"- **Introduce a canonical schema** once you have more than one source system or more than one consumer of\n  the same data, which in enterprise AI is almost immediately.\n- **Keep it focused** on the concepts your product reasons about, and agree crosswalks with the client's\n  data owners during onboarding.\n- **Measure** per source and mapping version: quarantine rate, per-field null rate and the count of\n  outstanding unmapped values.\n- **Day to day:** in FDE work this is where most onboarding questions come from.\n- **Interviews:** expect to explain why a canonical model reduces coupling and where it hurts.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the mapping engine, fixture tests and quarantine path, and stamps every canonical record with source identity and mapping version for replay.","architect":"Owns the canonical schema and its versioning policy, decides what is canonical versus an extension, and keeps the model focused on the product's domain concepts.","fde":"Sits with the client's data owners to agree crosswalks, chases unmapped values, and writes the tenant-specific mapping overrides for each customer's customizations."},"check":{"q":"A colleague proposes skipping canonical mapping for a new CRM source: \"just put the raw record in\nthe prompt, the model understands Salesforce fields.\" When is that acceptable and when is it not?","a":"It is acceptable for content the model only reads and shows, in a prototype with one source, where\ninconsistency costs little. It breaks for anything that is not an LLM call: retrieval filters, access\ncontrol, analytics and evaluation need a concrete field and value set. It also ties meaning to the\nmodel's guesses, which vary across records and model versions, and cannot know a customer's custom\npicklist meanings. With a second source or consumer, every component inherits every source's quirks.\nI would map the fields used for filtering, routing and permissions to the canonical schema, keep\neverything else in extensions, and let the LLM draft the mapping for review, not perform it at runtime."},"quiz":[{"q":"With 6 source systems and 4 consumers, roughly how many translations does a canonical schema need compared with point-to-point?","options":["24 with a canonical schema versus 10 point-to-point","About 10 with a canonical schema versus 24 point-to-point","6 in both cases","4 with a canonical schema versus 6 point-to-point"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Point-to-point needs one translation per source-consumer pair, 6 × 4 = 24. A canonical model needs one mapping in per source and one read per consumer, about 6 + 4 = 10."},{"q":"Why should every canonical record carry the mapping version it was produced with?","options":["To let the index sort records by recency","Because canonical schemas cannot be changed otherwise","So that after a mapping fix you can find and re-map exactly the affected records from raw","So that consumers can parse each version differently"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When a crosswalk is corrected, records mapped under the old rule must be reprocessed. Without a version stamp you cannot tell which ones, so you either remap everything or leave errors in place."},{"q":"What is the strongest reason to keep an extensions bag on canonical records?","options":["It lets consumers ignore the canonical schema entirely","It makes mapping lossless, so a source field can be promoted later without re-ingesting from the client","It replaces the need for value crosswalks","It stores the LLM's interpretation of each field"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Source fields without a canonical home are kept rather than dropped. If a consumer later needs one, it can be promoted and backfilled from stored data instead of negotiating a new pull from the client."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/data-profiling-and-quality-assessment","sources":["Wang & Strong, 1996 — Beyond Accuracy: What Data Quality Means to Data Consumers","Official docs: Great Expectations"],"why":"- **Schemas and documentation hide the quirks.** A column called `resolution_notes` is a text field; the\n  schema does not say 40% of rows contain \"see email\", one region writes in French, or rows before a 2021\n  migration are truncated at 255 characters. Documentation describes the system as designed, and the\n  people who know the quirks are busy.\n- **AI fails quietly on bad data.** A pipeline that crashes on a bad date gets fixed the same day. A\n  retriever that indexes 30,000 empty notes returns them as top results, and a summarizer given \"see\n  email\" invents a resolution; both look like model quality problems.\n- **Eyeballing misses what matters,** which is distributional: the 11% null rate in one region, the\n  duplicate records from a merge, the share of scanned PDFs.\n- **Quality is fitness for a purpose, not absolute.** A field 30% null is irrelevant if the use case never\n  reads it and blocking if it is the retrieval filter. Wang and Strong's framing of quality as defined by\n  the data's consumers is the useful one.\n- **What it buys:** measurements instead of impressions early, when findings change the plan cheaply, and\n  a decision someone owns for every measurement.","how":"### What to profile in structured data\nPer column:\n\n| Metric | What it reveals |\n|---|---|\n| Type conformance | values that do not parse as the declared type |\n| Null and empty rate | completeness |\n| Distinct count and cardinality | free text vs codes; undocumented values |\n| Top values and frequencies | dominant values, disguised nulls |\n| Min and max; length distribution for text | out-of-range values, truncation |\n| **Format patterns** | value shapes after replacing digits and letters with placeholders, e.g. `9999-99-99` versus `99/99/9999` for dates |\n\nAcross columns and tables:\n- **Candidate key uniqueness.**\n- **Referential integrity:** do child rows point to existing parents.\n- **Functional dependencies** you rely on.\n- **Exact and near duplicates.**\n- **Freshness:** how old is the newest record, how often does it change.\n\nTwo checks that standard null counts miss:\n- **Disguised missing values.** Placeholders that are not null: `N/A`, `-`, `0`, `1900-01-01`, `unknown`,\n  \"see email\". They show as one value with suspiciously high frequency in the top-values list, or a date\n  distribution with a spike on one day. Real completeness is the null rate *plus* their share.\n- **Sensitivity scan.** Pattern and dictionary detection of emails, phone numbers, national ID formats,\n  card numbers and names in free text tells you which fields need redaction or tighter handling before\n  anything is indexed or sent to a model; free-text fields often hold identifiers the schema never\n  declared.\n\n### How big a sample\nA sample can only report problems it contains.\n- **Detection probability.** If a defect affects a share *p* of records, a random sample of *n* contains at\n  least one with probability `1 − (1 − p)^n`, which is about `1 − e^(−np)`.\n\n| Defect rate | Sample size | Chance the sample contains one |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0.1% | 2,000 rows | about 86% |\n| 0.1% | 5,000 rows | over 99% |\n\n- **The converse, the \"rule of three\":** if *n* rows show zero defects, the 95% upper bound on the true\n  rate is roughly `3 / n`. A clean 5,000-row sample still allows up to about 0.06%.\n- **Apply it per slice, not overall:** a stratum with 200 sampled rows can hide a defect rate up to about\n  1.5%.\n- **So run cheap metrics on the full table** and reserve the sample for checks that need human eyes or\n  heavy compute.\n\n### What to profile in document corpora\n- **File type mix,** and the share of PDFs with a usable text layer versus image-only scans.\n- **Encrypted or corrupt files;** page counts and sizes; languages; date range.\n- **Exact duplicates by content hash** and near duplicates by shingling or MinHash (drafts, versions,\n  forwarded copies).\n- **Share of boilerplate** (signatures, disclaimers, templates).\n\nThese determine parsing routes, chunking and cost estimates.\n\n### Slice everything\nCompute every metric per meaningful slice (source system, region, year, department, document folder),\n*because* aggregate numbers hide the problems: an overall 3% null rate can be 0% everywhere and 40% in\nthe one region the pilot serves.\n\n### Assess against quality dimensions\n| Dimension | Question for the use case |\n|---|---|\n| **Completeness** | are the fields the use case reads populated? |\n| **Validity** | do values conform to type, format and allowed sets? |\n| **Consistency** | do related fields and systems agree (a closed ticket with no close date)? |\n| **Uniqueness** | are entities represented once? |\n| **Timeliness** | is data fresh enough for the decisions it supports? |\n| **Accuracy** | does the data match reality? |\n\nAccuracy cannot be profiled; it needs a sample checked by the client's experts against ground truth,\n*because* a perfectly formatted wrong value passes every automated test.\n\n### From findings to decisions\nClassify each finding:\n1. **Acceptable.**\n2. **Fixable in the pipeline:** normalize, deduplicate, route scans to OCR.\n3. **Fixable only at the source:** the client's data owner must act.\n4. **Blocking:** the use case cannot work without it.\n\nThe assessment names an owner for each and is shared with the client, because many fixes are theirs.\n\n### Profile from the agent's point of view\nStart from the agent, not the schema, because the use case defines fitness.\n1. **List what each tool reads:** the fields a record tool returns, the metadata a retrieval tool filters\n   on, the text that gets chunked and embedded.\n2. **Profile exactly those, per slice the agent serves** (tenant, region, department).\n3. **Translate findings into agent symptoms:** disguised nulls become invented answers, near duplicates\n   become crowded-out retrieval results, a 37% null slice becomes \"no information found\" for one region.\n4. **Mirror the slices in the agent's eval set,** so a weak slice in the profile shows up as a weak slice\n   in the scores.\n- **Tell the model what is missing.** Return placeholder values as explicit nulls in tool results, so the\n  agent says \"no resolution recorded\" instead of reading \"see email\" as content.\n\n### From one-off profile to continuous checks\n- **Thresholds become expectations on every load:** null rate of `loss_date` below 2%, `status` within the\n  agreed set, row count within a band of yesterday's.\n- **Express them declaratively** with tools such as Great Expectations or dbt tests.\n- **A failed check quarantines the batch or alerts,** *because* source-side changes (an upgrade, a new\n  form, a renamed picklist) are a common cause of sudden AI quality drops.","example":"### A sliced profiler for a ticket export\nA compact profiler over a client's ticket export using pandas, sliced by region. It computes the\nmeasurements that most often change an onboarding plan.\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd, re\n\ndef shape(v):  # format pattern: digits -> 9, letters -> A\n    return re.sub(r\"[A-Za-z]\", \"A\", re.sub(r\"\\d\", \"9\", str(v)))\n\ndef profile(df: pd.DataFrame, slice_col: str, key: str):\n    report = {\"rows\": len(df), \"dup_keys\": int(df[key].duplicated().sum())}\n    for col in df.columns:\n        s = df[col]\n        report[col] = {\n            \"null_pct\": round(s.isna().mean() * 100, 1),\n            \"distinct\": int(s.nunique()),\n            \"top\": s.value_counts().head(5).to_dict(),\n            \"shapes\": s.dropna().map(shape).value_counts().head(3).to_dict(),\n        }\n    report[\"null_pct_by_slice\"] = (df.isna().groupby(df[slice_col]).mean() * 100).round(1)\n    return report\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"tickets_sample.csv\", dtype=str)\nr = profile(df, slice_col=\"region\", key=\"ticket_id\")\nprint(r[\"dup_keys\"], r[\"status\"][\"distinct\"], r[\"opened_at\"][\"shapes\"])\nprint(r[\"null_pct_by_slice\"][[\"closed_at\", \"resolution_notes\"]])\n```\n\nIllustrative output on a 5,000-row stratified sample, and the assessment it leads to:\n\n| Finding | Decision |\n|---|---|\n| 212 duplicate `ticket_id` values (a past migration loaded one year twice) | deduplicate on key plus latest version (pipeline fix) |\n| `status` has 23 distinct values against 8 documented | crosswalk the 23 statuses with the support lead (mapping) |\n| `opened_at` has two shapes, `9999-99-99 99:99` and `99/99/9999 99:99` | parse dates per source once the order is confirmed (pipeline fix) |\n| `resolution_notes` is 4% null overall but 37% null in the APAC slice | ask whether notes live in another system; a resolution assistant without resolutions is blocked there (source question) |\n\n- **Slash dates hide their order.** The shape cannot reveal whether one source writes US `MM/DD` or\n  European `DD/MM`, so you check for values with a first field above 12 and confirm with the source owner.\n- **Disguised nulls show in `top`.** If \"see email\" is the most common `resolution_notes` value, the\n  field's effective completeness is far below what `null_pct` reports.\n\n### Sample sizing and disguised nulls in plain Python\nA runnable helper: how many rows you need to see a rare defect, and the effective completeness once\nplaceholders are counted.\n\n```python\nimport math\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef rows_to_detect(p, confidence=0.95):\n    return math.ceil(math.log(1 - confidence) / math.log(1 - p))\n\ndef rule_of_three_bound(n):\n    return 3 / n                          # 95% upper bound when n rows show zero defects\n\nprint(rows_to_detect(0.001))              # 2995 rows for a 0.1% defect at 95%\nprint(f\"{rule_of_three_bound(5000):.2%}\") # 0.06%\nprint(f\"{rule_of_three_bound(200):.1%}\")  # 1.5% per 200-row slice\n\nPLACEHOLDERS = {\"\", \"n/a\", \"-\", \"0\", \"unknown\", \"1900-01-01\", \"see email\"}\nnotes = [\"see email\"] * 40 + [None] * 4 + [\"Reset VPN token\"] * 30 + [\"Replaced dock\"] * 26\nreal = [v for v in notes if v is not None and v.strip().lower() not in PLACEHOLDERS]\nprint(f\"null rate {notes.count(None) / len(notes):.0%}, \"\n      f\"effective completeness {len(real) / len(notes):.0%}\")   # 4% null, 56% complete\nprint(Counter(notes).most_common(1))      # [('see email', 40)] is the tell\n```\n\n### Agent scenario: linking eval failures to profile findings\nA resolution-suggestion agent scores 81% on its 200-question eval overall. Splitting the failures by the\nprofile's slices (illustrative numbers):\n\n| Slice | Eval pass rate | Profile finding behind it | Action |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| EMEA | 90% | none material | none |\n| APAC | 52% | `resolution_notes` 37% null | source question: notes kept elsewhere? |\n| Pre-2021 tickets | 64% | notes truncated at 255 characters | exclude from retrieval or mark truncated |\n| All regions | (citations) | 212 duplicate `ticket_id` values | dedupe before indexing |\n\nNo prompt change would have fixed APAC; the profile shows the agent had nothing to retrieve.","patterns":["**Baseline profile per tenant and source.** Use from onboarding on. Store the onboarding profile (per slice) as a versioned artifact and diff each new profile against it. Monitor per-slice null rate, distinct count and shape changes on the fields retrieval and prompts read.","**Load-time expectations, warn first.** Use for every recurring load. Declare thresholds in Great Expectations or dbt tests, run them as warnings until their natural variance is known, then promote stable ones to blocking with quarantine. Monitor check failure rate and false-alarm rate.","**Disguised-null registry.** Use for free-text and date fields. Keep a per-source list of placeholder values found during profiling and report effective completeness alongside the raw null rate. Monitor the placeholder share per load.","**Sensitivity scan before indexing.** Use for any field or document sent to a model. Pattern and dictionary detection on a sample and on new loads, routing hits to redaction. Monitor detections per field, especially in fields the schema did not declare as sensitive.","**SME accuracy review on a schedule.** Use where answers drive decisions. A small, structured sample per quarter or per source change, checked against ground truth by the client's experts. Monitor the share of sampled values rejected.","**Eval slices aligned to profile slices.** Use for any agent over client data. Tag every eval question with the same slice keys the profile uses, and report pass rate per slice next to the slice's profile metrics. Monitor slices where a profile shift and an eval drop coincide."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A retrieval tool that returns \"see email\" or five drafts of one policy\n  makes the agent hallucinate or cite the wrong version, and it looks like a model problem. Profiling\n  the fields and documents behind each tool is how you tell a data failure from an agent failure.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The ingestion pipeline feeding the agent's index, metadata filters\n  the retrieval tool applies, redaction before content reaches the model, and the eval set whose slices\n  should mirror the profile's slices.\n- **What you control.** Which fields each tool reads, placeholder registries that turn disguised nulls into\n  real missing values, near-duplicate collapse before indexing, and load-time expectations that stop a bad\n  batch reaching the agent.\n- **What you can skip.** Full data-quality frameworks for fields no tool, filter or prompt ever reads.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Pre-launch checks for a RAG agent.** Profiling the fields and documents behind each tool finds\n  disguised nulls, duplicates and scanned files before users see invented answers.\n- **Debugging an agent quality drop.** Diffing the current profile against the baseline separates a\n  source-side change from a model or prompt regression.\n- **Designing the eval set.** Profile slices tell you which regions, years and document types the agent's\n  eval questions must cover.\n- **Guarding the index.** Load-time expectations quarantine bad batches before the agent can retrieve\n  them.","mini_project":"**Profile, then explain an agent's failures (2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** show that a retrieval agent's weak slice is caused by the data, using a profile and a sliced\n  eval.\n- **Steps:** generate a ticket dataset with a region slice that has many placeholder notes and duplicates;\n  run the sliced profiler and the disguised-null check; index the notes and build a small agent that\n  suggests resolutions; write 30 eval questions tagged by region; fix the data (placeholders to nulls,\n  dedupe) and re-run.\n- **Done when:** a table pairs per-slice pass rate with per-slice profile metrics before and after the\n  data fix, with no prompt change in between."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    D[(\"Client data\")] --> F[\"Cheap metrics on full table\"]\n    D --> S[\"Stratified sample\"]\n    S --> E[\"Expensive checks: near dups, sensitivity, SME accuracy\"]\n    F --> SL[\"Slice by source, region, year\"]\n    E --> SL\n    SL --> A{\"Fit for the use case?\"}\n    A -->|\"acceptable\"| X[\"Expectations on every load\"]\n    A -->|\"pipeline fix\"| P[\"Normalize, dedupe, OCR\"]\n    A -->|\"source fix or blocking\"| O[\"Client data owner\"]\n    P --> X\n    X -->|\"check fails\"| Q[\"Quarantine batch or alert\"]","caption":"Profiling runs per slice on the full table for cheap metrics and on a sample for expensive ones; each\nfinding gets a decision and an owner, and accepted thresholds become load-time checks in front of the\nagent's index."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between data profiling and quality assessment?","a":"- **Profiling is measurement:** null rates, value sets, format shapes, duplicates and freshness, computed\n  per slice.\n- **Assessment is judgement against a purpose:** the same 30% null rate is irrelevant for an unused field\n  and blocking for a retrieval filter, so each finding becomes acceptable, pipeline fix, source fix or\n  blocking, with an owner."},{"q":"A field shows 2% nulls but your agent keeps inventing answers from what its tool returns. What do you check?","a":"- **Disguised missing values:** look at the top values for placeholders like `N/A`, `-` or \"see email\"\n  with suspiciously high frequency.\n- **Recompute effective completeness** as nulls plus placeholder share, and add the placeholders to a\n  per-source registry so filters and prompts treat them as missing.\n- **Change the tool result:** return placeholders as explicit nulls, so the agent says \"no resolution\n  recorded\" instead of building an answer around \"see email\"."},{"q":"How big a sample do you profile, and what can a clean sample tell you?","a":"- **Detection:** a sample of *n* catches a defect of rate *p* with probability `1 − (1 − p)^n`; a 0.1%\n  defect needs roughly 3,000 rows for 95% confidence.\n- **Upper bound:** zero defects in *n* rows bounds the true rate at about `3 / n`, so 5,000 clean rows\n  still allow 0.06%, and a 200-row slice allows 1.5%.\n- **So** cheap metrics run on the full table and samples are reserved for expensive checks."},{"q":"Your agent fails 48% of eval questions for one region. How do you decide whether to fix the prompt or the data?","a":"- **Split failures by slice and read the traces:** if the retrieval tool returned empty, placeholder or\n  truncated content, it is a data problem, and the profile for that slice should show it.\n- **Fix data at its stage** (source question, pipeline fix, exclusion), re-run the eval slice, and only\n  then tune the prompt on what remains."},{"q":"When would you fix quality at the source rather than in your pipeline?","a":"- **When the error originates at data entry** and will keep recurring, such as a form that lets users skip\n  a required field; a pipeline patch must then be maintained forever and hides the problem from the client.\n- **Fix in the pipeline** when it is a stable format issue you can normalize reliably, such as known date\n  formats per source."},{"q":"How would you turn an onboarding profile into production monitoring?","a":"- **Convert thresholds into expectations** evaluated on every load, such as null rate of a key field below\n  2% or status within the agreed set.\n- **Start as warnings** to learn natural variance, then make stable checks blocking with quarantine.\n- **Diff each load's per-slice profile against the baseline,** because source-side upgrades are a common\n  cause of sudden AI quality drops."}],"tradeoffs":["**Sample vs full scan.** Profiling a stratified sample is fast and cheap but can miss rare problems; profile the full dataset for cheap metrics (counts, nulls, key uniqueness) and use samples for expensive ones such as near duplicate detection and manual accuracy review.","**Strict checks vs fresh data.** Strict load-time checks stop bad batches before they reach users, but every false alarm blocks fresh data; start as warnings, learn natural variance, then promote stable ones to blocking.","**Pipeline fix vs source fix.** Fixing quality in your pipeline is fast and under your control, but hides the problem from the client and must be maintained forever; for errors that originate at entry, pushing the fix to the source costs more time now and less over the life of the deployment.","**Automated metrics vs SME review.** Automated metrics scale across tenants but cannot measure accuracy; SME review of a sample is slow and expensive yet is the only way to learn whether correctly formatted data is actually right."],"pitfalls":["**Reporting only aggregate metrics.** Symptom: a dataset judged healthy that fails badly for the one department or region the pilot serves. Fix: slice every metric.","**Profiling once at onboarding and never again.** Symptom: a sudden quality drop months later traced to a source-side upgrade that renamed a picklist value. Fix: load-time expectations and baseline diffs.","**Counting only true nulls.** Symptom: a field reported 98% complete whose most frequent value is `N/A` or `1900-01-01`, and prompts or filters that treat the placeholder as real data. Fix: a disguised-null registry.","**Treating format validity as accuracy.** Symptom: clean-looking data that SMEs reject because values are stale, defaulted or entered into the wrong field. Fix: SME review of a sample.","**Ignoring duplicates and near duplicates in document corpora.** Symptom: retrieval returning five versions of the same policy and answers citing an outdated draft. Fix: hash and MinHash before indexing.","**A profile report with no decisions or owners.** Symptom: the same findings rediscovered in the pilot, after users have already seen the consequences. Fix: classify each finding and name an owner.","**Tuning the agent's prompt for what is a data gap.** Symptom: weeks of prompt iterations that lift one slice and hurt another, while the failing slice simply has no content to retrieve. Fix: split eval failures by profile slice before touching the prompt.","**Copying production data to a laptop to profile it.** Symptom: a security finding, because profiling exposes the most sensitive columns first. Fix: profile inside the approved environment."],"when":"- **Profile every new source** during onboarding before mapping or building.\n- **Profile again** whenever a source changes version, a new tenant joins on the same system, or AI quality\n  drops without a model or prompt change.\n- **Tie the profile to the use case:** start from the fields and documents the product reads and the\n  slices users care about.\n- **Measure:** per-slice null rates, quarantine counts and check failures over time.\n- **Customer conversations:** a short written assessment with owners is often the most valuable artifact\n  of the first weeks.\n- **Interviews:** distinguishing profiling (measurement) from assessment (fitness for a purpose) is the\n  point to make.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the profiling job and the load-time expectations, slices metrics by source and region, and wires failed checks to quarantine and alerts instead of silent loads.","architect":"Defines the quality dimensions and thresholds that gate production per use case, and decides which fixes belong in the pipeline versus at the client's source.","fde":"Presents the assessment to the client's data owners, gets source-side fixes assigned, and arranges the SME sample review that measures accuracy."},"check":{"q":"Your RAG assistant's answer quality for one customer fell noticeably last month. No model,\nprompt or code changed. How would you use profiling to investigate?","a":"Compare the current data profile with the onboarding baseline, per slice. Check volumes first:\ndid a sync stop, or did ingestion double-load a period. Then per-field null rates, value sets and format\nshapes on the fields retrieval and prompts use: a source upgrade may have renamed a status, moved notes\nto a new field, or switched documents to scanned attachments with no text layer. Check duplicates and\nfreshness of the newest records. Any shift localized to a slice points to a source-side change. Then I\nwould add an expectation that would have caught it, so the next one quarantines a batch instead of\nreaching users."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should profiling metrics be computed per slice (region, source, year) rather than only overall?","options":["Sliced metrics are cheaper to compute","Aggregates can hide a severe problem concentrated in one slice the use case depends on","Data quality tools require a slice column","Overall metrics are always biased upward"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A 4% overall null rate can be 0% in most regions and 37% in one; if the pilot serves that region, the aggregate leads to a wrong go decision."},{"q":"Which quality dimension cannot be established by automated profiling alone?","options":["Completeness","Validity","Uniqueness","Accuracy"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Profiling checks presence, format and consistency, but a well-formed wrong value passes all of them. Accuracy needs comparison against ground truth, usually SME review of a sample."},{"q":"A field is 30% null. What determines whether that is a problem?","options":["Whether the null rate exceeds an industry-standard threshold","Whether the column is indexed in the source database","Whether the use case reads that field, for example as a retrieval filter or prompt input","Whether the nulls are stored as empty strings or as NULL"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Quality is fitness for purpose. A sparse field the product never uses is irrelevant; the same rate on a field used for filtering or answering can block the use case."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/client-document-parsing","sources":["Official docs: pdfplumber","Official docs: Tesseract OCR"],"why":"- **Enterprise knowledge lives in messy documents.** Contracts, claims files, policies, invoices and\n  engineering reports. A demo corpus is a dozen clean PDFs exported from Word; a client's is hundreds of\n  thousands of files accumulated over years, with a large share scanned, skewed, stamped, handwritten on,\n  or exported by an old system with a broken font encoding.\n- **The mix is unknown until profiled,** which is why parsing is a per-client problem rather than a solved\n  library choice.\n- **One text extractor fails silently:**\n  - A scanned PDF has no text layer, so the extractor returns an empty string and the document disappears\n    from the index without an error.\n  - A born-digital PDF with a bad character map returns plausible-looking garbage that is embedded and\n    retrieved.\n  - A two-column contract is read across the columns, interleaving clauses.\n  - A table of policy limits becomes a run of numbers detached from their headers, so the model answers\n    with the wrong limit and cites a real page.\n- **OCR or a vision model on every page is the opposite mistake:** slow and expensive at enterprise\n  volume, and it can degrade born-digital text that was already exact.\n- **Scope of this lesson.** The mechanics of OCR, layout analysis and table extraction are in the RAG\n  document-parsing and multimodal OCR lessons. This lesson applies them to one client's corpus: triage,\n  routing, confidence, review and provenance.","how":"### 1. Profile the corpus first\nFrom a stratified sample, measure file type mix, share of PDFs with a usable text layer, encrypted or\ncorrupt files, page counts, languages, and recurring templates (the same form from the same system). This\nsets the routing rules and the cost and time estimate for the backfill.\n\n### 2. Triage each file, ideally each page\n| Case | Route | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Encrypted or corrupt** | report to the client with the file list | only they can supply passwords or exclude files |\n| **Born-digital with a text layer** | extract directly | cheapest and most exact, *because* the characters are already in the file |\n| **Image-only pages** | OCR | hybrid PDFs (a scanned contract with a digital cover page) are common, so decide per page, not per file |\n| **Text layer present but unusable** | treat as image | broken font encodings produce text that exists but means nothing |\n| **Text layer that is itself old OCR** | quality check; re-OCR on failure | passes a naive \"has text\" test, but its quality is unknown |\n| **Text plus embedded images** | OCR the image regions or the page | the text check passes and the image's content is lost |\n\nThe details behind the harder rows:\n- **Detect unusable text** with a quality check: the share of characters that are letters, the share of\n  tokens found in a dictionary for the expected language, or replacement and private-use characters.\n- **Old OCR layers are common.** Many scanners and document-management systems save \"searchable PDFs\" with\n  an invisible OCR layer under the page image, produced by whatever engine and settings were in use at the\n  time. Give it the same quality check.\n- **Embedded images hide content.** A born-digital page can carry a pasted scan, a screenshot of a table\n  or a stamped signature block as an image. Measure the share of page area covered by images (PDF\n  libraries expose image bounding boxes) and send those regions, or the whole page, to OCR when it is\n  large.\n\n### 3. OCR with preprocessing and confidence\n1. **Render at sufficient resolution** (Tesseract's documentation recommends at least 300 DPI), deskew,\n   and set the expected languages explicitly, *because* OCR accuracy depends heavily on image quality and\n   on the language model the engine applies.\n2. **Keep word-level confidence.** OCR engines report it, and the page's mean or low-percentile confidence\n   is the routing signal.\n3. **Treat confidence as a ranking, not a probability.** It is not calibrated against your documents: it\n   can be high on a confidently misread stamp or low on a perfectly read unusual font.\n4. **Set the threshold from data.** Plot confidence against measured error rate on labelled pages from\n   this client and choose the point where the error becomes unacceptable for the use case.\n\n### 4. Escalate the hard pages\n- **What escalates:** pages with low OCR confidence, complex layouts, dense tables, handwriting or stamps.\n- **Where to:** a layout-aware document model, a cloud document-AI service if the data may leave the\n  boundary, or a vision-language model prompted to transcribe; below a further threshold, human review.\n- **Why tiers:** a tiered route spends the cost where it buys accuracy, instead of on every page.\n\n### 5. Recover structure\n- **Keep** reading order, headings, list items and tables (cells tied to row and column headers).\n- **Drop** repeated headers, footers and page numbers, *because* chunking and retrieval quality depend on\n  structure.\n- **Write rules for recurring templates.** A dedicated extraction rule or schema is worth it, since one\n  form type can be a large share of a corpus.\n\n### 6. Keep provenance\n- **Store per text span:** source file, page number and, where available, bounding box, parse route and\n  confidence.\n- **Citations then point to a page the user can open,** and a later parser improvement can target exactly\n  the pages that used the weak route.\n\n### 7. Evaluate on the client's documents\n- **Build a small labelled set** from their corpus, stratified by route and template, and measure text\n  accuracy against a human transcription, table cell accuracy and, end to end, whether retrieval finds the\n  right page.\n- **Character error rate** is the edit distance between output and reference divided by the reference\n  length: `CER = (S + D + I) / N`, counting substituted, deleted and inserted characters against *N*\n  reference characters (word error rate is the same over words). Because insertions count, CER can exceed\n  100% on a page of hallucinated text.\n- **Report per route and per template,** not only averaged.\n- **Add a field-level check** for values the use case depends on (amounts, dates, policy numbers), since a\n  1% CER that lands on the digits of a limit is worse than 5% spread over boilerplate.\n- **Rerun the set** when you change a parser or a threshold.\n\n### 8. What the agent gets from parsing\n- **Retrieval results carry provenance and quality.** Each chunk returned by the agent's search tool\n  includes file, page, route and confidence, so the agent cites a page and can hedge on low-confidence\n  text instead of stating a misread number as fact.\n- **Critical values get a verify step.** For amounts or limits, the agent can call a tool that returns the\n  page image region or the field-validated value, rather than trusting one OCR read.\n- **Uploads mid-conversation use the same triage.** A `parse_document` tool runs the cheap routes inline\n  and returns \"pages 4-6 need review\" when they escalate, rather than handing the model empty text.\n- **Coverage gaps are explained.** When a file failed parsing, the tool says so (\"encrypted, excluded\"),\n  so the agent does not claim the document says nothing.\n\n### Boundary and sensitivity\nScanned documents frequently contain signatures, ID numbers and health or financial details. Where\nresidency or contract requires it, OCR and any vision model must run inside the client's boundary, which\nconstrains which services you can use; settle this before choosing tools.","example":"### Per-page triage with pdfplumber and Tesseract\nPer-page triage routing each page to text extraction, OCR, or escalation. Thresholds are illustrative\nand should be tuned on a labelled sample.\n\n```python\nimport pdfplumber, pytesseract\n\ndef text_is_usable(text: str, min_chars=200, min_alpha=0.6):\n    if len(text) < min_chars: return False\n    alpha = sum(c.isalpha() or c.isspace() for c in text) / len(text)\n    return alpha >= min_alpha and \"\\ufffd\" not in text     # replacement char hints at broken encoding\n\ndef parse_pdf(path, lang=\"eng+deu\", ocr_ok=70):\n    pages = []\n    with pdfplumber.open(path) as pdf:\n        for i, page in enumerate(pdf.pages, start=1):\n            text = page.extract_text() or \"\"\n            if text_is_usable(text):\n                pages.append({\"page\": i, \"route\": \"text_layer\", \"text\": text, \"conf\": None})\n                continue\n            img = page.to_image(resolution=300).original          # PIL image\n            data = pytesseract.image_to_data(img, lang=lang, output_type=pytesseract.Output.DICT)\n            words = [(w, float(c)) for w, c in zip(data[\"text\"], data[\"conf\"]) if w.strip() and float(c) >= 0]\n            conf = sum(c for _, c in words) / len(words) if words else 0.0\n            route = \"ocr\" if conf >= ocr_ok else \"escalate\"           # VLM / layout model / human\n            pages.append({\"page\": i, \"route\": route, \"conf\": round(conf, 1),\n                          \"text\": \" \".join(w for w, _ in words)})\n    return pages\n\nfor p in parse_pdf(\"claim_88213.pdf\"):\n    print(p[\"page\"], p[\"route\"], p[\"conf\"], len(p[\"text\"]))\n```\n\n- **Typical output.** On a scanned claim file this might print a text-layer cover page, several OCR pages\n  in the 80s, and one handwritten page in the 40s routed to escalation.\n- **Routes become a forecast.** Across the backfill, the share of pages per route becomes a cost forecast,\n  and the escalation queue size tells you how much review capacity the client must provide.\n- **The usability check has its own failure mode.** A born-digital rate table or invoice is mostly digits\n  and punctuation, falls under the alphabetic threshold, and gets sent to OCR, which can only make its\n  exact text worse, so count digits as valid characters for such templates.\n- **Two more simplifications to remove before production.** Joining OCR words with spaces discards the\n  line and block numbers Tesseract returns, which is where reading order and table rows live; and the\n  200-character floor sends short but genuine digital pages (a signature page) to OCR for no gain.\n\n### Measuring CER, and why field checks matter\nA runnable, dependency-free CER computation showing that a low overall rate can still corrupt the one\nvalue that matters.\n\n```python\ndef edit_distance(a: str, b: str) -> int:\n    prev = list(range(len(b) + 1))\n    for i, ca in enumerate(a, 1):\n        cur = [i]\n        for j, cb in enumerate(b, 1):\n            cur.append(min(prev[j] + 1, cur[j - 1] + 1, prev[j - 1] + (ca != cb)))\n        prev = cur\n    return prev[-1]\n\ndef cer(output: str, reference: str) -> float:\n    return edit_distance(output, reference) / len(reference)   # (S + D + I) / N\n\nref = \"Liability limit per occurrence: EUR 2,500,000. Deductible: EUR 5,000.\"\nocr = \"Liability limit per occurrence: EUR 2,800,000. Deductible: EUR 5,000.\"\nprint(f\"CER {cer(ocr, ref):.1%}\")                 # 1.4%: one substituted character\nprint(\"limit ok:\", \"2,500,000\" in ocr)            # False: the one field that matters is wrong\nprint(f\"hallucinated page CER {cer(ref * 3, ref):.0%}\")  # 200%: insertions push CER past 100%\n```\n\n### Agent tool: search results that carry parse quality\nThe retrieval tool returns each chunk with its provenance so the model can cite and hedge. `index.search`\nstands for your vector store query; the field names are this lesson's convention.\n\n```python\ndef search_policy_docs(query: str, user: str, k: int = 5) -> list[dict]:\n    hits = index.search(query, k=k, filter={\"acl\": user})\n    results = []\n    for h in hits:\n        m = h[\"metadata\"]\n        results.append({\n            \"text\": h[\"text\"],\n            \"cite\": f'{m[\"file\"]} p.{m[\"page\"]}',\n            \"route\": m[\"route\"],                      # text_layer | ocr | escalate\n            \"low_confidence\": m[\"route\"] != \"text_layer\" and (m.get(\"conf\") or 0) < 80,\n        })\n    return results   # system prompt: quote numbers from low_confidence chunks only with a caveat\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: route mix as a cost and staffing forecast\nProfiling a 1,500-document sample of an archive (illustrative numbers) gives a page-route mix to plan the\nbackfill.\n\n| Route | Share of pages | Relative cost per page | Note |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Text layer | 55% | lowest | includes digital cover pages of hybrid PDFs |\n| OCR, confident | 35% | low | scans at 300 DPI with language set |\n| Vision or layout model | 8% | high | tables, stamps, low-confidence scans |\n| Human review | 2% | highest | handwriting and limits or amounts below threshold |\n\nRunning the vision route on everything would multiply cost on the 90% of pages that cheaper routes handle;\nthe 2% review share tells the client how many reviewer hours to book.","patterns":["**Per-page route tagging.** Use on every corpus. Store route, confidence and parser version on each page and chunk. Monitor the share of pages per route per batch; a jump in OCR share often means a new scanner or source.","**Empty-extraction coverage alert.** Use for every batch. Count files and pages that produced zero or near-zero text, grouped by folder and year, and alert above a baseline. Monitor this as a coverage metric next to ingestion counts.","**Threshold tuned on a client labelled set.** Use before the backfill. Label a stratified set of pages, plot OCR confidence against CER, and set the escalation threshold where error becomes unacceptable. Monitor CER per route and template on the set after each parser change.","**Field-level validation for critical values.** Use when answers cite amounts, dates or policy numbers. Re-check extracted values against format rules or a second route, and send mismatches to review. Monitor mismatch rate per template.","**In-boundary OCR where residency requires it.** Use when images may not leave the client network. Run a self-hosted engine (for example Tesseract) next to the data and reserve hosted services for approved classes. Monitor throughput against the backfill window, since self-hosted capacity is the bottleneck.","**Provenance and confidence in the agent's tool results.** Use for any RAG agent over parsed documents. Return file, page, route and confidence with each chunk, and instruct the agent to caveat numbers from OCR routes. Monitor the share of cited chunks that came from OCR or escalated routes, and user-reported wrong numbers per route."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A scanned contract parsed to an empty string is invisible to the agent's\n  retrieval tool; a misread table digit becomes a confident wrong answer with a real citation. Most\n  \"the agent doesn't know about X\" reports on a document corpus are parsing failures.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The ingestion pipeline before chunking and embedding, a\n  `parse_document` tool when users upload files mid-conversation, the citation metadata the agent returns,\n  and the eval set that checks the right page is retrieved.\n- **What you control.** Per-page routing rules, confidence thresholds tuned on the client's pages, which\n  routes may run outside the client boundary, and the provenance fields (file, page, box, route) returned\n  in every retrieval result.\n- **What you can skip.** OCR and layout-model internals; the RAG and multimodal lessons cover them. Know\n  the silent failures and the routing pattern.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Claims and contract agents over scanned archives.** Per-page triage makes old scanned contracts\n  retrievable instead of silently empty.\n- **Agents that answer with amounts and limits.** Field-level checks and confidence in tool results stop\n  a misread digit from becoming a confident answer.\n- **Document upload in chat.** A parse tool with the same triage handles a user's scanned PDF inside a\n  conversation.\n- **Regulated deployments.** In-boundary OCR keeps document images inside the client network while the\n  agent still searches the extracted text.","mini_project":"**Parse a mixed PDF set for a citing agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** a small RAG agent that answers from a mix of digital and scanned PDFs and cites page numbers\n  with confidence.\n- **Steps:** collect or make 10 PDFs including two scans and one hybrid file; run the per-page triage with\n  pdfplumber and Tesseract; chunk and index with file, page, route and confidence; build a search tool\n  that returns provenance; write 15 questions, three of them about numbers on scanned pages; measure CER\n  on three labelled pages.\n- **Done when:** every answer cites a page, questions on scanned pages are answered rather than missed,\n  and low-confidence numbers come with a caveat in the agent's reply."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    F[\"Client file\"] --> E{\"Encrypted or corrupt?\"}\n    E -->|\"yes\"| R[\"Report to client\"]\n    E -->|\"no\"| P[\"Split into pages\"]\n    P --> T{\"Text layer passes quality check?\"}\n    T -->|\"yes\"| X[\"Extract text directly\"]\n    T -->|\"no\"| O[\"Render 300 DPI, OCR with languages\"]\n    O --> C{\"Confidence above tuned threshold?\"}\n    C -->|\"yes\"| S[\"Recover structure\"]\n    C -->|\"no\"| V[\"Layout or vision model\"]\n    V -->|\"still doubtful\"| H[\"Human review\"]\n    X --> S\n    V --> S\n    H --> S\n    S --> I[(\"Index with file, page, box, route\")]","caption":"Each page takes the cheapest route that passes its quality gate; low confidence escalates to stronger\nand costlier routes, and every route writes text with page provenance that the agent's retrieval tool\nreturns with each chunk."},"interview":[{"q":"Why not just run one PDF text extractor over the whole client corpus?","a":"- **It fails silently.** Scans return empty strings and vanish from the index, broken font encodings\n  return garbage that gets embedded, and multi-column layouts and tables lose their structure.\n- **Triage instead:** detect each page's type and route it to direct extraction, OCR, or a stronger model,\n  with quality and confidence gates between them."},{"q":"How do you set the OCR confidence threshold for escalation?","a":"- **Not from the engine's number alone,** because confidence is a ranking signal, not a calibrated\n  probability on your documents.\n- **Label a stratified set of the client's pages,** plot confidence against measured CER or field errors,\n  and pick the point where error becomes unacceptable for the use case.\n- **Re-tune** when a new source, scanner or template appears."},{"q":"A user found an answer citing the wrong policy limit, though the cited page is correct. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check the page's route and confidence in provenance;** a table read as a run of numbers, or a\n  confidently misread digit, both produce this.\n- **Compare the extracted table to the scan** to see whether cells lost their headers.\n- **Fix with structure recovery or escalation** for dense tables, plus a field-level check on amounts,\n  since a 1% CER on a limit's digits is worse than 5% on boilerplate."},{"q":"Users upload scanned PDFs to your agent mid-conversation. How do you design the parse tool?","a":"- **Run the same per-page triage inline:** text layer first, OCR for image pages, and return escalated\n  pages as \"needs review\" rather than empty text, so the agent never treats unread pages as blank.\n- **Return provenance and confidence** with the text and keep the parsed result for later turns, so\n  the agent cites pages and does not re-parse on every step."},{"q":"When would you send every page through a vision-language model?","a":"- **For a small corpus,** where per-page triage adds more maintenance than it saves and the cost is still\n  affordable.\n- **Not at enterprise volume,** because it is slow and expensive, can degrade exact born-digital text, and\n  can fluently transcribe text that is not there."},{"q":"The client forbids document images leaving their network. What changes?","a":"- **Hosted OCR and document-AI services are out** for those classes, because they send page images to a\n  third party.\n- **Run OCR and any vision model inside the boundary,** and plan backfill time around self-hosted\n  capacity; text-layer extraction and chunking can still run wherever approved."}],"tradeoffs":["**Per-page routing vs simplicity.** It spends expensive OCR and vision calls only where needed, but adds triage logic and thresholds to maintain; for a small corpus, running the strongest route on everything can be simpler and still affordable.","**Vision-language models vs classic OCR.** They handle messy layouts, tables and handwriting better in many cases, but are slower, costlier, and can produce fluent transcriptions of text that is not there; use them where classic OCR confidence is low and verify on a labelled sample.","**Cloud document-AI vs the boundary.** Hosted services are accurate and low-effort, but send document images outside the client's boundary; residency or contractual limits flip the choice to a self-hosted engine inside the boundary.","**Human review vs client staff time.** It gives the highest accuracy but costs client time; reserve it for pages that matter to the use case, such as limits, amounts and signatures, rather than every page.","**Template rules vs form changes.** Template-specific extraction is very accurate for recurring forms, but breaks when the client changes the form; detect template version and fall back to the generic route on a mismatch."],"pitfalls":["**Treating empty extraction as an empty document.** Symptom: a coverage gap of scanned files that never appear in retrieval, found only when a user asks about one. Fix: an empty-extraction coverage alert.","**Trusting any non-empty text layer.** Symptom: garbled text from broken font encodings embedded and retrieved, producing confident answers grounded in nonsense. Fix: a text-quality check before indexing.","**Treating OCR confidence as an accuracy guarantee,** with a threshold never checked against labelled pages. Symptom: a page that passed at high confidence with a misread amount, found by a user comparing the answer with the original scan. Fix: tune on labelled pages and add field-level checks.","**Deciding route per file instead of per page.** Symptom: hybrid PDFs where the digital cover page is indexed and the scanned body is lost. Fix: per-page triage.","**OCR with the wrong language setting.** Symptom: high error rates on accented and non-Latin characters, which also breaks keyword search on names and terms. Fix: set languages from the corpus profile.","**Discarding page numbers and positions.** Symptom: citations that point to a whole 200-page file, so users cannot verify answers and stop trusting them. Fix: store page and box provenance per span.","**Hiding parse failures from the agent.** Symptom: the agent answers \"the contract does not mention a termination clause\" because the scanned pages were empty. Fix: tool results report excluded or escalated pages so the agent says what it could not read.","**Evaluating the parser on public sample PDFs.** Symptom: a parser that looked accurate in testing and fails on the client's faxed, stamped and skewed originals. Fix: a labelled set from the client's corpus."],"when":"- **Apply this triage** whenever a client corpus is more than a handful of born-digital files, which in\n  enterprise work is nearly always.\n- **Start by profiling** a stratified sample to learn the route mix, then set thresholds on a small\n  labelled set from the client's own documents.\n- **Measure:** share of pages per route, OCR confidence distribution, escalation queue size, parse\n  failures, and end-to-end retrieval of the right page.\n- **Revisit routing** when a new document source or template appears.\n- **Interviews:** the strong answer names silent failures (empty and garbled text layers) and per-page\n  routing rather than a favourite parser.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements per-page triage, text-layer quality checks, OCR with confidence capture and an escalation path, and stores page and bounding-box provenance with every chunk.","architect":"Chooses the parsing tiers and where each runs relative to the client boundary, and sets the accuracy targets and review policy for each document class.","fde":"Profiles the client's actual document estate, gets passwords or exclusions for protected files, arranges reviewers for escalated pages, and builds the labelled sample with client staff."},"check":{"q":"After indexing a client's 120,000-document archive, users say the assistant \"doesn't know about\"\nmany older contracts, though they are in the source library. What is your diagnosis process?","a":"First compare per-document coverage: how many files produced zero or near-zero text, grouped by\nyear and folder. Older contracts are likely scans with no text layer, extracted as empty strings and\nsilently indexed as nothing, or hybrid PDFs where only a digital cover page was read. Also check for\ngarbled text layers by measuring alphabetic ratio and dictionary hit rate, including the invisible OCR\nlayers that older scanners wrote, which \"have text\" but may be too poor to retrieve on. The fix is per-page triage:\nroute image-only and unusable pages to OCR with the right languages, escalate low-confidence pages, and\nreprocess from the stored raw files. Then add a coverage check per batch so empty extractions alert."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should the parsing route be decided per page rather than per file?","options":["OCR engines can only process one page at a time","Hybrid PDFs mix digital and scanned pages, so a per-file decision loses one kind or overpays for the other","Per-page decisions make provenance unnecessary","Text layers are always stored per page in the PDF format"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A scanned contract with a digital cover page passes a per-file text check, and the scanned body is lost. Per-page triage extracts digital pages directly and OCRs only the image pages."},{"q":"A PDF's text layer extracts to non-empty text. What is the main remaining risk?","options":["The text is always in the wrong reading order","The file must still be OCRed to obtain confidence scores","A broken font encoding can produce text that exists but is meaningless, so it needs a quality check","Non-empty text layers cannot contain tables"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Some PDFs map glyphs to the wrong characters. The extractor returns garbage without error; checks such as alphabetic ratio, dictionary hits or replacement characters catch it before it is indexed."},{"q":"A client forbids document images from leaving their network. Which choice does this most directly constrain?","options":["Whether you can chunk documents by heading","Whether you can use a hosted cloud OCR or document-AI service instead of an engine running inside their boundary","Whether you keep page numbers for citations","Whether you can extract text layers from born-digital PDFs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Text-layer extraction and chunking can run anywhere approved, but a hosted OCR or vision service sends page images to a third party. Residency constraints push OCR and vision models inside the boundary."}]}]});
